#: src/introduction.md:31
msgid ""
"**New to EW:** the tutorial [A first schematic page with "
"potentials](tutorials/first-page.md), then [A terminal strip and a "
"cable](tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md) and [Reports and "
"PDF](tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md). If you come from QElectroTech, "
"[Importing a QElectroTech project](tutorials/import-qet.md) brings a project "
"of yours over."
msgstr ""
"**Nieuw bij EW:** de zelfstudie [Een eerste schemapagina met "
"potentialen](tutorials/first-page.md), daarna [Een klemmenstrook en een "
"kabel](tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md) en [Rapporten en "
"PDF](tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md). Komt u van QElectroTech, dan brengt [Een "
"QElectroTech-project importeren](tutorials/import-qet.md) een project van u "
"over."

#: src/introduction.md:35
msgid ""
"**PLCs, bus systems and pneumatics:** the tutorials [A PLC with its "
"modules](tutorials/plc-station.md), [A bus "
"network](tutorials/bus-network.md) and [A pneumatic "
"circuit](tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md)."
msgstr ""
"**PLC's, bussystemen en pneumatiek:** de zelfstudies [Een PLC met zijn "
"modules](tutorials/plc-station.md), [Een "
"busnetwerk](tutorials/bus-network.md) en [Een pneumatisch "
"schema](tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md)."

#: src/introduction.md:37
msgid ""
"**A task at hand:** the how-to guides, among them [Choose "
"parts](how-to/choose-parts.md), [Draw a panel "
"layout](how-to/panel-layout.md) and [Upgrade a "
"project](how-to/upgrade-a-project.md)."
msgstr ""
"**Een taak die u wilt uitvoeren:** de instructies, waaronder [Artikelen "
"kiezen](how-to/choose-parts.md), [Een paneellayout "
"tekenen](how-to/panel-layout.md) en [Een project "
"omzetten](how-to/upgrade-a-project.md)."

#: src/introduction.md:39
msgid ""
"**A fact to look up:** the [rule reference](reference/rules/index.md), with "
"a page per check rule; the window's [tools](reference/window/tools.md), "
"[dialogs](reference/window/dialogs.md) and "
"[panels](reference/window/panels.md); and the [release "
"notes](reference/release-notes/index.md) of every release."
msgstr ""
"**Een gegeven om op te zoeken:** de [regelnaslag](reference/rules/index.md), "
"met een pagina per controleregel; de "
"[gereedschappen](reference/window/tools.md), "
"[dialogen](reference/window/dialogs.md) en "
"[panelen](reference/window/panels.md) van het venster; en de [release "
"notes](reference/release-notes/index.md) van elke release."

#: src/introduction.md:42
msgid ""
"**Coming from another program:** [EPLAN and EW](explanation/eplan-and-ew.md) "
"and [QElectroTech and EW](explanation/qet-and-ew.md) relate the concepts you "
"know to EW's."
msgstr ""
"**Afkomstig van een ander programma:** [EPLAN en "
"EW](explanation/eplan-and-ew.md) en [QElectroTech en "
"EW](explanation/qet-and-ew.md) zetten de begrippen die u kent naast die van "
"EW."

#: src/introduction.md:45
msgid "What the guide does not contain"
msgstr "Wat de gids niet bevat"

#: src/introduction.md:47
msgid ""
"The design documents, the architecture decision records and the work plan "
"stay in the repository, next to the code they describe. This guide links to "
"them; it does not copy them. Start from the [documentation "
"map](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/README.md)."
msgstr ""
"De ontwerpdocumenten, de architectuurbesluiten en het werkplan blijven in de "
"repository, naast de code die ze beschrijven. Deze gids verwijst ernaar; hij "
"neemt ze niet over. Begin bij het "
"[documentatieoverzicht](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/README.md)."

#: src/introduction.md:51
msgid "Offline use"
msgstr "Offline gebruik"

#: src/introduction.md:53
msgid ""
"The guide builds without network access and is meant to be bundled with the "
"application, so that the help works without a connection. Pages that link to "
"the repository need a connection to follow those links."
msgstr ""
"De gids wordt zonder netwerktoegang gebouwd en is bedoeld om met de "
"applicatie mee te leveren, zodat de help ook zonder verbinding werkt. "
"Pagina's die naar de repository verwijzen, hebben een verbinding nodig om "
"die links te volgen."

#: src/introduction.md:56
msgid "Sample data"
msgstr "Voorbeeldgegevens"

#: src/introduction.md:58
msgid ""
"Every example uses the sample project (`fixtures/sample/sample.ew` in the "
"repository, `samples/sample.ew` in an installation; licence CC0-1.0), a "
"small conveyor drive that the program draws itself from a script. It holds "
"placeholders where a real project would name a customer, an end user or a "
"person, such as `Sample Customer`. Do not put real customer data into "
"examples that you contribute to this guide."
msgstr ""
"Elk voorbeeld gebruikt het voorbeeldproject (`fixtures/sample/sample.ew` in "
"de repository, `samples/sample.ew` in een installatie; licentie CC0-1.0), "
"een kleine transportbandaandrijving met een pneumatische klem die het "
"programma zelf tekent uit een script. Waar een echt project een klant, een "
"eindgebruiker of een persoon zou noemen, staat een plaatshouder, zoals "
"`Sample Customer`. Zet geen echte klantgegevens in voorbeelden die u aan "
"deze gids bijdraagt."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:3
msgid ""
"A tutorial is a lesson you follow once, from start to end, to learn how EW "
"works; a [how-to guide](../how-to/index.md) is where you look up one job "
"later, when you know what you want to do."
msgstr ""
"Een zelfstudie is een les die u één keer van begin tot eind volgt om te "
"leren hoe EW werkt; in een [handleiding](../how-to/index.md) zoekt u later "
"één taak op, als u weet wat u wilt doen."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:6
msgid ""
"A tutorial takes you through one complete task, with every step spelled out "
"and a visible result at the end. You do not need to understand why a step "
"works to do it; the [explanation](../explanation/index.md) section and the "
"[reference](../reference/index.md) say why and what exactly."
msgstr ""
"Een zelfstudie neemt u mee door één volledige taak, met elke stap "
"uitgeschreven en aan het eind een zichtbaar resultaat. U hoeft niet te "
"begrijpen waarom een stap werkt om hem uit te voeren; de onderdelen "
"[uitleg](../explanation/index.md) en [naslag](../reference/index.md) zeggen "
"waarom en wat precies."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:10
msgid ""
"Each tutorial runs as a test of EW's own build, so its steps, menu paths and "
"keys are the ones the program has."
msgstr ""
"Elke zelfstudie draait als test van EW's eigen build, dus de stappen, "
"menupaden en toetsen zijn die van het programma."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:12 src/how-to/index.md:9
msgid "Available now"
msgstr "Nu beschikbaar"

#: src/tutorials/index.md:14
msgid ""
"[A first schematic page with potentials](first-page.md): a new project, a "
"transformer and two lamps placed and wired, the potentials named, the "
"Potentials navigator and the Messages pane read. You need EW installed and "
"an empty folder."
msgstr ""
"[Een eerste schemapagina met potentialen](first-page.md): een nieuw project, "
"een transformator en twee lampen geplaatst en bedraad, de potentialen "
"benoemd, de navigator Potentialen en het paneel Meldingen gelezen. U hebt EW "
"nodig en een lege map."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:17
msgid ""
"[A terminal strip and a cable](terminal-strip-and-cable.md): on the project "
"of the first tutorial, a terminal strip placed and wired on both sides, a "
"cable created from its part with the field wires as its cores, the strip "
"editor and the Cables navigator read. You need the project you saved at the "
"end of the first tutorial."
msgstr ""
"[Een klemmenstrook en een kabel](terminal-strip-and-cable.md): op het "
"project van de eerste zelfstudie een klemmenstrook geplaatst en aan beide "
"zijden bedraad, een kabel uit zijn artikel gemaakt met de velddraden als "
"aders, de klemmenstrookeditor en de navigator Kabels gelezen. U hebt het "
"project nodig dat u aan het eind van de eerste zelfstudie hebt opgeslagen."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:20
msgid ""
"[Reports and PDF](reports-and-pdf.md): on a copy of the sample project, its "
"report pages read, a parts list and a terminal overview added, the project "
"exported as one PDF and the print preview read. You need EW installed; the "
"copy is made in your projects folder, and a project you have open stays open."
msgstr ""
"[Rapporten en PDF](reports-and-pdf.md): op een kopie van het "
"voorbeeldproject de rapportpagina's gelezen, een stuklijst en een "
"klemmenoverzicht toegevoegd, het project als één PDF geëxporteerd en het "
"afdrukvoorbeeld bekeken. U hebt EW nodig; de kopie komt in uw projectenmap, "
"en een project dat u open hebt blijft open."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:23
msgid ""
"[Importing a QElectroTech project](import-qet.md): a small QElectroTech "
"project imported, its migration report read, its missing wire drawn and its "
"parts chosen, until the report holds only the record of the import. You need "
"EW installed and the tutorial's file "
"[lamp-circuit.qet](files/lamp-circuit.qet)."
msgstr ""
"[Een QElectroTech-project importeren](import-qet.md): een klein "
"QElectroTech-project geïmporteerd, het migratierapport gelezen, de "
"ontbrekende draad getekend en de artikelen gekozen, tot het rapport alleen "
"nog het verslag van de import bevat. U hebt EW nodig en het bestand "
"[lamp-circuit.qet](files/lamp-circuit.qet) van de zelfstudie."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:26
msgid ""
"[Draw a six-pin power block](symbol-editor.md): a power block drawn in the "
"symbol editor, checked on the connection grid, its eight orientations "
"previewed, saved to a library and tested on a new page. You need EW "
"installed and an empty folder."
msgstr ""
"[Een voedingsblok met zes aansluitingen tekenen](symbol-editor.md): een "
"voedingsblok getekend in de symbooleditor, gecontroleerd op het "
"verbindingsraster, zijn acht oriëntaties bekeken, opgeslagen in een "
"bibliotheek en getest op een nieuwe pagina. U hebt EW nodig en een lege map."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:29
msgid ""
"[A PLC with its modules](plc-station.md): a CPU with an input and an output "
"module made in the New station dialog and drawn on a page, a position switch "
"and a lamp wired to channels, their addresses and symbols in the PLC grid, "
"the PLC overview page and the export to PLC software. You need EW installed "
"and an empty folder."
msgstr ""
"[Een PLC met zijn modules](plc-station.md): een CPU met een ingangs- en een "
"uitgangsmodule, gemaakt in het venster Nieuw station en op een pagina "
"getekend, een positieschakelaar en een lamp aangesloten op kanalen, hun "
"adressen en symbolen in de PLC-tabel, de PLC-overzichtspagina en de export "
"naar PLC-software. U hebt EW nodig en een lege map."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:32
msgid ""
"[A bus network](bus-network.md): a bus-topology page with a CPU, a remote "
"I/O station and a switch from the generic parts, the bus lines, the node "
"addresses, a bus cable with its plugs, a frequency converter of your own "
"made in Parts, and the check. You need EW installed and an empty folder."
msgstr ""
"[Een busnetwerk](bus-network.md): een bustopologiepagina met een CPU, een "
"decentraal I/O-station en een switch uit de generieke artikelen, de "
"buslijnen, de adressen van de knooppunten, een buskabel met zijn stekkers, "
"een eigen frequentieregelaar gemaakt in Artikelen, en de controle. U hebt EW "
"nodig en een lege map."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:35
msgid ""
"[A pneumatic circuit](pneumatic-circuit.md): a fluid page with an air supply "
"and its medium, a service unit, a 5/2 solenoid valve and a double-acting "
"cylinder with flow controls, the tubes, the valve's solenoid wired to a PLC "
"output, a valve terminal with its positions filled, and the tube list. You "
"need EW installed and an empty folder."
msgstr ""
"[Een pneumatisch schema](pneumatic-circuit.md): een fluïdumpagina met een "
"persluchtvoeding en haar medium, een verzorgingseenheid, een "
"5/2-magneetventiel en een dubbelwerkende cilinder met "
"smoorterugslagventielen, de slangen, de magneetspoel van het ventiel "
"aangesloten op een PLC-uitgang, een ventieleiland met gevulde posities, en "
"de slangenlijst. U hebt EW nodig en een lege map."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:39
msgid ""
"The `ew` command-line program has a how-to of its own: [Use the command line "
"on the sample project](../how-to/headless-workflow.md)."
msgstr ""
"Het opdrachtregelprogramma `ew` heeft een eigen instructie: [De "
"opdrachtregel gebruiken op het "
"voorbeeldproject](../how-to/headless-workflow.md)."

#: src/tutorials/index.md:42
msgid "Planned"
msgstr "Gepland"

#: src/tutorials/index.md:44
msgid ""
"The documentation requirements (NFR-DOC-03) name the tutorial that follows "
"at version 1.0: 2D/3D layout with the wire list."
msgstr ""
"De documentatie-eisen (NFR-DOC-03) noemen de zelfstudie die bij versie 1.0 "
"volgt: 2D/3D-layout met de draadlijst."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you draw your first schematic page in the ElektraWatt "
"window: a small control transformer that feeds two cabinet lamps. You place "
"the symbols, draw the wires, give the two potentials their names and read "
"what EW knows about the circuit. At the end you have a saved project whose "
"checks show no error."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie tekent u uw eerste schemapagina in het venster van "
"ElektraWatt: een kleine besturingstransformator die twee kastlampen voedt. U "
"plaatst de symbolen, tekent de draden, geeft de twee potentialen hun naam en "
"leest wat EW over de schakeling weet. Aan het eind hebt u een opgeslagen "
"project waarvan de controles geen fout tonen."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:7 src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:8 src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:7 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:8 src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:8 src/tutorials/plc-station.md:17 src/tutorials/bus-network.md:12 src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:11 src/how-to/install.md:7 src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:7 src/how-to/choose-parts.md:7
msgid "What you need"
msgstr "Wat u nodig hebt"

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:9 src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:9 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:10 src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:10 src/tutorials/plc-station.md:19 src/tutorials/bus-network.md:14 src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:13
msgid ""
"ElektraWatt installed and started; see [Install "
"ElektraWatt](../how-to/install.md)."
msgstr ""
"ElektraWatt, geïnstalleerd en gestart; zie [ElektraWatt "
"installeren](../how-to/install.md)."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:10 src/tutorials/plc-station.md:20 src/tutorials/bus-network.md:15 src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:14
msgid "An empty folder for the project, or a place where EW may create one."
msgstr "Een lege map voor het project, of een plek waar EW er een mag maken."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:12
msgid "Nothing else: choose **Empty project** in the New project dialog."
msgstr "Verder niets: kies **Leeg project** in het venster Nieuw project."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:14 src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:13 src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:11 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:16 src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:13 src/tutorials/plc-station.md:22 src/tutorials/bus-network.md:17 src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:16 src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:12
msgid "Steps"
msgstr "Stappen"

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:16
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ New project…** (`Ctrl+N`). Type the name `first-page.ew`, "
"choose **Empty project** under **Start from** and press **Create**. EW "
"creates the project and shows its one empty page, folio 1, with its frame."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Nieuw project…** (`Ctrl+N`). Typ de naam `first-page.ew`, "
"kies onder **Beginnen met** **Leeg project** en kies **Maken**. EW maakt het "
"project en toont zijn ene lege pagina, folio 1, met zijn kader."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:19
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** (`I`). In the symbol picker, choose "
"**transformer-1ph** and click near the top left of the page. Then choose "
"**Draw ▸ Select** to put the tool away. The transformer -1T1 is on the page, "
"with its primary pins 1U1 and 2U1 at the top and its secondary pins 1U2 and "
"2U2 at the bottom."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbool…** (`I`). Kies in de symboolkiezer "
"**transformer-1ph** en klik linksboven op de pagina. Kies daarna **Tekenen ▸ "
"Selecteren** om het gereedschap weg te leggen. De transformator -1T1 staat "
"op de pagina, met de primaire aansluitpunten 1U1 en 2U1 aan de bovenkant en "
"de secundaire aansluitpunten 1U2 en 2U2 aan de onderkant."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:23
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** again, choose **lamp**, and click twice, "
"to the right of and below the transformer, to place two lamps side by side. "
"Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The lamps -1E1 and -1E2 are on the page, each with "
"the pins X1 at the top and X2 at the bottom."
msgstr ""
"Kies opnieuw **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbool…**, kies **lamp** en klik twee "
"keer, rechts van en onder de transformator, om twee lampen naast elkaar te "
"plaatsen. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De lampen -1E1 en -1E2 staan op de "
"pagina, elk met het aansluitpunt X1 aan de bovenkant en X2 aan de onderkant."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:26
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`). Click the transformer's pin 1U2, then the pin "
"X1 of -1E1: the wire is drawn. Draw three more wires the same way: from X1 "
"of -1E1 to X1 of -1E2, from 2U2 to X2 of -1E1, and from X2 of \\-1E1 to X2 "
"of -1E2. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The two lamps are wired in parallel to "
"the transformer's secondary. Each wire takes a number, such as 1.1."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`). Klik op het aansluitpunt 1U2 van de "
"transformator en dan op het aansluitpunt X1 van -1E1: de draad is getekend. "
"Teken op dezelfde manier nog drie draden: van X1 van -1E1 naar X1 van -1E2, "
"van 2U2 naar X2 van -1E1, en van X2 van -1E1 naar X2 van -1E2. Kies "
"**Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De twee lampen zijn parallel op de secundaire kant "
"van de transformator aangesloten. Elke draad krijgt een nummer, zoals 1.1."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:30
msgid ""
"Click the wire between the two lamps' X1 pins, then choose **Draw ▸ Connect "
"▸ Define potential…**. Type the name `24VAC`, the type `L` and the voltage "
"`24 V AC`, and confirm the form. Every wire of that potential now carries "
"the name 24VAC."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de draad tussen de aansluitpunten X1 van de twee lampen en kies "
"**Tekenen ▸ Verbinden ▸ Potentiaal definiëren…**. Typ de naam `24VAC`, het "
"type `L` en de spanning `24 V AC`, en bevestig het formulier. Elke draad van "
"die potentiaal draagt nu de naam 24VAC."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:33
msgid ""
"Click the wire between the two lamps' X2 pins, choose **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ "
"Define potential…**, and give it the name `0VAC`, the type `N` and the "
"voltage `24 V AC`. The circuit has its two named potentials."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de draad tussen de aansluitpunten X2 van de twee lampen, kies "
"**Tekenen ▸ Verbinden ▸ Potentiaal definiëren…** en geef hem de naam `0VAC`, "
"het type `N` en de spanning `24 V AC`. De schakeling heeft haar twee "
"benoemde potentialen."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:36
msgid ""
"Choose **View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Potentials**. The Potentials navigator "
"lists 0VAC and 24VAC, each with its two wires."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Beeld ▸ Panelen ▸ Navigators ▸ Potentialen**. De navigator "
"Potentialen toont 0VAC en 24VAC, elk met zijn twee draden."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:38
msgid ""
"Choose **Check ▸ Messages**. The Messages header shows E 0, W 0 and pending "
"5: five findings wait on the page you are drawing until you leave it or run "
"the checks. They are three devices without a part (EW-CORE-041: -1E1, -1E2 "
"and -1T1) and the transformer's two open primary pins (EW-POT-009: -1T1:1U1 "
"and -1T1:2U1)."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Controle ▸ Meldingen**. De kop van Meldingen toont F 0, W 0 en 5 "
"wachtend: vijf bevindingen wachten op de pagina waarop u tekent tot u haar "
"verlaat of de controles uitvoert. Het zijn drie apparaten zonder artikel "
"(EW-CORE-041: -1E1, -1E2 en -1T1) en de twee open primaire aansluitpunten "
"van de transformator (EW-POT-009: -1T1:1U1 en -1T1:2U1)."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:42
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Save** (`Ctrl+S`). The title bar no longer marks the project "
"as changed, and the status bar says Saved."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Opslaan** (`Ctrl+S`). De titelbalk markeert het project "
"niet meer als gewijzigd, en de statusbalk zegt Opgeslagen."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:45 src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:57 src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:41 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:56 src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:45 src/tutorials/plc-station.md:78 src/tutorials/bus-network.md:79 src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:121
msgid "What you learnt"
msgstr "Wat u hebt geleerd"

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:47
msgid ""
"**File ▸ New project…** makes a project in a folder of its own, empty or "
"from a template (see [Start a project and use "
"templates](../how-to/start-a-project.md))."
msgstr ""
"**Bestand ▸ Nieuw project…** maakt een project in een eigen map, leeg of uit "
"een sjabloon (zie [Een project beginnen en sjablonen "
"gebruiken](../how-to/start-a-project.md))."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:49
msgid ""
"A symbol you place is a function of a device, and the device gets its tag "
"(-1T1, -1E1) from the numbering scheme."
msgstr ""
"Een symbool dat u plaatst, is een functie van een apparaat, en het apparaat "
"krijgt zijn kenmerk (-1T1, -1E1) uit het nummeringsschema."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:50
msgid ""
"Wires join connection points; the wires that touch form a potential, which "
"you name with Define potential…."
msgstr ""
"Draden verbinden aansluitpunten; de draden die elkaar raken vormen een "
"potentiaal, die u benoemt met Potentiaal definiëren…."

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:51
msgid ""
"The checks run while you draw. A warning tells you what is still open; the "
"[rule reference](../reference/rules/index.md) explains each rule, and `F1` "
"on a finding opens its rule's page."
msgstr ""
"De controles lopen terwijl u tekent. Een waarschuwing zegt wat er nog open "
"staat; de [regelnaslag](../reference/rules/index.md) legt elke regel uit, en "
"`F1` op een bevinding opent de pagina van de regel."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you take the lamp circuit of the first tutorial out of the "
"cabinet: a third lamp sits on the machine, and it is fed through a terminal "
"strip in the cabinet and a cable. You place the strip's terminals, wire "
"their cabinet side and their field side, create the cable with its cores, "
"and read the strip in the strip editor and the cable in the Cables "
"navigator. At the end you have a saved project whose checks show no error."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie haalt u de lampschakeling van de eerste zelfstudie "
"gedeeltelijk uit de kast: een derde lamp zit op de machine en wordt gevoed "
"via een klemmenstrook in de kast en een kabel. U plaatst de klemmen van de "
"klemmenstrook, bedraadt hun kastzijde en hun veldzijde, maakt de kabel met "
"zijn aders, en leest de klemmenstrook in de klemmenstrookeditor en de kabel "
"in de navigator Kabels. Aan het eind hebt u een opgeslagen project waarvan "
"de controles geen fout tonen."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:10
msgid ""
"The project of the tutorial [A first schematic page with "
"potentials](first-page.md), `first-page.ew`, as you saved it at its end."
msgstr ""
"Het project van de zelfstudie [Een eerste schemapagina met "
"potentialen](first-page.md), `first-page.ew`, zoals u het aan het eind hebt "
"opgeslagen."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:15
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Open…** (`Ctrl+O`) and open `first-page.ew`. Its page shows "
"the transformer -1T1 and the lamps -1E1 and -1E2."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Openen…** (`Ctrl+O`) en open `first-page.ew`. De pagina "
"toont de transformator -1T1 en de lampen -1E1 en -1E2."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:17
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Terminal strip**. EW creates an empty terminal "
"strip. It takes its tag when its first terminal is placed."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Klemmenstrook**. EW maakt een lege "
"klemmenstrook. Die krijgt haar kenmerk zodra de eerste klem is geplaatst."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:19
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** (`I`) and choose **terminal**. In the list "
"of strips, choose the new strip. Click twice, to the right of -1E2 and level "
"with it, to place two terminals side by side. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The "
"terminals -1X1:1 and -1X1:2 are on the page. Each has its cabinet side, "
"clamp a, at the bottom and its field side, clamp b, at the top. Click a "
"terminal: the letters a and b stand at its two ends. The symbol looks the "
"same turned half round, so after **R** the letters show which end is which."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbool…** (`I`) en kies **terminal**. Kies in "
"de lijst met klemmenstroken de nieuwe klemmenstrook. Klik twee keer, rechts "
"van -1E2 en op dezelfde hoogte, om twee klemmen naast elkaar te plaatsen. "
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De klemmen -1X1:1 en -1X1:2 staan op de "
"pagina. Elke klem heeft zijn kastzijde, klemaansluiting a, aan de onderkant "
"en zijn veldzijde, klemaansluiting b, aan de bovenkant. Klik op een klem: de "
"letters a en b staan aan zijn twee uiteinden. Het symbool ziet er een halve "
"slag gedraaid hetzelfde uit, dus na **R** laten de letters zien welk "
"uiteinde welk is."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** again, choose **lamp**, and click above "
"and to the right of the terminals. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The lamp -1E3, "
"the lamp on the machine, is on the page."
msgstr ""
"Kies opnieuw **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbool…**, kies **lamp** en klik boven "
"en rechts van de klemmen. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De lamp -1E3, de "
"lamp op de machine, staat op de pagina."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:27
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`). Draw the cabinet side: from X1 of -1E2 to the "
"bottom of -1X1:1, and from X2 of -1E2 to the bottom of -1X1:2. Then the "
"field side: from the top of -1X1:1 to X1 of -1E3, and from the top of "
"\\-1X1:2 to X2 of -1E3. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The terminal -1X1:1 "
"carries 24VAC and -1X1:2 carries 0VAC to the lamp on the machine."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`). Teken de kastzijde: van X1 van -1E2 naar de "
"onderkant van -1X1:1, en van X2 van -1E2 naar de onderkant van -1X1:2. "
"Daarna de veldzijde: van de bovenkant van -1X1:1 naar X1 van -1E3, en van de "
"bovenkant van -1X1:2 naar X2 van -1E3. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De "
"klem -1X1:1 brengt 24VAC en -1X1:2 brengt 0VAC naar de lamp op de machine."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:31
msgid ""
"Click the field wire of -1X1:1, then Shift+click the field wire of -1X1:2. "
"Choose **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Cable…** (**Cable** on the Draw tab). The form "
"proposes the tag -1W1 and lists the two wires with the core each gets. Its "
"**Cable type** lists the cable parts that fit the wires, the closest first; "
"here type `3G1.5` as the **Size or part number** instead, a three-core cable "
"of 1.5 mm² whose last core is green-yellow, without a part: the first wire "
"gets the core BN, the second BU. Leave **Length (m)** empty for now: Check "
"project lists a cable without a length. Choose **Apply**. The toast says "
"\"Cable -1W1 made from 2 wires\": the cable is made with its three cores and "
"the two field wires are its cores, in one step that one Undo takes back. The "
"cable takes its page number from its first core, and the wires take the "
"cores' 1.5 mm² and colours. The page shows the cable's mark at once: a line "
"across the two wires with a slanted stroke on each, the core's name (BN, BU) "
"beside its wire, and at the line's start the tag -1W1 with the type `3G1.5` "
"under it. Drag the mark to move it along the wires; select it and drag its "
"tag to move the tag alone."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de velddraad van -1X1:1 en Shift-klik daarna op de velddraad van "
"-1X1:2. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Verbinden ▸ Kabel…** (**Kabel** op het tabblad "
"Tekenen). Het formulier stelt het kenmerk -1W1 voor en toont de twee draden "
"met de ader die elk krijgt. Zijn lijst **Kabeltype** toont de kabelartikelen "
"die bij de draden passen, de best passende eerst; typ hier in plaats daarvan "
"`3G1.5` bij **Maat of artikelnummer**, een kabel met drie aders van 1,5 mm² "
"waarvan de laatste ader groen-geel is, zonder artikel: de eerste draad "
"krijgt de ader BN, de tweede BU. Laat **Lengte (m)** voorlopig leeg: Project "
"controleren meldt een kabel zonder lengte. Kies **Toepassen**. De "
"notificatie zegt \"Kabel -1W1 gemaakt van 2 draden\": de kabel is gemaakt "
"met zijn drie aders en de twee velddraden zijn zijn aders, in één stap die "
"één keer Ongedaan maken terugneemt. De kabel krijgt zijn paginanummer van "
"zijn eerste ader, en de draden krijgen de 1,5 mm² en de kleuren van de "
"aders. De pagina toont de markering van de kabel meteen: een lijn dwars over "
"de twee draden met een schuin streepje op elke draad, de naam van de ader "
"(BN, BU) naast zijn draad, en aan het begin van de lijn het kenmerk -1W1 met "
"het type `3G1.5` eronder. Sleep de markering om hem langs de draden te "
"verschuiven; selecteer hem en sleep zijn kenmerk om alleen het kenmerk te "
"verschuiven."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:43
msgid ""
"Choose **View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Cables** and click the cable -1W1. In "
"the Properties pane, set **Length** to `5 m`. The Cables navigator lists the "
"cable -1W1 with its three cores: BN, BU and GNYE."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Beeld ▸ Panelen ▸ Navigators ▸ Kabels** en klik op de kabel -1W1. Zet "
"in het paneel Eigenschappen **Lengte** op `5 m`. De navigator Kabels toont "
"de kabel -1W1 met zijn drie aders: BN, BU en GNYE."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:46
msgid ""
"Click the terminal -1X1:1 and choose **Edit ▸ Strip editor**. The Terminal "
"strips navigator shows the strip -1X1 as a table: terminal 1 on 24VAC, with "
"-1E2:X1 on side 1 and the field wire to -1E3:X1 via -1W1 BN on side 2; "
"terminal 2 on 0VAC, with -1E2:X2 and -1E3:X2 via -1W1 BU."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de klem -1X1:1 en kies **Bewerken ▸ Klemmenstrookeditor**. De "
"navigator Klemmenstroken toont de klemmenstrook -1X1 als tabel: klem 1 op "
"24VAC, met -1E2:X1 aan zijde 1 en de velddraad naar -1E3:X1 via -1W1 BN aan "
"zijde 2; klem 2 op 0VAC, met -1E2:X2 en -1E3:X2 via -1W1 BU."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:49
msgid ""
"Choose **Check ▸ Check project** (`F7`). The toast says \"Check: 0 errors, 9 "
"warnings, 2 infos\". The warnings are the devices and the cable that have no "
"part yet and the transformer's open primary pins; the infos say that no "
"report covers the strip or the cable yet. The cable's warning offers "
"**Choose a cable type…**: its part dialog lists the cable parts that fit "
"first, and the others greyed with the reason."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Controle ▸ Project controleren** (`F7`). De notificatie zegt "
"\"Controle: 0 fouten, 9 waarschuwingen, 2 info's\". De waarschuwingen zijn "
"de apparaten en de kabel die nog geen artikel hebben en de open primaire "
"aansluitpunten van de transformator; de info's zeggen dat nog geen rapport "
"de klemmenstrook of de kabel dekt. De waarschuwing van de kabel biedt **Kies "
"een kabeltype…**: het artikelvenster toont eerst de kabelartikelen die "
"passen, en de andere grijs met de reden."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:54 src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:38 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:53 src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:42 src/tutorials/plc-station.md:75 src/tutorials/bus-network.md:76 src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:112
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Save** (`Ctrl+S`). The title bar no longer marks the project "
"as changed."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Opslaan** (`Ctrl+S`). De titelbalk markeert het project "
"niet meer als gewijzigd."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:59
msgid ""
"A terminal strip is a device of its own, and each terminal you place from "
"the strip's list is one of its terminals, with a cabinet side and a field "
"side."
msgstr ""
"Een klemmenstrook is een apparaat op zich, en elke klem die u uit de lijst "
"van de klemmenstrook plaatst, is een van haar klemmen, met een kastzijde en "
"een veldzijde."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:61
msgid ""
"**Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Cable…** makes the selected wires a cable's cores in one "
"form: the cable's type as you write it (`3G1.5`, `4X0.75`, or a cable part's "
"number) gives the cores, and each wire's core can be changed before Apply. "
"The same command on a cable's wire opens that cable, to add a wire or take "
"one out. A cable without a drawn cable line takes its page number from its "
"first core."
msgstr ""
"**Tekenen ▸ Verbinden ▸ Kabel…** maakt de geselecteerde draden in één "
"formulier tot de aders van een kabel: het type van de kabel zoals u het "
"schrijft (`3G1.5`, `4X0.75`, of het artikelnummer van een kabel) geeft de "
"aders, en de ader van elke draad kan vóór Toepassen worden gewijzigd. "
"Dezelfde opdracht op de draad van een kabel opent die kabel, om een draad "
"toe te voegen of eruit te halen. Een kabel zonder getekende kabellijn krijgt "
"zijn paginanummer van zijn eerste ader."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:65
msgid ""
"**New cable type…**, the last entry of the form's **Cable type** list, makes "
"a cable part of your own in Parts: the maker, the type, the part number, the "
"cores and their cross-section, with or without green-yellow, the cores' "
"colours (HD 308), numbers or your own, a shield and the outer diameter. It "
"reads the cable as it will show (`3G1.5 · BN BU GNYE`); **Save and add "
"another size** keeps all but the part number and the size, and Save comes "
"back to the Cable form with the new type chosen. A cable part opened in "
"Parts shows the same group."
msgstr ""
"**Nieuw kabeltype…**, de laatste keuze van de lijst **Kabeltype** van het "
"formulier, maakt in Artikelen een eigen kabelartikel: de fabrikant, het "
"type, het artikelnummer, de aders en hun doorsnede, met of zonder "
"groen-geel, de kleuren van de aders (HD 308), nummers of uw eigen indeling, "
"een afscherming en de buitendiameter. Het toont de kabel zoals hij zal "
"verschijnen (`3G1.5 · BN BU GNYE`); **Opslaan en nog een maat** houdt alles "
"behalve het artikelnummer en de maat, en Opslaan keert terug naar het "
"formulier Kabel met het nieuwe type gekozen. Een kabelartikel dat u in "
"Artikelen opent, toont dezelfde groep."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:69
msgid ""
"A cable's mark is drawn as panel builders draw it: one line across the "
"cable's wires, a stroke on each core with its number or colour beside it, "
"and the cable's tag with its type and size (`3G1.5`: three cores with "
"green-yellow, `4X0.75`: four without). A cable whose wires run over several "
"pages gets a mark on each."
msgstr ""
"De markering van een kabel wordt getekend zoals kastenbouwers dat doen: één "
"lijn dwars over de draden van de kabel, een streepje op elke ader met haar "
"nummer of kleur ernaast, en het kenmerk van de kabel met zijn type en maat "
"(`3G1.5`: drie aders met groen-geel, `4X0.75`: vier zonder). Een kabel "
"waarvan de draden over meerdere pagina's lopen, krijgt op elke pagina een "
"markering."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:72
msgid ""
"The strip editor and the Cables navigator show what passes through each "
"terminal and each core, from the drawing."
msgstr ""
"De klemmenstrookeditor en de navigator Kabels tonen, uit de tekening, wat er "
"door elke klem en elke ader gaat."

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:73
msgid ""
"The reports that list them, the terminal diagram and the cable list, are the "
"subject of [Reports and PDF](reports-and-pdf.md)."
msgstr ""
"De rapporten die ze opsommen, het klemmenschema en de kabellijst, zijn het "
"onderwerp van [Rapporten en PDF](reports-and-pdf.md)."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you work on a copy of the sample project, a complete "
"control-cabinet project. You read its report pages, add two more, write the "
"whole project as one PDF and look through the print preview. Report pages "
"are not drawn: EW generates them from the schematic, so they follow every "
"change."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie werkt u op een kopie van het voorbeeldproject, een "
"volledig besturingskastproject. U leest de rapportpagina's, voegt er twee "
"toe, schrijft het hele project als één PDF en bekijkt het afdrukvoorbeeld. "
"Rapportpagina's worden niet getekend: EW genereert ze uit het schema, dus ze "
"volgen elke wijziging."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:13
msgid ""
"Choose **Help ▸ Tutorials ▸ Reports and PDF**. This tutorial's page of the "
"guide opens at once, and EW makes a fresh copy of the sample project in a "
"new folder under `Tutorials` in your projects folder (`Documents`, "
"`ElektraWatt`); a toast says so, and **Show in folder** shows it. With a "
"project open, the copy opens in a second window and your project stays open; "
"without one it opens here, and the banner says \"A fresh copy of the sample "
"project was made for the tutorials.\""
msgstr ""
"Kies **Help ▸ Zelfstudies ▸ Rapporten en PDF**. De pagina van deze "
"zelfstudie in de gebruikershandleiding opent meteen, en EW maakt een verse "
"kopie van het voorbeeldproject in een nieuwe map onder `Tutorials` in uw "
"projectenmap (`Documents`, `ElektraWatt`); een notificatie zegt dat, en "
"**Tonen in map** toont hem. Met een open project opent de kopie in een "
"tweede venster en blijft uw project open; zonder project opent hij hier, en "
"de banner zegt dat er een verse kopie van het voorbeeldproject voor de "
"zelfstudies is gemaakt."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:18
msgid ""
"Read the report pages in the Pages navigator, in section 9 (folios 900 to "
"999). They are marked generated: the parts list (Partlist, 901 and 901.1), "
"the legend of the panel layout 902 (902.1 and 902.2), the terminal diagrams "
"of the three terminal strips (903), the cable overview and diagrams (906), "
"the wire list, the PLC overview, the potential overview, the device tag "
"overview, the revision overview, and the workshop lists: the assembly lists "
"(912), the cut list (913) and the consumables (914). Click one, such as 903, "
"to see it."
msgstr ""
"Lees de rapportpagina's in de navigator Pagina's, in sectie 9 (folio's 900 "
"tot en met 999). Ze zijn gemarkeerd als gegenereerd: de stuklijst (Partlist, "
"901 en 901.1), de legenda van de paneellayout 902 (902.1 en 902.2), de "
"klemmenschema's van de drie klemmenstroken (903), het kabeloverzicht met de "
"kabelschema's (906), de draadlijst, het PLC-overzicht, het "
"potentiaaloverzicht, het overzicht van de apparaatkenmerken, het "
"revisieoverzicht en de werkplaatslijsten: de montagelijsten (912), de "
"zaaglijst (913) en de verbruiksmaterialen (914). Klik op een ervan, zoals "
"903, om hem te bekijken."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Report block…** (on the Page or the Output tab: "
"**Report**). In the form, choose the report **Parts list**, keep or type the "
"first folio `915`, type the title `Parts list`, and choose **Create**. The "
"toast says \"Added the report block Parts list from folio 915\". The Pages "
"navigator lists the new pages 915 to 915.2: the parts list device by device, "
"where the Partlist on 901 sums the parts up."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Uitvoer ▸ Rapportblok…** (op het tabblad Pagina of Uitvoer: "
"**Rapport**). Kies in het formulier het rapport **Stuklijst**, houd of typ "
"het eerste folio `915`, typ de titel `Parts list` en kies **Maken**. De "
"notificatie zegt \"Rapportblok Stuklijst toegevoegd vanaf folio 915\". De "
"navigator Pagina's toont de nieuwe pagina's 915 tot en met 915.2: de "
"stuklijst per apparaat, waar de Partlist op 901 de artikelen optelt."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:28
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Report block…** again, choose **Terminal overview**, the "
"first folio `916` and the title `Terminal overview`, and choose **Create**. "
"The page 916 lists every terminal strip of the project on one page."
msgstr ""
"Kies opnieuw **Uitvoer ▸ Rapportblok…**, kies **Klemmenoverzicht**, het "
"eerste folio `916` en de titel `Terminal overview`, en kies **Maken**. "
"Pagina 916 toont alle klemmenstroken van het project op één pagina."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:31
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Export PDF…** (`Ctrl+Shift+E`) and save the file as "
"`reports.pdf`. When the export is done, the toast says \"Exported "
"reports.pdf: 10 drawing pages, 3 section divider pages and 32 report "
"pages\", with the file's size. Open the file from the toast to see it: one "
"PDF/A-2b file with every page, its outline and its cross-reference links."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Uitvoer ▸ PDF exporteren…** (`Ctrl+Shift+E`) en sla het bestand op "
"als `reports.pdf`. Als de export klaar is, zegt de notificatie \"reports.pdf "
"geëxporteerd: 10 tekeningpagina's, 3 scheidingspagina's en 32 "
"rapportpagina's\", met de grootte van het bestand. Open het bestand vanuit "
"de notificatie om het te bekijken: één PDF/A-2b-bestand met elke pagina, een "
"inhoudsopgave en links voor de kruisverwijzingen."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:35
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Print…** (`Ctrl+P`). The Print dialog shows the range, the "
"paper and a preview of the first page: \"1 of 46\". Step to the next page in "
"the preview to see page 2. Choose **Cancel** to close the dialog without "
"printing."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Uitvoer ▸ Afdrukken…** (`Ctrl+P`). Het venster Afdrukken toont het "
"bereik, het papier en een voorbeeld van de eerste pagina: \"1 van 46\". Ga "
"in het voorbeeld naar de volgende pagina om pagina 2 te zien. Kies "
"**Annuleren** om het venster te sluiten zonder af te drukken."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:43
msgid ""
"A report block generates report pages from the project: a parts list, "
"terminal diagrams, a cable list and more. **Output ▸ Report block…** (the "
"Output tab's **Report**) adds one, from the first folio you give: every "
"list, a tube list or a PLC card list too, is made this way; a list is no "
"page type of **New page**. Without a title of your own, its pages take the "
"report's name (\"915 Tube list\"). A block with nothing to list yet makes no "
"page: its toast says so and what gives it rows, such as a valve terminal for "
"the valve-terminal configuration."
msgstr ""
"Een rapportblok genereert rapportpagina's uit het project: een stuklijst, "
"klemmenschema's, een kabellijst en meer. **Uitvoer ▸ Rapportblok…** "
"(**Rapport** op het tabblad Uitvoer) voegt er een toe, vanaf het eerste "
"folio dat u opgeeft: elke lijst, ook een slangenlijst of een "
"PLC-kaartenlijst, wordt zo gemaakt; een lijst is geen paginatype van "
"**Nieuwe pagina**. Zonder eigen titel krijgen de pagina's de naam van het "
"rapport (\"915 Slangenlijst\"). Een blok dat nog niets te tonen heeft, maakt "
"geen pagina: de notificatie zegt dat en wat er rijen aan geeft, zoals een "
"ventieleiland voor de ventieleilandconfiguratie."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:48
msgid ""
"Report pages follow the drawing: change a device, a terminal or a cable, and "
"its report pages change with it."
msgstr ""
"Rapportpagina's volgen de tekening: verander een apparaat, een klem of een "
"kabel, en de rapportpagina's veranderen mee."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:49
msgid ""
"**Output ▸ Export PDF…** writes every page into one PDF; **Output ▸ Print…** "
"shows each page before it prints."
msgstr ""
"**Uitvoer ▸ PDF exporteren…** schrijft elke pagina in één PDF; **Uitvoer ▸ "
"Afdrukken…** toont elke pagina voordat die wordt afgedrukt."

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:50
msgid ""
"[A terminal strip and a cable](terminal-strip-and-cable.md) shows where the "
"terminal diagrams and the cable list take their rows from."
msgstr ""
"[Een klemmenstrook en een kabel](terminal-strip-and-cable.md) laat zien waar "
"de klemmenschema's en de kabellijst hun rijen vandaan halen."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you bring a small QElectroTech project into ElektraWatt: a "
"24 V supply that feeds a lamp through a switch. You import it, read the "
"migration report that says what the import changed, guessed or left out, and "
"fix the findings that are yours to fix, until the report shows only the "
"record of the import itself. At the end you have a saved EW project."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie brengt u een klein QElectroTech-project over naar "
"ElektraWatt: een voeding van 24 V die via een schakelaar een lamp voedt. U "
"importeert het, leest het migratierapport dat zegt wat de import heeft "
"veranderd, geraden of weggelaten, en lost de bevindingen op die aan u zijn, "
"tot het rapport alleen nog het verslag van de import zelf toont. Aan het "
"eind hebt u een opgeslagen EW-project."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:11
msgid ""
"The tutorial's QElectroTech project, "
"[lamp-circuit.qet](files/lamp-circuit.qet). Save it in a folder of your own."
msgstr ""
"Het QElectroTech-project van de zelfstudie, "
"[lamp-circuit.qet](files/lamp-circuit.qet). Sla het op in een eigen map."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:13
msgid ""
"The project has one folio with four elements: the supply G1, the switch S1, "
"a lamp without a label and an earth symbol wired to the supply's PE "
"terminal. One conductor is missing on purpose: the lamp's return to the "
"supply."
msgstr ""
"Het project heeft één folio met vier elementen: de voeding G1, de schakelaar "
"S1, een lamp zonder label en een aardsymbool dat op de PE-aansluiting van de "
"voeding is aangesloten. Eén geleider ontbreekt met opzet: de terugleiding "
"van de lamp naar de voeding."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:18
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Import…**, pick `lamp-circuit.qet`, and name the new "
"project's folder `lamp-circuit.ew`. The import's card says what it will "
"make: \"Create project makes a new project in the folder lamp-circuit.ew, "
"with 1 page, 4 devices and 3 connections.\" Its verdict follows: \"The file "
"was imported. 7 points are worth a look, and none stops you from working.\" "
"Under **Worth a look** and **For your information** it lists the kinds of "
"findings, each with its count. Nothing is written yet."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Importeren…**, kies `lamp-circuit.qet` en geef de map van "
"het nieuwe project de naam `lamp-circuit.ew`. De kaart van de import zegt "
"wat er gemaakt wordt: \"Project maken maakt een nieuw project in de map "
"lamp-circuit.ew, met 1 pagina, 4 apparaten en 3 verbindingen.\" Daarna volgt "
"het oordeel: \"Het bestand is geïmporteerd. 7 punten zijn het bekijken "
"waard; geen ervan houdt u op.\" Onder **Het bekijken waard** en **Ter "
"informatie** staan de soorten bevindingen, elk met het aantal. Er is nog "
"niets geschreven."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **Create project**. EW creates the project and opens it. The folio "
"looks as it did in QElectroTech: the frame and the title block, the labels "
"S1 and G1, and the three conductors where QElectroTech drew them. The "
"Messages pane shows the **Migration report**, chosen in its header beside "
"the project's own scheme: no errors, 7 warnings and 7 infos."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Project maken**. EW maakt het project en opent het. Het folio ziet "
"eruit zoals in QElectroTech: het kader en het titelblok, de labels S1 en G1, "
"en de drie geleiders waar QElectroTech ze tekende. Het paneel Meldingen "
"toont het **Migratierapport**, gekozen in de kop van het paneel naast het "
"eigen schema van het project: geen fouten, 7 waarschuwingen en 7 info's."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:28
msgid ""
"Read the report, rule by rule. Some findings are the record of the import, "
"and stay:"
msgstr ""
"Lees het rapport per controleregel. Sommige bevindingen zijn het verslag van "
"de import en blijven staan:"

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:29
msgid ""
"EW-IMP-119: the supply's element has no meaning EW knows, so G1 came in as a "
"generic black box."
msgstr ""
"EW-IMP-119: het element van de voeding heeft geen betekenis die EW kent, dus "
"G1 is als algemene black box binnengekomen."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:30
msgid ""
"EW-IMP-103 and EW-NUM-001: the labels S1 and G1 are kept as frozen tags, "
"because they do not follow the project's numbering."
msgstr ""
"EW-IMP-103 en EW-NUM-001: de labels S1 en G1 blijven als vastgezette "
"kenmerken bewaard, omdat ze de nummering van het project niet volgen."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:32
msgid ""
"EW-IMP-122: the lamp and the earth had no label. EW gave them the tags -1A1 "
"and -1X1, and the page prints no tag for them, as QElectroTech printed none."
msgstr ""
"EW-IMP-122: de lamp en de aarde hadden geen label. EW gaf ze de kenmerken "
"-1A1 en -1X1, en de pagina drukt voor hen geen kenmerk af, zoals "
"QElectroTech er geen afdrukte."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:34
msgid ""
"EW-IMP-117: the file uses QElectroTech's default cross-reference formula."
msgstr ""
"EW-IMP-117: het bestand gebruikt de standaardformule van QElectroTech voor "
"kruisverwijzingen."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:35
msgid "EW-IMP-006: the file's font, Sans Serif, is printed in Noto Sans."
msgstr ""
"EW-IMP-006: het lettertype van het bestand, Sans Serif, wordt in Noto Sans "
"afgedrukt."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:37
msgid ""
"The others are live checks of the project, and yours to fix: EW-POT-009 says "
"that the lamp's pin X2 and the supply's pin - are not connected; and "
"EW-CORE-041 says that none of the four devices has a part."
msgstr ""
"De andere zijn actuele controles van het project, en aan u om op te lossen: "
"EW-POT-009 zegt dat het aansluitpunt X2 van de lamp en het aansluitpunt - "
"van de voeding niet verbonden zijn; en EW-CORE-041 zegt dat geen van de vier "
"apparaten een artikel heeft."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:39
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`). Click the lamp's pin X2, at its bottom, then "
"the supply's pin -, at the bottom of G1: the return wire is drawn. Choose "
"**Draw ▸ Select**. The two EW-POT-009 warnings are gone. While you edit, the "
"Messages pane shows the project's own scheme, drafting."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`). Klik op het aansluitpunt X2 van de lamp, aan "
"de onderkant, en dan op het aansluitpunt - van de voeding, aan de onderkant "
"van G1: de terugleiding is getekend. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De twee "
"waarschuwingen EW-POT-009 zijn weg. Terwijl u bewerkt, toont het paneel "
"Meldingen het eigen schema van het project."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:42
msgid ""
"Click the lamp and choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**. Type `lamp`, choose "
"**EW-GEN-LMP-1**, and confirm with **Choose**. The lamp has its part."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de lamp en kies **Bewerken ▸ Artikel kiezen…**. Typ `lamp`, kies "
"**EW-GEN-LMP-1** en bevestig met **Kiezen**. De lamp heeft haar artikel."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:45
msgid ""
"Click the switch S1, choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**, type `on/off` and "
"choose **EW-GEN-SW-1**."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de schakelaar S1, kies **Bewerken ▸ Artikel kiezen…**, typ `on/off` "
"en kies **EW-GEN-SW-1**."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:46
msgid ""
"Click the supply G1, choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**, type `power supply` and "
"choose **EW-GEN-PSU-1**."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de voeding G1, kies **Bewerken ▸ Artikel kiezen…**, typ `power "
"supply` en kies **EW-GEN-PSU-1**."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:47
msgid ""
"Click the earth, choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**, type `earth` and choose "
"**EW-GEN-TRM-2**. Every device has its part, and EW-CORE-041 is gone."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de aarde, kies **Bewerken ▸ Artikel kiezen…**, typ `earth` en kies "
"**EW-GEN-TRM-2**. Elk apparaat heeft zijn artikel, en EW-CORE-041 is weg."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:49
msgid ""
"Choose **Check ▸ Check project** (`F7`). The Messages pane shows the "
"migration report again, with no error, 1 warning (EW-IMP-119) and 7 infos "
"(EW-IMP-006, EW-IMP-103 twice, EW-IMP-117, EW-IMP-122 and EW-NUM-001 twice): "
"the record of the import, nothing left to fix."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Controle ▸ Project controleren** (`F7`). Het paneel Meldingen toont "
"weer het migratierapport, met geen fout, 1 waarschuwing (EW-IMP-119) en 7 "
"info's (EW-IMP-006, twee keer EW-IMP-103, EW-IMP-117, EW-IMP-122 en twee "
"keer EW-NUM-001): het verslag van de import, er is niets meer op te lossen."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:58
msgid ""
"**File ▸ Import…** says what an import will make before it writes anything, "
"and makes a new EW project of its own; the QElectroTech file is not changed."
msgstr ""
"**Bestand ▸ Importeren…** zegt wat een import maakt voordat er iets wordt "
"geschreven, en maakt een nieuw, eigen EW-project; het QElectroTech-bestand "
"verandert niet."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:60
msgid ""
"The folios arrive as QElectroTech drew them: the wires on their paths, the "
"texts, shapes and images, the frame and the title block."
msgstr ""
"De folio's komen binnen zoals QElectroTech ze tekende: de draden op hun "
"paden, de teksten, vormen en afbeeldingen, het kader en het titelblok."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:62
msgid ""
"The migration report lists two kinds of finding: the import's own record "
"(the EW-IMP rules, and the frozen tags), which stays as a note of what "
"changed, and the live checks of the project, which go away when you fix them."
msgstr ""
"Het migratierapport kent twee soorten bevindingen: het eigen verslag van de "
"import (de regels EW-IMP en de vastgezette kenmerken), dat blijft staan als "
"notitie van wat er veranderde, en de actuele controles van het project, die "
"verdwijnen als u ze oplost."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:64
msgid ""
"In the Messages pane's header you switch between the migration report and "
"the project's own scheme."
msgstr ""
"In de kop van het paneel Meldingen wisselt u tussen het migratierapport en "
"het eigen schema van het project."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:65
msgid ""
"[QElectroTech and EW](../explanation/qet-and-ew.md) explains how "
"QElectroTech's elements, labels, conductors and folio reports become EW's "
"functions, devices, wires and interruption points."
msgstr ""
"[QElectroTech en EW](../explanation/qet-and-ew.md) legt uit hoe de "
"elementen, labels, geleiders en folioverwijzingen van QElectroTech de "
"functies, apparaten, draden en onderbrekingspunten van EW worden."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:68
msgid "Binding elements with a symbol map"
msgstr "Elementen koppelen met een symbooltabel"

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:70
msgid ""
"Without help, every element comes in with a function EW makes up for it. A "
"symbol map binds an element to one of EW's own coils, contacts, terminals or "
"interruption points while it keeps its drawing, place and texts, so that its "
"device can take a part and its tag, number and cross-reference follow the "
"project."
msgstr ""
"Zonder hulp komt elk element binnen met een functie die EW ervoor bedenkt. "
"Een symbooltabel koppelt een element aan een van EW's eigen spoelen, "
"contacten, klemmen of onderbrekingspunten, terwijl het zijn tekening, plaats "
"en teksten houdt, zodat zijn apparaat een artikel kan krijgen en zijn "
"kenmerk, nummer en kruisverwijzing het project volgen."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:74
msgid ""
"The map is a table `symbol-map.csv` in the folder of the `.qet` file, one "
"row per element:"
msgstr ""
"De symbooltabel is een tabel `symbol-map.csv` in de map van het "
"`.qet`-bestand, één regel per element:"

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:83
msgid ""
"`element`: the element's file name, a longer end of its collection path, or "
"its UUID."
msgstr ""
"`element`: de bestandsnaam van het element, een langer einde van zijn pad in "
"de collectie, of zijn UUID."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:84
msgid ""
"`function`: the key of an EW coil, contact, terminal or interruption point, "
"such as `ew.fn.coil`."
msgstr ""
"`function`: de sleutel van een spoel, contact, klem of onderbrekingspunt van "
"EW, zoals `ew.fn.coil`."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:85
msgid ""
"`cps`: the function's connection points, one for each terminal of the "
"element in its order; empty, in order."
msgstr ""
"`cps`: de aansluitpunten van de functie, één voor elke aansluiting van het "
"element in zijn volgorde; leeg, op volgorde."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:86
msgid ""
"`tag`, `number`, `xref`: the element's text that prints the tag, a "
"terminal's number and an interruption point's cross-reference: `label`, "
"`user` (its first user text) or the name of an element information; empty, "
"the usual one."
msgstr ""
"`tag`, `number`, `xref`: de tekst van het element die het kenmerk, het "
"nummer van een klem en de kruisverwijzing van een onderbrekingspunt drukt: "
"`label`, `user` (zijn eerste eigen tekst) of de naam van een "
"elementinformatie; leeg, de gewone."

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:90
msgid ""
"**File ▸ Import…** reads the map when it lies beside the file: the import "
"card says so, and the migration report says what each row bound. For a "
"project you imported before, put the map beside the file and choose "
"**Project ▸ Re-import QElectroTech project…**: the re-import applies the "
"map, and keeps what you changed since. A wrong row, such as an unknown "
"function, a connection point the function does not have or a row that does "
"not fit its element, stops the import with the line and the column at fault, "
"and nothing is imported."
msgstr ""
"**Bestand ▸ Importeren…** leest de symbooltabel als die naast het bestand "
"staat: de importkaart zegt het, en het migratierapport zegt wat elke regel "
"heeft gekoppeld. Voor een project dat u eerder importeerde, zet u de tabel "
"naast het bestand en kiest u **Project ▸ QElectroTech-project opnieuw "
"importeren…**: de nieuwe import past de tabel toe en houdt wat u sindsdien "
"veranderde. Een foute regel, zoals een onbekende functie, een aansluitpunt "
"dat de functie niet heeft of een regel die niet bij zijn element past, stopt "
"de import met de regel en de kolom die fout zijn, en er wordt niets "
"geïmporteerd."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you draw a reusable symbol: a power block with six "
"connections, designated 1 to 5 and FE. You check it on the connection grid, "
"preview its eight orientations, save it to a library, keep a portable copy "
"of the draft and test the symbol on a new page of a project. At the end you "
"have a library with the symbol and a saved project with a test copy, ready "
"to wire."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie tekent u een herbruikbaar symbool: een voedingsblok met "
"zes aansluitingen, aangeduid met 1 tot en met 5 en FE. U controleert het op "
"het verbindingsraster, bekijkt zijn acht oriëntaties, slaat het op in een "
"bibliotheek, bewaart een draagbare kopie van het concept en test het symbool "
"op een nieuwe pagina van een project. Aan het eind hebt u een bibliotheek "
"met het symbool en een opgeslagen project met een testkopie, klaar om te "
"bedraden."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:11
msgid "An empty folder."
msgstr "Een lege map."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:15
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ New project…** (`Ctrl+N`) and pick an empty folder named "
"`symbol-scratch.ew`."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Nieuw project…** (`Ctrl+N`) en wijs een lege map aan met de "
"naam `symbol-scratch.ew`."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:16
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol editor…**. The editor takes the place of the "
"page and shows its start card: choose **New symbol**. With nothing selected, "
"**Properties** on the right shows the symbol's own rows: type **Power "
"block** as its **Name**, and under **More** the **Key** `example.power`."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbooleditor…**. De editor neemt de plaats van "
"de pagina in en toont zijn startkaart: kies **Nieuw symbool**. Zonder "
"selectie toont **Eigenschappen** rechts de eigen rijen van het symbool: typ "
"**Power block** als **Naam**, en onder **Meer** bij **Sleutel** "
"`example.power`."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:19
msgid ""
"Choose **Rectangle** and draw the body from `(4, 0)` to `(24, 28)` mm: drag "
"from corner to corner, or click both corners. The rectangle is selected, and "
"its rows in **Properties** (x, y, width, height) take exact numbers."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Rechthoek** en teken de behuizing van `(4, 0)` tot `(24, 28)` mm: "
"sleep van hoek naar hoek, of klik op beide hoeken. De rechthoek is "
"geselecteerd, en zijn rijen in **Eigenschappen** (x, y, breedte, hoogte) "
"nemen exacte getallen aan."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:21
msgid ""
"Choose **Connection point**. The card at the drawing's top left holds its "
"**Designation** and **Direction**: set the direction to **Facing left** and "
"click six connection points at x = 0 mm and y = 4, 8, 12, 16, 20 and 24 mm; "
"the designation counts on from 1, and the last one is retyped as FE in its "
"**Designation** row. Choose **Line** and draw a short lead from each "
"connection point to the body's left edge, at x = 4 mm."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Aansluitpunt**. De kaart linksboven in de tekening bevat zijn "
"**Aanduiding** en **Richting**: zet de richting op **Links** en klik zes "
"aansluitpunten op x = 0 mm en y = 4, 8, 12, 16, 20 en 24 mm; de aanduiding "
"telt door vanaf 1, en de laatste wordt in zijn rij **Aanduiding** omgetypt "
"naar FE. Kies **Lijn** en teken van elk aansluitpunt een korte aansluitlijn "
"naar de linkerrand van de behuizing, op x = 4 mm."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:25
msgid ""
"Choose **Text field**. On its card choose what the page writes there: the "
"number of each connection point, placed beside that point, and the **Device "
"tag**, placed above the body. The outline on the left lists the connection "
"points, the texts and the shapes, and says **Ready to save**. FE is a "
"printed designation: before you use the symbol, select the FE point and give "
"it its **Potential**, and never confuse it with PE."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekstveld**. Kies op zijn kaart wat de pagina daar schrijft: het "
"nummer van elk aansluitpunt, naast dat punt geplaatst, en het "
"**Apparaatkenmerk**, boven de behuizing geplaatst. Het overzicht links toont "
"de aansluitpunten, de teksten en de vormen, en zegt **Klaar om op te "
"slaan**. FE is een opgedrukte aanduiding: selecteer voordat u het symbool "
"gebruikt het punt FE, geef het zijn **Potentiaal**, en verwar het nooit met "
"PE."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:30
msgid ""
"Click B to H under **Turned and mirrored**: the drawing shows the symbol "
"turned and mirrored, every pin keeping its number and turning its direction "
"with the drawing. Choose A to edit again."
msgstr ""
"Klik op B tot en met H onder **Gedraaid en gespiegeld**: de tekening toont "
"het symbool gedraaid en gespiegeld, en elke pin houdt zijn nummer en draait "
"zijn richting met de tekening mee. Kies A om weer te bewerken."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:32
msgid ""
"Select the first connection point (click it in the drawing or in the "
"outline) and change its **x (mm)** to 4.5. **To do** says \"Connection point "
"1 is off the connection grid. Move it onto the grid before saving.\"; a "
"click on the sentence selects the point. Press `Ctrl+Z` to undo the change. "
"Fine drawing coordinates, such as 0.5 mm, remain valid."
msgstr ""
"Selecteer het eerste aansluitpunt (klik erop in de tekening of in het "
"overzicht) en wijzig zijn **x (mm)** in 4.5. **Nog te doen** zegt "
"\"Aansluitpunt 1 ligt buiten het verbindingsraster. Verplaats het naar het "
"raster voordat u opslaat.\"; een klik op de zin selecteert het punt. Druk op "
"`Ctrl+Z` om de wijziging ongedaan te maken. Fijne tekencoördinaten, zoals "
"0,5 mm, blijven geldig."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:36
msgid ""
"Click empty paper, so that nothing is selected, type `symbols.ewlib` as the "
"**Library folder** and choose **Save**: a name without a folder is a library "
"in your user library folder. A message says where the symbol was saved."
msgstr ""
"Klik op het lege papier, zodat niets is geselecteerd, typ `symbols.ewlib` "
"als **Bibliotheekmap** en kies **Opslaan**: een naam zonder map is een "
"bibliotheek in uw gebruikersbibliotheekmap. Een melding zegt waar het "
"symbool is opgeslagen."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:38
msgid ""
"Open **Draft** in the command bar and choose **Export draft**, and name the "
"file `power.symbol.json`; it is written beside the library. The file is the "
"portable draft, with the identity the save gave it."
msgstr ""
"Open **Concept** in de opdrachtbalk, kies **Concept exporteren** en geef het "
"bestand de naam `power.symbol.json`; het wordt naast de bibliotheek "
"geschreven. Het bestand is het draagbare concept, met de identiteit die het "
"opslaan het gaf."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:40
msgid ""
"Choose **Test on a page**. The editor makes way for a new page with a test "
"copy of the symbol selected, ready to wire."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Testen op een pagina**. De editor maakt plaats voor een nieuwe pagina "
"met een geselecteerde testkopie van het symbool, klaar om te bedraden."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:47
msgid ""
"The symbol editor keeps its own draft and undo history; saving it to a "
"library does not change placed copies. A changed symbol is saved as the next "
"version, 1.0.1 after 1.0.0."
msgstr ""
"De symbooleditor heeft een eigen concept en een eigen geschiedenis om "
"ongedaan te maken; opslaan in een bibliotheek verandert geplaatste kopieën "
"niet. Een gewijzigd symbool wordt opgeslagen als de volgende versie, 1.0.1 "
"na 1.0.0."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:49
msgid ""
"Connection points must lie on the 4 mm connection grid; the editor refuses "
"to save a point off it, and says which."
msgstr ""
"Aansluitpunten moeten op het verbindingsraster van 4 mm liggen; de editor "
"weigert een punt daarbuiten op te slaan, en zegt welk punt."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:50
msgid ""
"**Close editor** keeps the draft and its undo history: **Draw ▸ Place ▸ "
"Symbol editor…** returns to it. In a later session, choose **Start**, then "
"**Import a file…**, and open `power.symbol.json`: its next save updates the "
"same symbol."
msgstr ""
"**Editor sluiten** bewaart het concept en de geschiedenis om ongedaan te "
"maken: **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbooleditor…** keert ernaar terug. Kies in "
"een latere sessie **Start**, dan **Een bestand importeren…**, en open "
"`power.symbol.json`: de volgende keer opslaan werkt hetzelfde symbool bij."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:53
msgid ""
"What you know from a page works on the drawing: a click selects, a dragged "
"rectangle selects several things, the arrow keys move them a grid step, and "
"`Delete`, `Ctrl+C`, `Ctrl+V` and `Ctrl+D` do what they do on a page."
msgstr ""
"Wat u van een pagina kent, werkt ook op de tekening: een klik selecteert, "
"een opgetrokken rechthoek selecteert meerdere dingen, de pijltjestoetsen "
"verschuiven ze een rasterstap, en `Delete`, `Ctrl+C`, `Ctrl+V` en `Ctrl+D` "
"doen wat ze op een pagina doen."

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:56
msgid ""
"The same workflow is available to AI tools and repeatable scripts in "
"[`symbol-editor.ewui`](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/book/tutorial-scripts/symbol-editor.ewui). "
"Run it in a fresh directory:"
msgstr ""
"Dezelfde werkwijze is beschikbaar voor AI-hulpmiddelen en herhaalbare "
"scripts in "
"[`symbol-editor.ewui`](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/book/tutorial-scripts/symbol-editor.ewui). "
"Voer het uit in een nieuwe map:"

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:64
msgid ""
"The script creates the project beside itself and the library and "
"`power.symbol.json` in your user library folder, and reopens the draft. See "
"the [reference](../reference/symbol-editor.md) for JSON, actions and import "
"compatibility."
msgstr ""
"Het script maakt het project naast zichzelf en de bibliotheek en "
"`power.symbol.json` in uw gebruikersbibliotheekmap, en opent het concept "
"opnieuw. Zie de [naslag](../reference/symbol-editor.md) voor JSON, acties en "
"importcompatibiliteit."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you draw a small PLC in a new project: a CPU with a digital "
"input module and a digital output module (the window says _module_ for what "
"you may call a card). A position switch on a door is wired to the first "
"input and a pilot lamp to the first output; you give both their PLC symbols, "
"add the PLC overview page and export the hardware for your PLC software. It "
"takes about fifteen minutes."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie tekent u een kleine PLC in een nieuw project: een CPU met "
"een digitale ingangsmodule en een digitale uitgangsmodule (het venster zegt "
"_module_ voor wat u misschien een kaart noemt). Een positieschakelaar op een "
"deur komt op de eerste ingang en een signaallamp op de eerste uitgang; u "
"geeft beide hun PLC-symbool, voegt de PLC-overzichtspagina toe en exporteert "
"de hardware voor uw PLC-software. Het duurt ongeveer een kwartier."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:8
msgid "Three words of the program, in the trade's meaning:"
msgstr "Drie woorden van het programma, in de betekenis van het vak:"

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:10
msgid ""
"A **station** is a CPU or a remote I/O head with the modules it carries: "
"what TIA Portal calls a device in its network view."
msgstr ""
"Een **station** is een CPU of een decentrale I/O-kop met de modules die hij "
"draagt: wat TIA Portal in zijn netwerkweergave een apparaat noemt."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:12
msgid ""
"Its **rack** is the row of **slots** its modules sit in, numbered from 1. "
"The slot order gives the addresses: the first input module starts at I0.0."
msgstr ""
"Zijn **rek** is de rij **slots** waarin zijn modules zitten, genummerd vanaf "
"1. De volgorde van de slots geeft de adressen: de eerste ingangsmodule "
"begint bij I0.0."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:14
msgid ""
"A **symbol** here is the PLC tag name of a channel, such as `Door_closed`, "
"as in the PLC tag table; the drawing symbol of a device is another thing."
msgstr ""
"Een **symbool** is hier de PLC-tagnaam van een kanaal, zoals `Door_closed`, "
"zoals in de PLC-tagtabel; het tekensymbool van een apparaat is iets anders."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ New project…** (`Ctrl+N`) and pick an empty folder named "
"`plc-station.ew`. EW shows the new project's one empty page, folio 1."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Nieuw project…** (`Ctrl+N`) en wijs een lege map aan met de "
"naam `plc-station.ew`. EW toont de ene lege pagina van het nieuwe project, "
"folio 1."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:26
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** (`Shift+I`), or **Device** on "
"the Draw tab, type `position switch`, choose **Position switch with one "
"normally open contact** (EW-GEN-POS-1) and click near the top of the page, "
"about a quarter from the left. Then choose **Draw ▸ Select** to put the tool "
"away. The switch -1B1 is on the page, with its contact 13 at the top and 14 "
"at the bottom."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…** (`Shift+I`), of "
"**Apparaat** op het tabblad Tekenen, typ `position switch`, kies **Position "
"switch with one normally open contact** (EW-GEN-POS-1) en klik bovenaan op "
"de pagina, ongeveer een kwart van links. Kies daarna **Tekenen ▸ "
"Selecteren** om het gereedschap weg te leggen. De schakelaar -1B1 staat op "
"de pagina, met zijn contact 13 boven en 14 onder."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:30
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** again, choose **Pilot lamp** "
"(EW-GEN-LMP-1) and click to the right of the switch, at the same height. "
"Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The lamp -1E1 is on the page, with X1 at the top "
"and X2 at the bottom."
msgstr ""
"Kies opnieuw **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…**, kies **Pilot "
"lamp** (EW-GEN-LMP-1) en klik rechts van de schakelaar, op dezelfde hoogte. "
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De lamp -1E1 staat op de pagina, met X1 boven "
"en X2 onder."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:33
msgid ""
"Click the **PLC** tab of the command bar and choose **New station** (**PLC ▸ "
"New station…** in the app menu). A tab **New station** opens beside the "
"page's tab, and the window keeps working around it. It shows what the "
"station is (**PLC (CPU)** is chosen), the PLC it belongs to (**New PLC**), "
"the rack with a place for the head and a free slot 1, and at the right the "
"**Catalogue**, which lists the CPUs. Nothing is written before Create."
msgstr ""
"Klik op het tabblad **PLC** van de opdrachtbalk en kies **Nieuw station** "
"(**PLC ▸ Nieuw station…** in het programmamenu). Naast het tabblad van de "
"pagina opent een tabblad **Nieuw station**, en het venster blijft eromheen "
"werken. Het toont wat het station is (**PLC (CPU)** is gekozen), de PLC "
"waartoe het hoort (**Nieuwe PLC**), het rek met een plaats voor de kop en "
"een vrij slot 1, en rechts de **Catalogus**, die de CPU's opsomt. Er wordt "
"niets geschreven vóór Maken."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:37
msgid ""
"Double-click **PLC CPU without on-board I/O** (EW-GEN-CPU-1) in the "
"catalogue. The CPU is the station's head, and the catalogue lists the "
"modules that fit the station."
msgstr ""
"Dubbelklik in de catalogus op **PLC CPU without on-board I/O** "
"(EW-GEN-CPU-1). De CPU is de kop van het station, en de catalogus toont de "
"modules die bij het station passen."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:39
msgid ""
"Double-click **PLC digital input card, 8 channels** (EW-GEN-DI-1) in the "
"catalogue. Slot 1 holds the input module, giving 8 DI; slot 2 is free."
msgstr ""
"Dubbelklik in de catalogus op **PLC digital input card, 8 channels** "
"(EW-GEN-DI-1). Slot 1 bevat de ingangsmodule, die 8 DI geeft; slot 2 is vrij."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:41
msgid ""
"Double-click **PLC digital output card, 8 channels** (EW-GEN-DO-1). The rack "
"is filled, drawn as cards and listed as a table under them:"
msgstr ""
"Dubbelklik op **PLC digital output card, 8 channels** (EW-GEN-DO-1). Het rek "
"is gevuld, getekend als kaarten en eronder opgesomd als tabel:"

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:44
msgid ""
"![The New station tab: the CPU as the head, the input module in slot 1, the "
"output module in slot 2, the catalogue at the "
"right](images/plc-new-station.png)"
msgstr ""
"![Het tabblad Nieuw station: de CPU als kop, de ingangsmodule in slot 1, de "
"uitgangsmodule in slot 2, de catalogus rechts](images/plc-new-station.png)"

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:46
msgid ""
"Choose **Create**. The tab is the station's now: the input module has the "
"addresses I0.0–I0.7, the output module Q0.0–Q0.7, each card says **not "
"drawn**, and **To do** under the table says that they are drawn on no page "
"yet. One Undo takes the whole station back."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Maken**. Het tabblad is nu van het station: de ingangsmodule heeft de "
"adressen I0.0–I0.7, de uitgangsmodule Q0.0–Q0.7, elke kaart zegt **niet "
"getekend**, en **Te doen** onder de tabel zegt dat ze op geen pagina "
"getekend zijn. Eén keer Ongedaan maken neemt het hele station terug."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:50
msgid ""
"Choose **All on one page…** in the To do list and pick page **1**. Page 1 "
"shows, and the CPU -1A1 and its modules -1A1-A1 and -1A1-A2 stand on it as "
"boxes. Each module is a low, wide box with its channels' pins in one row: "
"the input module at the foot of the page with its pins on its upper edge, "
"the output module at the head of the page with its pins on its lower edge, "
"the supply pins on their left sides. Each module's pins are numbered from 1: "
"pin 1 is channel 0. The station's tab stays beside the page's: click it to "
"see the rack again, where each module now names its page."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Alles op één pagina…** in de lijst Te doen en kies pagina **1**. "
"Pagina 1 verschijnt, en de CPU -1A1 en zijn modules -1A1-A1 en -1A1-A2 staan "
"erop als kaders. Elke module is een laag, breed kader met de pennen van zijn "
"kanalen in één rij: de ingangsmodule onderaan de pagina met zijn pennen aan "
"de bovenrand, de uitgangsmodule bovenaan de pagina met zijn pennen aan de "
"onderrand, de voedingspennen aan hun linkerzijde. De pennen van elke module "
"zijn genummerd vanaf 1: pen 1 is kanaal 0. Het tabblad van het station "
"blijft naast dat van de pagina: klik erop om het rek weer te zien, waar elke "
"module nu zijn pagina noemt."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:56
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`). Click contact 14 of -1B1, then pin 1 at the "
"top of -1A1-A1; click X2 of \\-1E1, then pin 1 at the bottom of -1A1-A2. "
"Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The switch is wired to the first input and the "
"lamp to the first output. Each module's pins print their addresses, I0.0 at "
"pin 1 of -1A1-A1, and the switch becomes the target of channel I0.0."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`). Klik op contact 14 van -1B1 en dan op pen 1 "
"bovenaan -1A1-A1; klik op X2 van \\-1E1 en dan op pen 1 onderaan -1A1-A2. "
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De schakelaar zit op de eerste ingang en de "
"lamp op de eerste uitgang. De pennen van elke module tonen hun adressen, "
"I0.0 bij pen 1 van -1A1-A1, en de schakelaar wordt het doel van kanaal I0.0."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:60
msgid ""
"Choose **PLC grid** on the PLC tab (**PLC ▸ PLC grid**). The PLC grid opens "
"under the page: one row per channel, I0.0 to I0.7 of the module -1A1-A1, "
"then Q0.0 to Q0.7 of \\-1A1-A2. I0.0 is channel 0 of the input module, the "
"pin you wired the switch to."
msgstr ""
"Kies **PLC-tabel** op het tabblad PLC (**PLC ▸ PLC-tabel**). De PLC-tabel "
"opent onder de pagina: één rij per kanaal, I0.0 tot en met I0.7 van de "
"module -1A1-A1, daarna Q0.0 tot en met Q0.7 van -1A1-A2. I0.0 is kanaal 0 "
"van de ingangsmodule, de pen waarop u de schakelaar hebt aangesloten."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:63
msgid ""
"Click the **Symbol** cell of I0.0, type `Door_closed` and press `Enter`; do "
"the same for Q0.0 with `Lamp_door`. The toast says \"Set symbol on 1 "
"channel.\" The symbols are the tag names the PLC program will use."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de cel **Symbool** van I0.0, typ `Door_closed` en druk op `Enter`; "
"doe hetzelfde voor Q0.0 met `Lamp_door`. Een notificatie bevestigt het "
"symbool van het kanaal. De symbolen zijn de tagnamen die het PLC-programma "
"gebruikt."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:65
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Report block…** (**Report** on the Page or the Output "
"tab). Choose the report **PLC overview**, the first folio `2` and the title "
"`PLC overview`, and choose **Create**. The toast says \"Added the report "
"block PLC overview from folio 2\". Click page 2 in the Pages navigator: it "
"lists every channel with its module, part, address and symbol, `Door_closed` "
"at I0.0 and `Lamp_door` at Q0.0. EW keeps it up to date; you never draw it."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Uitvoer ▸ Rapportblok…** (**Rapport** op het tabblad Pagina of "
"Uitvoer). Kies het rapport **PLC-overzicht**, het eerste folio `2` en de "
"titel `PLC overview`, en kies **Maken**. De notificatie zegt dat het "
"rapportblok vanaf folio 2 is toegevoegd. Klik op pagina 2 in de navigator "
"Pagina's: die toont elk kanaal met zijn module, artikel, adres en symbool, "
"`Door_closed` op I0.0 en `Lamp_door` op Q0.0. EW houdt hem bij; u tekent hem "
"nooit."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:70
msgid ""
"Choose **Export to PLC** on the PLC tab (**PLC ▸ Export to PLC software…**). "
"Keep the format **AutomationML AR APC 1.4.0**, choose **Export** and save "
"the file as `plc-station.aml`. The toast says \"Exported plc-station.aml: 1 "
"station, 2 modules, 16 channels, 2 symbols.\" The file carries the station, "
"its modules in their slots, the addresses and the symbols to TIA Portal; the "
"format **TIA Portal PLC tags (XLSX)** gives the tag table alone, and "
"**PLCopen XML** is for CODESYS."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Export naar PLC** op het tabblad PLC (**PLC ▸ Naar PLC-software "
"exporteren…**). Houd het formaat **AutomationML AR APC 1.4.0**, kies "
"**Exporteren** en sla het bestand op als `plc-station.aml`. De notificatie "
"meldt 1 station, 2 modules, 16 kanalen en 2 symbolen. Het bestand brengt het "
"station, zijn modules in hun slots, de adressen en de symbolen naar TIA "
"Portal; het formaat **TIA Portal PLC tags (XLSX)** geeft alleen de tagtabel, "
"en **PLCopen XML** is voor CODESYS."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:80
msgid ""
"**New station** builds a station the way TIA Portal does: its head part "
"first, then its modules slot by slot from the catalogue, and **Create** "
"makes them all in one step. **Draw each card on a page of its own** draws "
"each module on a new page, **All on one page…** draws them all on the page "
"you pick, bound to their pins."
msgstr ""
"**Nieuw station** bouwt een station zoals TIA Portal dat doet: eerst zijn "
"kopartikel, dan zijn modules slot voor slot uit de catalogus, en **Maken** "
"maakt ze allemaal in één stap. **Elke kaart op een eigen pagina tekenen** "
"tekent elke module op een nieuwe pagina, **Alles op één pagina…** tekent ze "
"allemaal op de pagina die je kiest, gekoppeld aan hun pennen."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:83
msgid ""
"The slot order gives the addresses, and the PLC grid shows them channel by "
"channel, where you type the symbols."
msgstr ""
"De volgorde van de slots geeft de adressen, en de PLC-tabel toont ze kanaal "
"voor kanaal; daar typt u de symbolen."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:84
msgid ""
"The PLC overview is a report page, made by a report block and kept up to "
"date by EW."
msgstr ""
"Het PLC-overzicht is een rapportpagina, gemaakt door een rapportblok en door "
"EW bijgehouden."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:85
msgid ""
"**Export to PLC** hands the hardware and the symbols to the PLC software."
msgstr ""
"**Export naar PLC** geeft de hardware en de symbolen door aan de "
"PLC-software."

#: src/tutorials/plc-station.md:87
msgid ""
"To look up one of these jobs later, see [Work in the PLC "
"workspace](../how-to/plc-workspace.md): making and changing a station, "
"addresses, the PLC grid and the exchange with PLC software. The next "
"tutorial, [A bus network](bus-network.md), puts a station on PROFINET."
msgstr ""
"Om een van deze taken later op te zoeken, zie [Werken in de "
"PLC-werkruimte](../how-to/plc-workspace.md): een station maken en wijzigen, "
"adressen, de PLC-tabel en de uitwisseling met PLC-software. De volgende "
"zelfstudie, [Een busnetwerk](bus-network.md), zet een station op PROFINET."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you draw a small PROFINET network on a bus-topology page: a "
"CPU, a remote I/O station with an input module and an unmanaged switch, all "
"from the generic starter parts, then a frequency converter of your own that "
"you type into Parts. You give the nodes their names and IP addresses, set "
"the cable and its plugs on one bus line, and check the network. It takes "
"about fifteen minutes."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie tekent u een klein PROFINET-netwerk op een "
"bustopologiepagina: een CPU, een decentraal I/O-station met een "
"ingangsmodule en een unmanaged switch, allemaal uit de generieke "
"startartikelen, en daarna een eigen frequentieregelaar die u in Artikelen "
"invoert. U geeft de knooppunten hun naam en IP-adres, legt de kabel en zijn "
"stekkers vast op één buslijn en controleert het netwerk. Het duurt ongeveer "
"een kwartier."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:8
msgid ""
"A bus-topology page shows the network the way TIA Portal's network view "
"does: each device as a box per bus interface (PROFINET X1, PROFIBUS DP) with "
"its sockets, and the bus lines between the sockets. The power and the I/O "
"wiring of the same devices stay on the schematic pages."
msgstr ""
"Een bustopologiepagina toont het netwerk zoals de netwerkweergave van TIA "
"Portal: elk apparaat als een kader per businterface (PROFINET X1, PROFIBUS "
"DP) met zijn aansluitingen, en de buslijnen tussen de aansluitingen. De "
"voeding en de I/O-bedrading van dezelfde apparaten blijven op de "
"schemapagina's."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:19
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ New project…** (`Ctrl+N`) and pick an empty folder named "
"`bus-network.ew`. EW shows the new project's one empty page, folio 1."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Nieuw project…** (`Ctrl+N`) en wijs een lege map aan met de "
"naam `bus-network.ew`. EW toont de ene lege pagina van het nieuwe project, "
"folio 1."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:21
msgid ""
"Click the **PLC** tab and choose **New bus page**. The **Page ▸ New page…** "
"form opens with the type **Bus topology** chosen: type the folio `2` and the "
"title `Network`, and choose **Create**. The empty page 2 Network shows. On "
"this page the Wire tool draws bus lines."
msgstr ""
"Klik op het tabblad **PLC** en kies **Nieuwe buspagina**. Het formulier "
"**Pagina ▸ Nieuwe pagina…** opent met het type Bustopologie gekozen: typ het "
"folio `2` en de titel `Network`, en kies **Maken**. De lege pagina 2 Network "
"verschijnt. Op deze pagina tekent het gereedschap Draad buslijnen."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** (`Shift+I`), type `CPU` and "
"choose **PLC CPU with PROFINET and PROFIBUS DP interfaces** (EW-GEN-CPU-2). "
"Click near the top left of the page, then a little lower and to the right, "
"for the two corners of its box. The toast says \"Placed as 2 boxes, one for "
"each bus interface\": the CPU -2A1 stands as its DP box and its X1 box, each "
"with its socket. Its rack opens in a tab beside the page's, because a CPU is "
"a station; press `Esc` to go back to the page (the tab stays, for later)."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…** (`Shift+I`), typ `CPU` "
"en kies **PLC CPU with PROFINET and PROFIBUS DP interfaces** (EW-GEN-CPU-2). "
"Klik linksboven op de pagina en daarna iets lager en naar rechts, voor de "
"twee hoeken van zijn kader. De notificatie zegt \"Geplaatst als 2 kaders, "
"één per businterface\": de CPU -2A1 staat er als zijn DP-kader en zijn "
"X1-kader, elk met zijn aansluiting. Zijn rek opent in een tabblad naast dat "
"van de pagina, omdat een CPU een station is; druk op `Esc` om terug te gaan "
"naar de pagina (het tabblad blijft, voor later)."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:30
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** again and choose **Remote I/O "
"head with PROFINET and PROFIBUS DP interfaces** (EW-GEN-RIO-1). Click two "
"corners low on the page, under the CPU. The remote I/O station -2A2 stands "
"as its DP and X1 boxes, and its rack opens in a tab."
msgstr ""
"Kies opnieuw **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…** en kies **Remote "
"I/O head with PROFINET and PROFIBUS DP interfaces** (EW-GEN-RIO-1). Klik "
"twee hoeken laag op de pagina, onder de CPU. Het decentrale I/O-station -2A2 "
"staat er als zijn DP- en X1-kader, en zijn rek opent in een tabblad."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:33
msgid ""
"In the rack's **Catalogue**, double-click **PLC digital input card, 8 "
"channels** (EW-GEN-DI-1), then press `Esc` to go back to the page. The "
"station carries an input module, -2A2-A1, drawn on no page yet."
msgstr ""
"Dubbelklik in de **Catalogus** van het rek op **PLC digital input card, 8 "
"channels** (EW-GEN-DI-1) en druk dan op `Esc` om terug te gaan naar de "
"pagina. Het station draagt een ingangsmodule, -2A2-A1, die nog op geen "
"pagina getekend is."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:36
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…**, choose **Industrial Ethernet "
"switch, unmanaged, 5 ports** (EW-GEN-ESW-5) and click two corners between "
"the CPU and the remote I/O station. Press `Esc`, then choose **Draw ▸ "
"Select**. The switch -2K1 stands as one box with its sockets P1 to P3 at the "
"top and P4 and P5 at the bottom."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…**, kies **Industrial "
"Ethernet switch, unmanaged, 5 ports** (EW-GEN-ESW-5) en klik twee hoeken "
"tussen de CPU en het decentrale I/O-station. Druk op `Esc` en kies dan "
"**Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De switch -2K1 staat er als één kader met zijn "
"aansluitingen P1 tot en met P3 boven en P4 en P5 onder."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:40
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`). Click the socket P1 of the CPU's X1 box, then "
"P1 of the switch; click P4 of the switch, then the socket P1 of the remote "
"I/O station's X1 box. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The two bus lines join the "
"CPU, the switch and the remote I/O station into one PROFINET network."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`). Klik op aansluiting P1 van het X1-kader van "
"de CPU en dan op P1 van de switch; klik op P4 van de switch en dan op "
"aansluiting P1 van het X1-kader van het decentrale I/O-station. Kies "
"**Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De twee buslijnen maken van de CPU, de switch en "
"het decentrale I/O-station één PROFINET-netwerk."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:43
msgid ""
"Choose **View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ PLC** (the PLC button on the rail at "
"the left). The PLC panel lists the PLC PLC1 with the CPU -2A1 and the remote "
"I/O station -2A2, a head station, with its input module: both are stations "
"of the same PLC."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Beeld ▸ Panelen ▸ Navigators ▸ PLC** (de knop PLC in de rail links). "
"Het paneel PLC toont de PLC PLC1 met de CPU -2A1 en het decentrale "
"I/O-station -2A2, een kopstation, met zijn ingangsmodule: beide zijn "
"stations van dezelfde PLC."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:46
msgid ""
"Choose **Bus panel** on the PLC tab (**PLC ▸ Bus panel**) and switch it to "
"its table with the table button left of its filter. Type `plc1 192.168.0.1` "
"into the **Address** cell of the row -2A1 X1, and `rio1 192.168.0.2` into "
"that of -2A2 X1. Each X1 box on the page prints its node: \"Node plc1 "
"192.168.0.1\". The switch passes the network through and takes no address."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Buspaneel** op het tabblad PLC (**PLC ▸ Buspaneel**) en zet het op "
"zijn tabel met de tabelknop links van het filter. Typ `plc1 192.168.0.1` in "
"de cel **Adres** van de rij -2A1 X1, en `rio1 192.168.0.2` in die van -2A2 "
"X1. Elk X1-kader op de pagina drukt zijn knooppunt af. De switch geeft het "
"netwerk door en krijgt geen adres."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:51
msgid ""
"Click the bus line between the CPU and the switch and choose **Cable** on "
"the Draw tab. The Cable form opens with **EW-GEN-CBL-3 · Industrial Ethernet "
"cable, 4 cores, shielded (PROFINET)**, the cables made for PROFINET first. "
"Type `5` as the **Length (m)**, choose **EW-GEN-PLG-3 · RJ45 plug for "
"Industrial Ethernet** as the plug at each end, and choose **Apply**. The "
"**Tag** field proposes `-2W1`, the page's next cable tag. The toast says "
"\"Cable -2W1 carries the bus line.\" The line carries the cable's mark: its "
"tag with its type under it, which you can move as any tag; the cable and its "
"two plugs go into the parts list. The lists of cables and plugs end in **New "
"bus cable…** and **New plug…**: a new part of your own for the bus opens in "
"Parts (a cable's cores come as the bus has them) and chooses it at once."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de buslijn tussen de CPU en de switch en kies **Kabel** op het "
"tabblad Tekenen. Het kabelformulier opent met **EW-GEN-CBL-3 · Industrial "
"Ethernet cable, 4 cores, shielded (PROFINET)**, de kabels voor PROFINET "
"eerst. Typ `5` als **Lengte (m)**, kies **EW-GEN-PLG-3 · RJ45 plug for "
"Industrial Ethernet** als stekker aan beide einden, en kies **Toepassen**. "
"Het veld **Kenmerk** stelt `-2W1` voor, het volgende kabelkenmerk van de "
"pagina. De notificatie zegt dat kabel -2W1 de buslijn draagt. De lijn draagt "
"de markering van de kabel: zijn kenmerk met zijn type eronder, die u als elk "
"kenmerk kunt verschuiven; de kabel en zijn twee stekkers komen in de "
"stuklijst. De lijsten met kabels en stekkers eindigen met **Nieuwe "
"buskabel…** en **Nieuwe stekker…**: een nieuw eigen artikel voor de bus "
"opent in Artikelen (de aders van een kabel komen zoals de bus ze heeft) en "
"wordt meteen gekozen."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:59
msgid ""
"Choose **Edit ▸ New part…** and, under **What is it?**, **Device drawn as a "
"box**. Type the **Maker** `Example`, the **Part number** `FC-PN-2` and the "
"**Description** `Frequency converter with PROFINET, 2 ports`. Under "
"**Network ports** choose **Add interface**: it proposes the interface `X1` "
"on **PROFINET** with the role **Device**; type **Ports** `2`. Choose "
"**Save**. The toast says \"Part FC-PN-2 is in the project and in My "
"parts.\": your next project has it too. Choose **Back**."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bewerken ▸ Nieuw artikel…** en, onder **Wat is het?**, **Apparaat "
"getekend als blok**. Typ als **Fabrikant** `Example`, als **Artikelnummer** "
"`FC-PN-2` en als **Beschrijving** `Frequency converter with PROFINET, 2 "
"ports`. Kies onder **Netwerkpoorten** **Interface toevoegen**: die stelt de "
"interface `X1` op PROFINET voor met de rol Device; typ bij de poorten `2`. "
"Kies **Opslaan**. De notificatie zegt \"Artikel FC-PN-2 staat in het project "
"en in Mijn artikelen.\": uw volgende project heeft het ook. Kies **Terug**."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:64
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…**, type `FC-PN-2` and click two "
"corners to the right of the remote I/O station. Press `Esc`, then choose "
"**Draw ▸ Select**. The converter -2K2 stands as its X1 box with its sockets "
"P1 and P2."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…**, typ `FC-PN-2` en klik "
"twee hoeken rechts van het decentrale I/O-station. Druk op `Esc` en kies dan "
"**Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De regelaar -2K2 staat er als zijn X1-kader met "
"zijn aansluitingen P1 en P2."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:67
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`), click P5 of the switch, then P1 of -2K2, and "
"choose **Draw ▸ Select**. In the Bus panel's table, type `drive1 "
"192.168.0.3` into the **Address** cell of -2K2 X1. The converter is a node "
"of the same network:"
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`), klik op P5 van de switch en dan op P1 van "
"-2K2, en kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. Typ in de tabel van het buspaneel "
"`drive1 192.168.0.3` in de cel **Adres** van -2K2 X1. De regelaar is een "
"knooppunt van hetzelfde netwerk:"

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:71
msgid ""
"![The bus page: the CPU, the switch, the remote I/O station and the "
"converter, joined by their bus lines](images/bus-network.png)"
msgstr ""
"![De buspagina: de CPU, de switch, het decentrale I/O-station en de "
"regelaar, verbonden door hun buslijnen](images/bus-network.png)"

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:73
msgid ""
"Choose **Check ▸ Check project** (`F7`). The toast says \"Check: 0 errors, 0 "
"warnings, 5 infos.\" The infos say that the DP addresses are still TBD (this "
"network uses PROFINET only) and that no report lists the stations and the "
"cable yet."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Controle ▸ Project controleren** (`F7`). De notificatie meldt 0 "
"fouten, 0 waarschuwingen en 5 meldingen. De meldingen zeggen dat de "
"DP-adressen nog TBD zijn (dit netwerk gebruikt alleen PROFINET) en dat nog "
"geen rapport de stations en de kabel opsomt."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:81
msgid ""
"A bus-topology page holds the network: a device stands as one box per bus "
"interface, and **Draw ▸ Wire** draws the bus lines between their sockets. A "
"CPU or a remote I/O head is a station, with its rack."
msgstr ""
"Een bustopologiepagina houdt het netwerk: een apparaat staat er als één "
"kader per businterface, en **Tekenen ▸ Draad** tekent de buslijnen tussen "
"hun aansluitingen. Een CPU of een decentrale I/O-kop is een station, met "
"zijn rek."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:83
msgid "The Bus panel's table takes each node's address; the box prints it."
msgstr ""
"De tabel van het buspaneel neemt het adres van elk knooppunt; het kader "
"drukt het af."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:84
msgid "**Cable** on a bus line sets its cable, length and plugs in one form."
msgstr ""
"**Kabel** op een buslijn legt in één formulier de kabel, de lengte en de "
"stekkers vast."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:85
msgid ""
"A device of your own with its ports is typed from its data sheet in Parts; "
"[Make your own parts](../how-to/own-parts.md) has the whole of it."
msgstr ""
"Een eigen apparaat met zijn poorten voert u vanuit zijn datablad in "
"Artikelen in; [Eigen artikelen maken](../how-to/own-parts.md) beschrijft het "
"helemaal."

#: src/tutorials/bus-network.md:88
msgid ""
"To look up these jobs later, see [Bus ports and "
"nets](../how-to/plc-workspace.md#bus-ports-and-nets) in the PLC guide. The "
"tutorial before this one, [A PLC with its modules](plc-station.md), fills a "
"station's rack and wires its channels."
msgstr ""
"Om deze taken later op te zoeken, zie [Buspoorten en "
"netten](../how-to/plc-workspace.md#bus-ports-and-nets) in de "
"PLC-handleiding. De zelfstudie hiervoor, [Een PLC met zijn "
"modules](plc-station.md), vult het rek van een station en sluit zijn kanalen "
"aan."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you draw a pneumatic circuit on a fluid page: an air supply "
"with its medium, a service unit, a 5/2 solenoid valve and a double-acting "
"cylinder with a one-way flow control in each line, joined by tubes. You draw "
"the valve's solenoid on an electrical page and wire it to a PLC output, then "
"do the same job with a valve terminal, and read the tube list. It takes "
"about fifteen minutes."
msgstr ""
"In deze zelfstudie tekent u een pneumatisch schema op een fluïdumpagina: een "
"persluchtvoeding met haar medium, een verzorgingseenheid, een "
"5/2-magneetventiel en een dubbelwerkende cilinder met een "
"smoorterugslagventiel in elke leiding, verbonden door slangen. U tekent de "
"magneetspoel van het ventiel op een elektrische pagina en sluit hem aan op "
"een PLC-uitgang, doet daarna hetzelfde met een ventieleiland en leest de "
"slangenlijst. Het duurt ongeveer een kwartier."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:8
msgid ""
"On a fluid page the symbols are the ISO 1219 symbols, the Wire tool draws "
"tubes, and a valve's solenoid is not drawn on the fluid page: it belongs to "
"the valve and stands on an electrical page, where it is wired."
msgstr ""
"Op een fluïdumpagina zijn de symbolen die van ISO 1219, tekent het "
"gereedschap Draad slangen, en wordt de magneetspoel van een ventiel niet op "
"de fluïdumpagina getekend: hij hoort bij het ventiel en staat op een "
"elektrische pagina, waar hij wordt aangesloten."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:18
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ New project…** (`Ctrl+N`) and pick an empty folder named "
"`pneumatic-circuit.ew`. EW shows the new project's one empty page, folio 1, "
"an electrical page."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Bestand ▸ Nieuw project…** (`Ctrl+N`) en wijs een lege map aan met de "
"naam `pneumatic-circuit.ew`. EW toont de ene lege pagina van het nieuwe "
"project, folio 1, een elektrische pagina."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:20
msgid ""
"Click the **Fluid** tab and choose **Fluid page**. The **Page ▸ New page…** "
"form opens with the type **Fluid** chosen: type the folio `2` and the title "
"`Pneumatics`, and choose **Create**. The empty page 2 Pneumatics shows."
msgstr ""
"Klik op het tabblad **Fluïdum** en kies **Fluïdumpagina**. Het formulier "
"**Pagina ▸ Nieuwe pagina…** opent met het type Fluïdum gekozen: typ het "
"folio `2` en de titel `Pneumatics`, en kies **Maken**. De lege pagina 2 "
"Pneumatics verschijnt."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:23
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…**. On a fluid page the symbol browser offers "
"the fluid symbols: choose **air-supply** and click near the bottom left of "
"the page. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The compressed-air supply point -2X1 is "
"on the page, with its port 1. (**Air supply** on the Fluid tab places a "
"supply with its part instead, from the device browser.)"
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbool…**. Op een fluïdumpagina biedt de "
"symboolbrowser de fluïdumsymbolen aan: kies **air-supply** en klik "
"linksonder op de pagina. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. Het "
"persluchtaansluitpunt -2X1 staat op de pagina, met zijn poort 1. "
"(**Persluchtvoeding** op het tabblad Fluïdum plaatst in plaats daarvan een "
"voeding met haar artikel, vanuit de apparatenbrowser.)"

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:27
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** again, choose **frl**, the service unit "
"with filter, regulator and lubricator, and click to the right of the supply "
"point. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The service unit -2V1 is on the page, with "
"port 1 in, port 2 out."
msgstr ""
"Kies opnieuw **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbool…**, kies **frl**, de "
"verzorgingseenheid met filter, regelaar en olievernevelaar, en klik rechts "
"van het aansluitpunt. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De verzorgingseenheid "
"-2V1 staat op de pagina, met poort 1 in en poort 2 uit."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:30
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** (`Shift+I`), choose **Solenoid "
"valve 5/2, spring return** (EW-GEN-VLV-1) and click in the middle of the "
"page, to the right. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The valve -2Q1 is on the page: "
"its working ports 4 and 2 at the top, the supply port 1 and the exhausts 3 "
"and 5 at the bottom, its solenoid at the left and its spring at the right."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…** (`Shift+I`), kies "
"**Solenoid valve 5/2, spring return** (EW-GEN-VLV-1) en klik in het midden "
"van de pagina, naar rechts. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. Het ventiel -2Q1 "
"staat op de pagina: zijn werkpoorten 4 en 2 boven, de voedingspoort 1 en de "
"ontluchtingen 3 en 5 onder, zijn magneetspoel links en zijn veer rechts."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:34
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…**, choose **Cylinder "
"double-acting, bore 32 mm, stroke 100 mm** (EW-GEN-CYL-1) and click near the "
"top of the page, above the valve. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The cylinder "
"-2M1 is on the page, with its ports A and B."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…**, kies **Cylinder "
"double-acting, bore 32 mm, stroke 100 mm** (EW-GEN-CYL-1) en klik bovenaan "
"op de pagina, boven het ventiel. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De cilinder "
"-2M1 staat op de pagina, met zijn poorten A en B."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:37
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** and choose **One-way flow "
"control valve** (EW-GEN-FCV-1). Before you click, choose **Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ "
"Rotate clockwise** (`R`) to stand it upright. Hold `Alt` and click twice, "
"side by side, between the cylinder and the valve and a little to the left. "
"Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The flow controls -2R1 and -2R2 stand upright, "
"port 1 at the top. `Alt` places a symbol without joining it with a tube to a "
"port it faces."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…** en kies **One-way flow "
"control valve** (EW-GEN-FCV-1). Kies voordat u klikt **Tekenen ▸ Schikken ▸ "
"Rechtsom draaien** (`R`) om hem rechtop te zetten. Houd `Alt` ingedrukt en "
"klik twee keer, naast elkaar, tussen de cilinder en het ventiel en iets naar "
"links. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De smoorterugslagventielen -2R1 en "
"-2R2 staan rechtop, poort 1 boven. Met `Alt` plaatst u een symbool zonder "
"het met een slang te verbinden aan een poort waar het tegenover staat."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:42
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`) and draw the tubes, each from one port to the "
"other: A of -2M1 to 1 of \\-2R1, 2 of -2R1 to 4 of -2Q1; B of -2M1 to 1 of "
"-2R2, 2 of -2R2 to 2 of -2Q1; 1 of -2X1 to 1 of -2V1, and 2 of \\-2V1 to 1 "
"of -2Q1. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The circuit is joined: supply, service "
"unit, valve, and the two lines to the cylinder through the flow controls."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`) en teken de slangen, elk van de ene poort "
"naar de andere: A van -2M1 naar 1 van -2R1, 2 van -2R1 naar 4 van -2Q1; B "
"van -2M1 naar 1 van -2R2, 2 van -2R2 naar 2 van -2Q1; 1 van -2X1 naar 1 van "
"-2V1, en 2 van -2V1 naar 1 van -2Q1. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. Het "
"schema is verbonden: voeding, verzorgingseenheid, ventiel, en de twee "
"leidingen naar de cilinder door de smoorterugslagventielen."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:46
msgid ""
"Choose **New medium** on the Fluid tab (**Fluid ▸ New medium…**). Under "
"**Start from** choose **Compressed air**, which fills the name and 6 bar, "
"and choose **Create**. The toast says \"Added the medium Compressed air.\""
msgstr ""
"Kies **Nieuw medium** op het tabblad Fluïdum (**Fluïdum ▸ Nieuw medium…**). "
"Kies onder **Beginnen met** **Perslucht**, dat de naam en 6 bar invult, en "
"kies **Maken**. Een notificatie zegt dat het medium is toegevoegd."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:49
msgid ""
"Click the supply point -2X1. In Properties, open the **Medium** row and "
"choose **Compressed air** (or right-click \\-2X1 and choose **Medium:** "
"Compressed air, 6 bar). Properties say what -2X1 is, \"Compressed-air supply "
"point · pneumatic\", and its Medium row reads \"Compressed air, 6 bar\", its "
"own medium. Every tube it feeds through the valve now carries compressed air."
msgstr ""
"Klik op het aansluitpunt -2X1. Open in Eigenschappen de rij **Medium** en "
"kies **Perslucht** (of klik met rechts op -2X1 en kies **Medium:** "
"Perslucht, 6 bar). Eigenschappen zeggen wat -2X1 is, een "
"persluchtaansluitpunt, pneumatisch, en zijn rij Medium toont \"Perslucht, 6 "
"bar\", zijn eigen medium. Elke slang die het via het ventiel voedt, voert nu "
"perslucht."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:54
msgid ""
"Click the valve -2Q1. In Properties the row **Solenoid 14** says \"not "
"drawn: draw it…\": click it (or right-click the valve and choose **Draw "
"solenoid 14 on an electrical page…**). The window asks on which electrical "
"page: page 1 is chosen, so choose **Draw**. Page 1 shows with the coil on "
"the pointer, and the toast says \"Click where solenoid 14 of -2Q1 goes on "
"page 1.\""
msgstr ""
"Klik op het ventiel -2Q1. In Eigenschappen zegt de rij **Magneetspoel 14** "
"\"niet getekend: tekenen…\": klik erop (of klik met rechts op het ventiel en "
"kies **Magneetspoel 14 op een elektrische pagina tekenen…**). Het venster "
"vraagt op welke elektrische pagina: pagina 1 is gekozen, dus kies "
"**Tekenen**. Pagina 1 verschijnt met de spoel aan de aanwijzer, en een "
"notificatie zegt waar u moet klikken."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:58
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Mirror top to bottom** (`Y`), so that the coil's "
"A1 is at the bottom, and click near the top of page 1, a quarter from the "
"left. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The coil carries the valve's tag, -2Q1: it "
"is the valve's own solenoid, not a device of its own."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Schikken ▸ Boven-onder spiegelen** (`Y`), zodat A1 van de "
"spoel onder staat, en klik bovenaan op pagina 1, een kwart van links. Kies "
"**Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De spoel draagt het kenmerk van het ventiel, -2Q1: "
"het is de eigen magneetspoel van het ventiel, geen apparaat op zich."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:61
msgid ""
"Click the **PLC** tab and choose **New station** (**PLC ▸ New station…**). "
"In the tab that opens, double-click **PLC CPU without on-board I/O** "
"(EW-GEN-CPU-1) in the catalogue, then **PLC digital output card, 8 "
"channels** (EW-GEN-DO-1); choose **Create**, then **Draw on a page…** in the "
"To do list and pick page **1**. Page 1 shows, with the CPU -1A1 and its "
"output module -1A1-A1 under the coil. [A PLC with its "
"modules](plc-station.md) shows the station's tab in full."
msgstr ""
"Klik op het tabblad **PLC** en kies **Nieuw station** (**PLC ▸ Nieuw "
"station…**). Dubbelklik in het tabblad dat opent in de catalogus op **PLC "
"CPU without on-board I/O** (EW-GEN-CPU-1), dan op **PLC digital output card, "
"8 channels** (EW-GEN-DO-1); kies **Maken**, dan **Op een pagina tekenen…** "
"in de lijst Te doen en kies pagina **1**. Pagina 1 verschijnt, met de CPU "
"-1A1 en zijn uitgangsmodule -1A1-A1 onder de spoel. [Een PLC met zijn "
"modules](plc-station.md) toont het tabblad van het station helemaal."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:66
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`), click A1 of the coil, then pin 1 at the top of "
"-1A1-A1, the output Q0.0. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The solenoid is wired to "
"the PLC output."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`), klik op A1 van de spoel en dan op pen 1 "
"bovenaan -1A1-A1, de uitgang Q0.0. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De "
"magneetspoel zit op de PLC-uitgang."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:69
msgid ""
"Click page 2 in the Pages navigator and click the valve -2Q1. Its row "
"**Solenoid 14** now names the place where the coil is drawn, 1-A4."
msgstr ""
"Klik op pagina 2 in de navigator Pagina's en klik op het ventiel -2Q1. Zijn "
"rij **Magneetspoel 14** noemt nu de plaats waar de spoel getekend is, 1-A4."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:71
msgid ""
"Choose **Valve terminal** on the Fluid tab (**Fluid ▸ Valve terminal…**). "
"The device browser opens at the valve terminals: choose **Valve terminal "
"with PROFIBUS DP bus node, 4 valve positions, 8 outputs** (EW-GEN-VTM-1) and "
"click once at the left of the page, below the middle. Its rack opens in a "
"tab beside the page's; press `Esc` to go back to the page, then choose "
"**Draw ▸ Select**. The valve terminal -2K1 is drawn as its positions: the "
"supply end at the left with the terminal's supply port 1 and exhaust ports 3 "
"and 5 on its bottom edge, then four empty cells numbered 1 to 4 on the "
"sub-base, where the supply and exhaust galleries run through every position. "
"The toast says \"Placed -2K1 at 2-…: 4 positions, all empty.\"; one click "
"places one terminal."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Ventieleiland** op het tabblad Fluïdum (**Fluïdum ▸ "
"Ventieleiland…**). De apparatenbrowser opent bij de ventieleilanden: kies "
"**Valve terminal with PROFIBUS DP bus node, 4 valve positions, 8 outputs** "
"(EW-GEN-VTM-1) en klik één keer links op de pagina, onder het midden. Zijn "
"rek opent in een tabblad naast dat van de pagina; druk op `Esc` om terug te "
"gaan naar de pagina en kies dan **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. Het ventieleiland "
"-2K1 is getekend als zijn posities: de voedingskant links met de "
"voedingspoort 1 en de ontluchtingspoorten 3 en 5 van het eiland op zijn "
"onderrand, dan vier lege cellen, genummerd 1 tot en met 4, op de grondplaat, "
"waar de voedings- en ontluchtingskanalen door elke positie lopen. De "
"notificatie zegt \"-2K1 geplaatst op 2-…: 4 posities, alle leeg.\"; één klik "
"plaatst één ventieleiland."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:79
msgid ""
"Click the empty cell 1. The device browser opens with the valves made for a "
"terminal; type `valve terminal` into its search and choose **Solenoid valve "
"5/2 for a valve terminal, spring return** (EW-GEN-VLV-3). The valve -2Q2 "
"stands in cell 1 with its output, Q1.0: its working ports 4 and 2 on the top "
"edge of the cell, its ports 5, 1 and 3 joined to the galleries below it."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de lege cel 1. De apparatenbrowser opent met de ventielen die voor "
"een ventieleiland gemaakt zijn; typ `valve terminal` in het zoekveld en kies "
"**Solenoid valve 5/2 for a valve terminal, spring return** (EW-GEN-VLV-3). "
"Het ventiel -2Q2 staat in cel 1 met zijn uitgang, Q1.0: zijn werkpoorten 4 "
"en 2 op de bovenrand van de cel, zijn poorten 5, 1 en 3 verbonden met de "
"kanalen eronder."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:84
msgid ""
"Click the empty cell 2 and choose the same valve. The valve -2Q3 stands in "
"cell 2 with Q1.2. Click the terminal: its row **Valve positions** reads \"1 "
"-2Q2 · 2 -2Q3 · 3 empty · 4 empty\". The check asks for a valve or a "
"blanking plate in the empty cells; a right click on a cell offers both. A "
"valve put on a position another way, in the terminal's rack or its row in "
"the PLC list, stands in its cell the same way."
msgstr ""
"Klik op de lege cel 2 en kies hetzelfde ventiel. Het ventiel -2Q3 staat in "
"cel 2 met Q1.2. Klik op het eiland: zijn rij **Ventielplaatsen** toont \"1 "
"-2Q2 · 2 -2Q3 · 3 leeg · 4 leeg\". De controle vraagt om een ventiel of een "
"blindplaat in de lege cellen; een rechterklik op een cel biedt beide. Een "
"ventiel dat op een andere manier op een positie is gezet, in het rek van het "
"eiland of in zijn rij in de PLC-tabel, staat op dezelfde manier in zijn cel."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:89
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…**, choose **air-supply**, then **Draw ▸ "
"Arrange ▸ Mirror top to bottom** (`Y`), so that its port 1 points up, and "
"click under the left end of the terminal. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The "
"supply point -2X2 stands under the terminal, joined by a tube to its supply "
"port 1: the toast says \"Placed \\-2X2 … and connected 1 to …\", with the "
"terminal's port."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Symbool…**, kies **air-supply**, dan **Tekenen ▸ "
"Schikken ▸ Boven-onder spiegelen** (`Y`), zodat zijn poort 1 omhoog wijst, "
"en klik onder het linkereinde van het eiland. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. "
"Het aansluitpunt -2X2 staat onder het eiland, door een slang verbonden met "
"de voedingspoort 1 ervan: de notificatie zegt \"\\-2X2 geplaatst … en 1 "
"verbonden met …\", met de poort van het eiland."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:93
msgid ""
"Click -2X2. In Properties, open the **Medium** row and choose **Compressed "
"air**. The air reaches the terminal's supply gallery, and through it port 1 "
"of every valve on the terminal."
msgstr ""
"Klik op -2X2. Open in Eigenschappen de rij **Medium** en kies **Perslucht**. "
"De lucht bereikt het voedingskanaal van het eiland, en daardoor poort 1 van "
"elk ventiel op het eiland."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:95
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…**, choose **Cylinder "
"double-acting, bore 32 mm, stroke 100 mm** (EW-GEN-CYL-1) and click above "
"the terminal, to the right of -2Q2. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The cylinder "
"-2M2 is on the page."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Plaatsen ▸ Apparaat uit artikel…**, kies **Cylinder "
"double-acting, bore 32 mm, stroke 100 mm** (EW-GEN-CYL-1) en klik boven het "
"eiland, rechts van -2Q2. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De cilinder -2M2 "
"staat op de pagina."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:98
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`) and draw two tubes: 4 of -2Q2 to A of -2M2, and "
"2 of -2Q2 to B of -2M2. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The tubes leave the "
"valve's working ports upwards. Click -2M2: its rows **Port A** and **Port "
"B** read \"Compressed air, 6 bar\", the air that comes from -2X2 through the "
"terminal and the valve."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Tekenen ▸ Draad** (`W`) en teken twee slangen: 4 van -2Q2 naar A van "
"-2M2, en 2 van -2Q2 naar B van -2M2. Kies **Tekenen ▸ Selecteren**. De "
"slangen verlaten de werkpoorten van het ventiel naar boven. Klik op -2M2: "
"zijn rijen **Aansluiting A** en **Aansluiting B** tonen \"Perslucht, 6 "
"bar\", de lucht die van -2X2 komt door het eiland en het ventiel."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:103
msgid ""
"![The fluid page: the circuit to the cylinder, and the valve terminal with "
"two valves, its supply and a cylinder](images/pneumatic-circuit.png)"
msgstr ""
"![De fluïdumpagina: het schema naar de cilinder, en het ventieleiland met "
"twee ventielen, zijn voeding en een cilinder](images/pneumatic-circuit.png)"

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:105
msgid ""
"Choose **Fluid settings** on the Fluid tab (**Fluid ▸ Fluid settings…**). "
"Type the default tube's outer diameter `6`, inner diameter `4`, choose the "
"material **PU**, and confirm. The toast says \"The default tube is 6 × 4 mm "
"PU (polyurethane) now: every tube without one of its own takes it, the tubes "
"drawn already too.\""
msgstr ""
"Kies **Instellingen fluïdtechniek** op het tabblad Fluïdum (**Fluïdum ▸ "
"Instellingen fluïdtechniek…**). Typ voor de standaardslang de buitendiameter "
"`6` en de binnendiameter `4`, kies het materiaal **PU** en bevestig. Een "
"notificatie zegt dat de standaardslang nu 6 × 4 mm PU is en dat elke slang "
"zonder eigen slang hem neemt, ook de slangen die al getekend zijn."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:109
msgid ""
"Choose **Tube table** on the Fluid tab (**Fluid ▸ Tube table**). The tube "
"list opens under the page, one row per tube with its two ends, its medium "
"(Compressed air), its line kind and its size, 6×4 PU. The length stays empty "
"until you type it."
msgstr ""
"Kies **Slangentabel** op het tabblad Fluïdum (**Fluïdum ▸ Slangentabel**). "
"De slangenlijst opent onder de pagina, één rij per slang met zijn twee "
"einden, zijn medium (Perslucht), zijn leidingsoort en zijn maat, 6×4 PU. De "
"lengte blijft leeg tot u hem typt."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:115
msgid "Two kinds of valve"
msgstr "Twee soorten ventielen"

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:117
msgid ""
"A stand-alone valve, such as -2Q1, has a coil of its own: you draw it on an "
"electrical page and wire it to a PLC output. A valve on a valve terminal "
"with a bus node, such as -2Q2, is driven inside the terminal, which is a PLC "
"station on the bus: its output is given by its position, and nothing is "
"wired per solenoid."
msgstr ""
"Een los ventiel, zoals -2Q1, heeft een eigen spoel: u tekent hem op een "
"elektrische pagina en sluit hem aan op een PLC-uitgang. Een ventiel op een "
"ventieleiland met busknooppunt, zoals -2Q2, wordt in het eiland aangestuurd, "
"dat een PLC-station op de bus is: zijn uitgang volgt uit zijn positie, en "
"per magneetspoel wordt niets bedraad."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:123
msgid ""
"A fluid page draws the circuit with ISO 1219 symbols; the Wire tool draws "
"tubes there."
msgstr ""
"Een fluïdumpagina tekent het schema met symbolen volgens ISO 1219; het "
"gereedschap Draad tekent daar slangen."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:124
msgid ""
"The medium is defined once, at the air supply, and every tube it reaches "
"carries it."
msgstr ""
"Het medium wordt één keer vastgelegd, bij de persluchtvoeding, en elke slang "
"die het bereikt voert het."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:125
msgid ""
"A valve's solenoid is drawn on an electrical page from the valve, and the "
"two know each other."
msgstr ""
"De magneetspoel van een ventiel wordt vanuit het ventiel op een elektrische "
"pagina getekend, en de twee kennen elkaar."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:126
msgid ""
"A valve terminal is drawn as its positions; a click on a cell fills it, and "
"its valve stands there with ports a tube reaches. The terminal's own supply "
"port 1 feeds every valve on it."
msgstr ""
"Een ventieleiland wordt getekend als zijn posities; een klik op een cel vult "
"hem, en zijn ventiel staat daar met poorten die een slang bereikt. De "
"voedingspoort 1 van het eiland zelf voedt elk ventiel erop."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:128
msgid ""
"The tube table lists the tubes with their ends, medium and size; **Output ▸ "
"Report block…** prints it as a tube list."
msgstr ""
"De slangentabel toont de slangen met hun einden, medium en maat; **Uitvoer ▸ "
"Rapportblok…** drukt hem af als slangenlijst."

#: src/tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md:130
msgid ""
"To look up these jobs later, see [Work in the Fluid "
"workspace](../how-to/fluid-workspace.md)."
msgstr ""
"Om deze taken later op te zoeken, zie [Werken in de werkruimte "
"fluïdtechniek](../how-to/fluid-workspace.md)."

#: src/how-to/index.md:3
msgid ""
"A how-to guide is where you look up one job when you know what you want to "
"do; a [tutorial](../tutorials/index.md) is a lesson you follow once, from "
"start to end, to learn how EW works."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:6
msgid ""
"A guide gives short steps for one goal and no background. If you are new to "
"EW, or new to its PLC, bus or pneumatic pages, start with a tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:11
msgid ""
"[Install ElektraWatt](install.md): the Windows setup, silent installs, the "
"Linux AppImage, the CLI archives and `EW_CONTENT_DIR`, upgrading and "
"uninstalling."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:13
msgid ""
"[Use the application window](application-window.md): the start page, new, "
"sample and imported projects, recovery, the command palette, workspaces, the "
"first-run setup and F1."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:15
msgid ""
"[Start a project and use templates](start-a-project.md): the New project "
"dialog, what a template brings, making, changing and no longer using a "
"template, and the check level."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:17
msgid ""
"[Draw a page from the text interface](text-interface.md): reading a page as "
"text, placing, autoconnect, frozen tags, terminal strips, external points, "
"wires and junctions, delete and export, without a screen."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:19
msgid ""
"[Use the command line on the sample project](headless-workflow.md): the `ew` "
"program checks a project, writes its pages as PDF and SVG, exports the cable "
"list and the bill of materials, and queries it in SQL. You need a checkout "
"of the repository and the `ew` program."
msgstr ""
"[De opdrachtregel gebruiken op het voorbeeldproject](headless-workflow.md): "
"het programma `ew` controleert een project, schrijft de pagina's als PDF en "
"SVG, exporteert de kabellijst en de stuklijst en bevraagt het project in "
"SQL. U hebt een checkout van de repository nodig en het programma `ew`."

#: src/how-to/index.md:22
msgid ""
"[Define a supply potential](define-supply-potential.md): a potential's name, "
"type and voltage from a selected wire, junction or terminal."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:24
msgid ""
"[Choose parts](choose-parts.md): a device's part from the property grid or "
"the part selection, a device placed from its part, and where the parts are "
"listed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:26
msgid ""
"[Draw a panel layout](panel-layout.md): a panel layout page, its enclosure "
"and mounting plate, rails and ducts, and the devices placed on them."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:28
msgid ""
"[Work in the PLC workspace](plc-workspace.md): what a station, a rack, a "
"slot, a channel and a symbol are, the jobs of TIA Portal and where they are "
"here, making and changing a station, addresses, the PLC grid, bus pages and "
"their nets, and the exchange with PLC software."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:31
msgid ""
"[Make your own parts](own-parts.md): a terminal, a cable, a cabinet, a card, "
"a head station such as an IM 152, a switch such as a SCALANCE or a device on "
"PROFINET, typed from its data sheet in Parts and kept in My parts."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:33
msgid ""
"[Work in the Fluid workspace](fluid-workspace.md): a circuit on a fluid "
"page, its medium, a valve's solenoid on an electrical page, the two kinds of "
"valve terminal, and the tubes and the tube list."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:35
msgid ""
"[Upgrade a project](upgrade-a-project.md): what a release reads and writes, "
"opening a project of an older format, the backup of the first save, `ew "
"upgrade`, and what changes in the format from 0.1.0."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:38
msgid ""
"More follow the [command-line how-to](headless-workflow.md), for example "
"replaying a command log with `ew apply`, checking a project in a CI job, and "
"exporting for a single page range."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:41
msgid "Writing a how-to guide"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:43
msgid ""
"A guide names its goal in the title (\"Export one page range as SVG\"), "
"lists what you need, gives numbered steps and ends when the goal is met. "
"Facts that belong to a command or a rule go to the "
"[reference](../reference/index.md), and are linked, not repeated."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide installs ElektraWatt on Windows with its setup or on Linux as an "
"AppImage, or the command-line program `ew` alone from its archive on Windows "
"or Linux, and then upgrades or removes it. Each download carries the version "
"in its name, shown here as `<version>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:9
msgid ""
"For the setup: Windows 10 or 11 on an x86-64 PC. No administrator rights and "
"no network connection are needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:10
msgid ""
"For the AppImage: Linux with glibc 2.35 or newer (as on Ubuntu 22.04 and "
"Debian 12) on x86-64. No administrator rights, no network connection and no "
"other package are needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:12
msgid ""
"For a CLI archive: Windows or Linux (glibc 2.35 or newer, as on Ubuntu 22.04 "
"and Debian 12) on x86-64."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:14
msgid "Verify a download"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:16
msgid ""
"Every release lists the SHA-256 checksum of each of its files in "
"`SHA256SUMS`, one of the release's files. Check a download before you run it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:19
msgid ""
"Linux: put the downloaded files and `SHA256SUMS` in one folder and run, in "
"that folder,"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:25
msgid ""
"It prints `OK` after the name of each file you downloaded. Any other word "
"means the file is not the released one: delete it and download it again."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:27
msgid ""
"Windows (PowerShell): `Get-FileHash <file> -Algorithm SHA256` prints the "
"file's checksum. It must be the one that `SHA256SUMS` lists before the "
"file's name (PowerShell prints it in capitals)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:30
msgid ""
"The releases are not signed yet; this check shows that a file is the one the "
"project published."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:32
msgid "Install with the setup on Windows"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:34
msgid "Run `ElektraWatt-<version>-x64-user-setup.exe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:35
msgid ""
"The setup is not signed yet, so Windows may stop it with **Windows protected "
"your PC**. Choose **More info**, check that the file is the one you "
"downloaded, then choose **Run anyway**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:37
msgid ""
"Keep the folder the setup proposes, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\ElektraWatt`, "
"or choose another one of your own, then choose **Install**. The folder must "
"be new or empty, hold both `elektrawatt.exe` and `uninstall.exe` of an "
"earlier installation, or be the folder of the installed version. For any "
"other folder that holds files, **Install** stays off, so that removing "
"ElektraWatt later never deletes them: choose another folder, or, when it is "
"an earlier installation that lost one of those files, delete that folder and "
"run the setup again."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:42
msgid ""
"On the last page, leave **Run ElektraWatt** ticked to start it, and choose "
"**Finish**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:44
msgid ""
"The setup installs for you alone: the program, the command-line program "
"`ew.exe`, the starter content, the sample project and this guide, all in the "
"one folder, with a shortcut **ElektraWatt** in the Start menu. It does not "
"change `PATH`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:48
msgid ""
"To install without questions, for example from a script, run the setup with "
"`/S`. Add `/D=<folder>` as the last argument, without quotes even when the "
"folder has spaces, to install into another folder:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:55
msgid ""
"The same folders are refused here too: the setup installs nothing and ends "
"with exit code 2. Give another folder, or delete the old one and run the "
"setup again."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:58
msgid "Run the AppImage on Linux"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:60
msgid ""
"The AppImage is one file, `ElektraWatt-<version>-x86_64.AppImage`, that "
"holds the program, the command-line program `ew`, the starter content, the "
"sample project and this guide. It installs nothing: you run the file itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:63
msgid "Make it executable, then start it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:70
msgid ""
"The AppImage opens itself through FUSE, which most desktops have. Where FUSE "
"is missing (in a container, or on a system without the `fuse` kernel "
"module), it stops with a message about FUSE. Run it with "
"`--appimage-extract-and-run` as the first argument instead: it unpacks "
"itself into a temporary folder for that run."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:78
msgid ""
"To run `ew`, give `ew` as the first argument, then its own arguments (after "
"`--appimage-extract-and-run` where FUSE is missing):"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:86
msgid ""
"Like the `ew` of a CLI archive, it reads the starter content only through "
"`--content` or `EW_CONTENT_DIR`. The AppImage's own content sits in a folder "
"that changes with each run, so for now unpack the AppImage once, which "
"writes the folder `squashfs-root` where you run it, and point "
"`EW_CONTENT_DIR` at the content in it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:90
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"./ElektraWatt-<version>-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract\n"
"export EW_CONTENT_DIR=\"$PWD/squashfs-root/usr/lib/elektrawatt/content\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:95
msgid "Unpack it again after an upgrade."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:97
msgid ""
"Keep the file where you like, for example in `~/Applications`. The AppImage "
"is not signed yet: check that the file is the one you downloaded against the "
"checksums published with its release."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:100
msgid "Use the CLI archive"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:102
msgid ""
"The archives hold `ew` with the starter content and the sample project, for "
"a machine without the window or for a CI job: "
"`ew-<version>-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip` and "
"`ew-<version>-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:105
msgid "Unpack the archive. It holds one folder, `ew-<version>-<target>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:106
msgid ""
"`ew` finds the starter content through `--content` or the environment "
"variable `EW_CONTENT_DIR`, not beside itself. Set `EW_CONTENT_DIR` to the "
"folder's `content` folder:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:108
msgid "Linux: `export EW_CONTENT_DIR=<folder>/content`"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:109
msgid "Windows (PowerShell): `$env:EW_CONTENT_DIR = \"<folder>\\content\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:110
msgid ""
"Check it: `ew --version` prints the version, and `ew export pdf "
"<folder>/samples/sample.ew --out sample.pdf` writes the sample project's "
"drawings as a PDF."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:113
msgid ""
"The `ew.exe` that the setup installs finds the starter content the same way: "
"set `EW_CONTENT_DIR` to the `content` folder of the installation."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:116
msgid "Upgrade"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:118
msgid ""
"Run the setup of the newer version as above. It finds the installed version "
"and proposes its folder, removes it first with its own uninstaller, then "
"installs, so no file of the older version remains. Your settings and keymaps "
"stay as they are."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:122
msgid ""
"To upgrade the AppImage, download the newer file, make it executable and "
"delete the older one. Your settings and keymaps stay as they are."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:125
msgid ""
"To upgrade `ew` from an archive, unpack the newer archive and point "
"`EW_CONTENT_DIR` at its `content` folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:127
msgid "Uninstall"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:129
msgid ""
"Open **Settings**, then **Apps** and **Installed apps** (on Windows 10: "
"**Apps & features**)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:130
msgid "Find **ElektraWatt** and choose **Uninstall**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:132
msgid ""
"To uninstall without questions, run `uninstall.exe /S` from the installation "
"folder. The uninstall removes the installation folder, the Start-menu "
"shortcut and the entry in the list of apps. It leaves your projects, your "
"settings and keymaps (in `%APPDATA%\\ElektraWatt`), and your libraries and "
"the program's cache (in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ElektraWatt`): delete those folders "
"yourself when you no longer want them."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:137
msgid ""
"The AppImage installs nothing: delete its file. It leaves your projects, "
"your settings and keymaps (in `~/.config/elektrawatt`), your libraries and "
"the program's cache (in `~/.local/share/elektrawatt` and "
"`~/.cache/elektrawatt`), and the program's lock and journal files (in "
"`~/.local/state/elektrawatt`): delete those folders yourself when you no "
"longer want them."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:142
msgid "A CLI archive installs nothing: delete its folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide describes the desktop application `elektrawatt`: what is on the "
"start page, how to make or open a project, how to arrange the window, and "
"where F1 leads. Every action of the window is an action of one registry, so "
"the same names work in the menu, in the command palette (`Ctrl+K`) and in a "
"headless UI script (`elektrawatt --headless --script`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:8
msgid "Start page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:10
msgid ""
"With no project open the window shows the start page. It has no news feed "
"and opens no connection."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:12
msgid ""
"**New project** opens the New project dialog: the name, the folder, and what "
"the project starts from (see [Start a project and use "
"templates](start-a-project.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:14
msgid ""
"**Open...** opens a project directory; the recent projects are listed below "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:15
msgid ""
"**Open sample project** opens the CC0 sample project read-only; use **Save "
"as…** to make your own copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:16
msgid ""
"**Import QElectroTech project** reads a `.qet` file into a new project "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:17
msgid ""
"**Templates…** lists your templates, makes a new one and adds one somebody "
"gave you (see [Your templates](start-a-project.md#your-templates))."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:19
msgid ""
"**Tutorials and guides** lists the tutorials (lessons you follow step by "
"step) and the how-to guides (how to do one job), as **Help ▸ Tutorials** and "
"**Help ▸ Guides** do."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:21
msgid ""
"The quick switches set the language and the keymap preset. The privacy line "
"says that this version has no update check; **change** opens the privacy "
"step of the [first-run setup](#first-run-setup)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:24
msgid "Project view"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:26
msgid ""
"With a project open the window shows the page list, the page view and the "
"status bar. The page view shows the current page read-only. `Page Down` and "
"`Page Up` move between pages, `Ctrl+G` goes to a folio, `Ctrl+Z` and "
"`Ctrl+Shift+Z` undo and redo, `Ctrl+S` saves and `Ctrl+Shift+S` saves under "
"a new name."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:30
msgid "Sample project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:32
msgid ""
"**File, Open sample project** opens the sample project read-only, so that "
"nothing is written into the installation. **Save as…** writes a copy into a "
"directory of your own and continues in the copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:35
msgid "Import a QElectroTech project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:37
msgid ""
"**File, Import...**, beside **Open...**, asks for a `.qet` file and then for "
"a new, empty directory. The importer reads the file into a new project and "
"writes it to the directory; a banner counts the pages and the findings of "
"the migration report. The same entry takes a project archive (`.ewz`): it "
"asks whether to extract it into a new folder and open that, or to open it "
"read-only."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:42
msgid "Unsaved changes and recovery"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:44
msgid ""
"Closing, quitting or opening another project with unsaved changes asks "
"whether to save, discard or cancel. If the application ends while a project "
"has unsaved changes, the next open finds the journal of that session and "
"opens the project read-only with a banner. **File, Discard unsaved changes "
"of the earlier session** deletes the journal after a confirmation and opens "
"the project for editing."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:49
msgid "Command palette"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:51
msgid ""
"`Ctrl+K` (or `Ctrl+Shift+P`) opens the palette: type a part of a name and "
"press `Enter` to run the selected entry. Disabled entries are listed last "
"with the reason. `Escape` closes it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:54
msgid "Keyboard shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:56
msgid ""
"**Help, Keyboard shortcuts** lists the bindings of the EW preset. `F1` opens "
"the guide at what is under the pointer "
"([below](#help-for-what-is-under-the-pointer))."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:59
msgid "Help for what is under the pointer"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:61
msgid ""
"Point at something and press `F1`: the guide opens at the page about it. It "
"answers for the most specific thing under the hand:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:64
msgid ""
"what the pointer rests on: a button of the command bar or a menu entry (the "
"page of its command), a row of Properties (what that row means, in "
"[Properties](../reference/properties.md)), a row of a list (what its thing "
"is; a finding its rule's page), a card of a station's view, a part of the "
"symbol editor, or a thing on the page by what it is: a contactor's coil the "
"devices', a wire the wires', a cable's mark the cables' section of [Things "
"on a page](../reference/window/things.md);"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:69
msgid ""
"else what has the keys: the row of a list or of Properties the cursor is on;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:70
msgid "else what is selected on the page;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:71
msgid "else the active tool;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:72
msgid ""
"else the screen: an open dialog or wizard (those come first), the symbol "
"editor, a station's view, the guide of the page's kind (a fluid, "
"bus-topology or panel layout page), the project view or the start page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:75
msgid ""
"While the pointer rests on something, the status bar says what `F1` opens "
"there, such as \"F1: Wires\", and a button's or a menu entry's tooltip ends "
"with \"F1: help\". **Help ▸ What is this?** opens the page of what has the "
"keys, is selected or is in front, as `F1` does with the pointer on nothing. "
"The **?** in the PLC, Bus and Fluid panels' headers and the tabs' **Guide** "
"open the guide of that job. The guide opens in your browser from the copy "
"installed with the program; in a Dutch window it opens the Dutch page where "
"the guide has one."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:81
msgid "Appearance"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:83
msgid ""
"The **View** menu sets how the window looks and behaves: **Theme**, "
"**Density**, **UI scale**, **Minimum text size**, **Reduce motion**, **Show "
"tips**, **Language**, **Wire colours**, **Mouse wheel scrolls** (instead of "
"zooming), **Collapse command bar** and the **Bottom dock**. **Use the CPU "
"renderer for the canvas** draws the page without the graphics card where its "
"driver gives trouble, and **Frame statistics** shows how fast the window "
"draws. The settings are kept for the next start."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:89
msgid "Notifications"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:91
msgid ""
"What a command did, such as \"Cable -1W1 made from 2 wires\", shows as a "
"toast at the window's corner for a few seconds; a pointer resting on it "
"keeps it, and its button undoes the change where it can be undone. "
"**Notifications** in the status bar opens the log of every toast of this "
"session."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:95
msgid "Workspaces"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:97
msgid ""
"The project view is a dock: drag the border between two panes to resize "
"them, and drag a pane's title onto another pane to rearrange or tab it. The "
"program ships the workspaces **Schematic**, **PLC** (see [Work in the PLC "
"workspace](plc-workspace.md)) and **Fluid power** (see [Work in the Fluid "
"workspace](fluid-workspace.md)). The switch at the right of the tab row, "
"beside the command search, shows them with the active one marked (a saved "
"workspace by its name while it is active); a click loads another. **View ▸ "
"Workspaces** lists them and the saved workspaces, saves the current layout "
"under a name and resets the layout (a shipped workspace to its own layout). "
"A workspace is a file `workspaces/<name>.json` in the configuration "
"directory; the layout is saved a second after its last change."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:106
msgid "First-run setup"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:108
msgid ""
"On the first start the application asks for the language and what you come "
"from (EPLAN, QElectroTech, KiCad or other), says what it does with your data "
"(this version has no update check and sends no crash reports; **Privacy "
"(PRIVACY.md)** opens the full statement), asks for the appearance (theme and "
"scale), and then what to do first. Every step can be skipped. **Help, "
"First-run setup** runs it again. Nothing in it makes a network connection."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:113
msgid "About"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:115
msgid ""
"**Help, About ElektraWatt** shows the version, the page-view renderer, the "
"privacy state, the licence, the engineering-verification disclaimer, the "
"support line (before 1.0 only the latest 0.x release is supported), and the "
"third-party notices."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide shows how to start a new project, what a template is and what it "
"brings to a project, and how to make a template, change it and stop using "
"it. The words in **bold** are the words of the window."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:6
msgid "Start a new project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:8
msgid ""
"On the start page press **New project**, or press `Ctrl+N` (**File ▸ New "
"project…**). The **New project** dialog opens."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:10
msgid ""
"Type the project's **Name**. Under it, **Folder** is where the project's "
"folder is made; **Browse…** chooses another one. A folder typed without its "
"whole path, such as `Pumps`, is a folder in your projects folder. The line "
"under the folder says where the project will be. A folder that is not there "
"yet is made with the project."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:13
msgid ""
"Under **Start from**, choose what the project starts with. Each line says in "
"words what it brings:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:14
msgid ""
"**Empty project**: one empty page, with the program's frame and title block "
"and its settings."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:15
msgid ""
"Each of your templates, with its frame and title block, its check level, its "
"text styles and its sections. The template you use for new projects is "
"marked **used for new projects** and is chosen when the dialog opens."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:17
msgid ""
"**Another project…**: choose an earlier project to start like it, for "
"example a standard cabinet."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:18
msgid ""
"A template or a project that has pages shows the box **Also copy its pages "
"and the devices on them**. It is off: without it the new project starts with "
"one empty page and none of the old pages or devices. Tick it only when you "
"want those pages, for example to start from a standard cabinet."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:21
msgid ""
"Press **Create**. The project is made and opens. While something is missing "
"or wrong (no name, a file where the folder would go, a drive that is not "
"there, a project of that name there already) the line over **Create** says "
"what; when the folder is to be made, the line says so."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:25
msgid "Nothing comes into the new project that the dialog did not name."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:27
msgid "What a template is"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:29
msgid ""
"A template is a project folder that says how new projects start. It holds:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:31
msgid "the frame and title block, and your company's name and logo in it;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:32
msgid "the check level (see [The check level](#the-check-level));"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:33
msgid ""
"the text styles, the wire colours and sizes, the numbering and the "
"cross-reference style;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:34
msgid ""
"the sections and the structure your drawings always have, and the lists and "
"overviews on their own pages;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:35
msgid "the first revision label."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:37
msgid ""
"A template can hold pages and devices too, but a new project takes them only "
"when you tick **Also copy its pages and the devices on them**. A project "
"made from a template keeps what it started with: changing the template later "
"changes only the projects you make after that."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:41
msgid "Your templates"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:43
msgid ""
"**Templates…** on the start page, and in the Project tab under "
"**Templates**, lists your templates. Each line shows the template's name, "
"what it holds and its folder. Click a template to choose it, then:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:46
msgid ""
"**Use for new projects** marks it for the New project dialog; **Do not use** "
"takes the mark off, so new projects start empty unless you choose a template."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:48
msgid ""
"**Edit** opens the template to change it (see [Change a "
"template](#change-a-template))."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:49
msgid ""
"**Rename** changes the name the list shows and New project offers: type the "
"name and press **Save name** or `Enter`. The folder keeps its own name. A "
"name another template has is refused."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:51
msgid ""
"**Remove from the list** takes it out of the list. Its folder stays on the "
"disk; **Add a folder…** brings it back."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:53
msgid ""
"Under the list, **New template…** makes a template for your company's "
"drawings with the wizard, and **Add a folder…** takes a template somebody "
"gave you (any project folder)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:56
msgid "Make a template for your company's drawings"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:58
msgid ""
"**New template…** opens the wizard. It has ten steps: the company, the frame "
"and title block, the colour profile, the wire cross-sections, identifiers "
"and numbering, structure and sections, revisions, languages and formats, "
"reports and checks, and a review. Each step says what it is and where you "
"can change it later, and its **Result** shows what your choices make, such "
"as the name of the third contactor on page 102; the **Example** beside it "
"shows a cover page and a schematic page with your choices. Your choices are "
"kept until you finish: **Close** asks first, and keeps them unless you "
"choose **Close and drop my answers**; **New template…** then opens the "
"wizard where you left off."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:65
msgid ""
"A new template's texts print readably: no style is lower than 1.2 mm. Their "
"heights and typefaces are on **Text styles…** in the template (see [Text "
"styles](#text-styles))."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:68
msgid ""
"The last step lists every choice. **Use this template for new projects** is "
"ticked: the New project dialog then chooses this template first. Untick it "
"to keep the template in the list without using it yet. The line under it "
"says where the template is made: a folder named after it in your templates "
"folder (`Templates` in your projects folder), with a number after the name "
"when that folder is taken. **Choose another folder…** puts it in another "
"folder. **Create the template** writes it there, the check level included."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:74
msgid "Make a template of a project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:76
msgid ""
"When a project is set up the way you like, **Save as template…** (Project "
"tab, **Templates**) makes the next projects start like it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:79
msgid "Type the template's **Name** and the **Folder** it is made in."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:80
msgid ""
"**Its pages and their devices** is off: the template takes the project's "
"settings, frames and title blocks, text styles, sections and structure, and "
"no pages. Tick it to take the pages too."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:82
msgid ""
"**Use for new projects** is ticked: the New project dialog then chooses this "
"template first."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:83
msgid "Press **Save as template**. The template is in your list."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:85
msgid "Change a template"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:87
msgid ""
"In **Templates…** choose the template and press **Edit**. The template opens "
"like a project, with a band across the window: \"You are editing the "
"template …. Projects made from it later start like this.\" The title bar "
"says **Template**. Change what you want and save; **Close the template** on "
"the band closes it. A template does not appear among the recent projects."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:92
msgid "Stop using a template"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:94
msgid ""
"In **Templates…** choose the template and press **Do not use**: new projects "
"start empty unless you choose a template in the New project dialog. **Remove "
"from the list** also takes it out of the list; the folder stays on the disk."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:98
msgid "Text styles"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:100
msgid ""
"Every text on a drawing prints in a text style: device tags in **Device "
"tag**, wire numbers in **Wire marker**, the number beside a terminal in "
"**Terminal number**, and so on. **Text styles…** (Project tab, under "
"**Setup**) lists the project's styles under what they print: Devices, Wires "
"and cables, Terminals, Page, and Cover and sections. Each line says where "
"the style prints and has:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:105
msgid ""
"**Typeface**: Standard (Noto Sans), Narrow (Noto Sans Condensed), Mono (Noto "
"Sans Mono, every character equally wide) or Technical drawing (Routed "
"Gothic, the single-stroke lettering of technical drawings). The typefaces "
"are part of the program, so a drawing looks the same on every computer; the "
"fonts installed on your computer are not offered. Technical drawing has no "
"bold: with it, **B** is off and the style prints regular."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:109
msgid ""
"**Height**: the height of a capital letter on paper, in millimetres, as "
"drawing standards give a text's size. Type a value, or choose one of the "
"standard heights 1.8, 2.5, 3.5, 5 and 7 mm beside it. A height under 1.2 mm "
"is allowed, and the line says that it is too small to read on paper; Check "
"project then warns about it too."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:112
msgid "**B**: bold."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:113
msgid ""
"A sample in the style, in proportion to the others, and **Reset**, which "
"brings the style back to what a new project has."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:116
msgid ""
"Above the styles, **Standard typeface** says what prints the Standard "
"typeface. **Choose a font file…** takes a TrueType or OpenType file (`.ttf`, "
"`.otf`, at most 2.5 MiB) into the project, and every text in the Standard "
"typeface then prints in it: on screen, in the PDF and the print, and as the "
"font the DXF names. Use it to match a drawing set printed in another "
"typeface, such as Liberation Sans. The project carries the whole file, so "
"give the project only to those the font's licence allows; a font whose "
"licence forbids embedding it in a document is refused. **Use Noto Sans** "
"takes it back."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:123
msgid ""
"A change shows on the open page at once and is one step of **Undo**. "
"**Standard sizes** gives every style the height a new project has and keeps "
"typefaces and bold; **Reset all** brings back every style. A new project, "
"and every template the wizard makes, starts readable; the styles are the "
"project's, and a template carries its own to the projects made from it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:128
msgid ""
"The warning \"Wire annotation prints 0.94 mm high, too small to read on "
"paper\" opens **Text styles** at that style when you open it in Messages "
"(double-click, or Enter); **Set to 1.2 mm** on the message mends it in one "
"step."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:131
msgid ""
"A free text has its own **Style** in its Properties, chosen by name, and its "
"own **Height** for that one text. The Properties of a text or a device also "
"say which style it prints in, with **Change styles…** beside it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:134
msgid "The check level"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:136
msgid ""
"Before drawings go out, the program checks them. The check level says how "
"strict that check is:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:138
msgid ""
"**Pre-delivery**: what must be right before drawings go out, such as an "
"interruption point without its partner on another page. A few tidiness "
"checks are left out, such as a structure code with a separator in it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:140
msgid "**Full**: every check, the tidiness checks too."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:142
msgid ""
"While you draw, the Messages panel shows every check whatever the level. A "
"project made with an earlier version may have the level **Drafting**: it "
"keeps working and checks as **Full** does, and **Check level…** shows it as "
"Full."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/start-a-project.md:145
msgid ""
"The wizard's step **Reports and checks** sets it for a template, and "
"**Create the template** writes it. In a project (or an open template), "
"**Check level…** in the Project tab under **Setup** changes it. Only a "
"person sets the check level, in the window: a script or the command line "
"cannot."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide is for drawing without a screen: a headless UI script "
"(`elektrawatt --headless --script`), or any client that reads the interface "
"as text (`ui.describe`) and the content of a page as text (`page.describe`). "
"Every step below is an action of the same registry as the window's menus; "
"the parameters are those the action list prints."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:7
msgid "Read the page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:9
msgid ""
"`page.describe` (with `folio`, or the page shown) lists the page in reading "
"order: a `rep` line per drawn symbol with its tag, cell, symbol and "
"position, its `cp` lines (connection points, the wires drawn to each, the "
"potential), a `wire` line per drawn connection and a `junction` line per "
"junction."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:13
msgid ""
"**Positions are the positions you click.** Every `at <x> <y> mm` is a point "
"of the sheet, the same coordinates `canvas.press`, `canvas.pointer` and "
"`cursor.set` take: `cursor.set` with a `cp` line's position puts the grid "
"cursor on that connection point, and the cursor line names it. `cursor.set` "
"with `pin` (`-102F1:2`, as a `cp` line names it) puts it there by name, and "
"with `tag` (`-102F1`) on the insertion point of that device's one symbol on "
"the page, so a script that names them still reaches them when the page is "
"laid out anew."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:18
msgid ""
"**A `wire` line ends with a point on the wire** (`at <x> <y> mm`) where a "
"click with the Select tool picks that wire and nothing else. A wire whose "
"drawn route runs along another wire or through a symbol for its whole length "
"has no such point; select it by its ends instead: `select.set` with `refs` "
"`[\"wire:-101F1:1 -> -103F1:1\"]` (the ends as the `wire` line prints them, "
"either way round; a junction end is `junction <n>`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:23
msgid "Place symbols"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:25
msgid ""
"`place.pick` (or Insert, Symbol... and the picker) arms the Place tool; "
"`nav.goto` with a cell (`101-B2`) or `cursor.set` moves the ghost, and "
"`canvas.activate` (Enter) places it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:27
msgid ""
"**Autoconnect.** When the ghost's free connection points face free "
"connection points on the page, the ghost proposes wires to them: "
"`ui.describe` prints them as `canvas proposals <n> \"<cp>\" -> "
"\"<target>\"`, and the status hint says that Enter connects them. "
"`canvas.activate` with `alt` (Alt+Enter, Alt+Click) places the symbol "
"without connecting; `tool.next_proposal` (Tab) tries the next target along "
"each ray. After a placement that connected, the toast names the wires it "
"drew; one Undo removes the symbol with its wires. A connection point further "
"than 20 grid steps away, or one behind another wire, is not proposed: the "
"toast offers it instead, and its Connect draws the wire. Tab still proposes "
"it. A pole that faces nothing in line joins the nearest free point, up to "
"three grid steps aside, of a device another of its wires joins, by a wire "
"that steps aside halfway: a two-pole breaker under a two-pole switch whose "
"poles stand closer together gets both poles wired."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:37
msgid "Freeze a tag"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:39
msgid ""
"Select the device (`select.set` with `refs` `[\"tag:-101S1\"]`) and set its "
"frozen tag as the page should print it: `props.set` with `row` "
"`device/tag.frozen` and `value` `S51` prints `S51`; `-S51` prints `-S51` "
"(the sign shown). An empty value unfreezes it. The derived tag row's Freeze "
"action and the `tag.frozen.text` and `tag.frozen.sign` rows do the same in "
"several steps."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:44 src/reference/window/panels.md:101
msgid "Terminal strips"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:46
msgid ""
"`strip.new` creates an empty terminal strip; the toast names it. It draws "
"nothing until its first terminal is placed: `tool.place_terminal` with "
"`strip` (and `kind`, `part`, `count`, `from` where they differ from its "
"form's proposals) arms the Place tool on that strip. A terminal part chosen "
"in the device browser, or `place.pick` with a terminal symbol and no "
"`strip`, opens a second picker that lists the strips (`row strip \"<tag>\" "
"terminal \"<next>\" @<n>`); choose the strip there. In a project without a "
"strip, the terminal part chosen in the device browser goes on a new strip "
"made for it, and `place.pick` without `strip` is refused. The terminal takes "
"the row's proposed designation unless the choice names one (`picker.choose` "
"with `designation` `PE`, on either picker). A strip without terminals prints "
"its tag with question marks in it (`-X??`) because its counter is given with "
"its first terminal; the `@n` at the end of its row selects it (`select.set` "
"with `ids` `[\"@n\"]`), and `edit.delete_selection` deletes a strip that has "
"no terminals. After its first terminal the strip has its real tag "
"(`-X1011`); `place.pick` with a strip name the project does not have fails "
"with a message that lists the project's strips."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:58
msgid ""
"In the Terminal strips navigator a row is named by what it prints: a "
"terminal by its tag, the strip's label, a colon and its own (`-X1031:1`), "
"and a strip by its tag (`-X1031`). `navigator.select` with `keys` and "
"`table.set` with `rows` take these names (`table.set` with `kind` `strips`, "
"`rows` `[\"-X1021:1\",\"-X1021:2\"]`, a `column` and a `value`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:62
msgid ""
"A terminal's connection points print `strip:terminal/clamp` (`-X1021:1/b`): "
"the starter terminal has clamp `a` on side 1 (internal) and `b` on side 2 "
"(field), `-X1021:1/a` and `-X1021:1/b`. Where two clamps of one level carry "
"the same letter, as on a library terminal that names them so, the label adds "
"`#n` in connection-point order: `-X1:1/a#1` and `-X1:1/a#2`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:66
msgid "Bridge terminals"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:68
msgid ""
"Select two or more terminals of one strip on the page (click the first "
"terminal symbol, Shift+click the others, or `select.set` with their "
"representations), then run `strip.bridge` (Draw ▸ Bridge terminals). It "
"draws a plug-in bridge between each two terminals that follow each other in "
"the strip's order among the selected ones, in one undo step: selecting "
"`-X1031:1` to `:4` draws 1-2, 2-3 and 3-4, whatever order you selected them "
"in. The bridges join the group of a bridge they touch, or else the strip's "
"next free group. A bridge ends on the terminal's bridge shaft when its part "
"has one; a terminal without a part (or with a part that has no shaft) is "
"bridged at its lowest side-1 connection point, so you can draw the bridges "
"before choosing the parts. A pair that is bridged already is left as it is. "
"The command is refused when fewer than two terminals are selected, when they "
"belong to more than one strip, or when every pair is bridged already; the "
"refusal names the reason."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:78
msgid "External points"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:80
msgid ""
"`structure.new` with `kind` `location` and the location (`+J-E-206`), then "
"`external_ref.new` with the location, the device and the connection, once "
"per connection. Each says in the toast what it created; the external "
"reference's label is `<device>:<connection>` (`JE-N1000-03:L`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:83
msgid ""
"With the Interruption point tool armed, `ip.external` takes that label, or "
"the label after the location as the page prints it (`+J-E-206 / "
"JE-N1000-03:L`); the ghost becomes an external point. A text that no "
"reference has fails with a message that lists the labels the project has."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:87
msgid "Draw wires"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:89
msgid ""
"With the Wire tool armed, put the grid cursor on a connection point (its "
"`cp` position), press Enter, move to the other end and press Enter. The "
"`canvas route` line names the start (`from`) and the end (`end`) before you "
"commit."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:92
msgid ""
"`cp.next` and `cp.previous` step the cursor through the page's connection "
"points in reading order of the cell **column by column** (column 0 to 16, "
"and in a column row A to H), then by position; this differs from the page "
"text's order, which goes row by row. Going to a `cp` position with "
"`cursor.set` needs no walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:95
msgid ""
"**Ending a wire on a wire** makes a junction there and splits that wire. The "
"route's `end` names the wire it would split by its ends (`@102-D4 -102S1:13 "
"-> -102Q1:13`): check it before Enter when two wires run through one cell."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:98
msgid "Delete"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:100
msgid ""
"`edit.delete_selection` (Delete) deletes what is selected on the page shown: "
"a representation, a wire, a junction or an item. A device selected by its "
"tag is deleted as its representations on the page shown (the device stays, "
"its functions unplaced); a device that no page draws, such as an empty "
"terminal strip, is deleted itself. A device drawn only on another page is "
"refused (`reason-not-on-page`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:105
msgid "Save and export"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:107
msgid ""
"`file.save` saves. `file.export_pdf` with a file name writes the PDF; the "
"toast names the file, its page count and its size."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:3
msgid ""
"Here you use the `ew` command-line program on the sample project: you check "
"the project, write its pages as PDF and SVG, export the cable list and the "
"bill of materials, and ask the project questions in SQL. Nothing needs the "
"desktop application, and nothing leaves your machine: `ew` never uses the "
"network."
msgstr ""
"Hier gebruikt u het opdrachtregelprogramma `ew` op het voorbeeldproject: u "
"controleert het project, schrijft de pagina's als PDF en SVG, exporteert de "
"kabellijst en de stuklijst, en stelt het project vragen in SQL. Niets heeft "
"de desktopapplicatie nodig, en niets verlaat uw computer: `ew` gebruikt "
"nooit het netwerk."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:7
msgid ""
"The sample project is a small conveyor drive with a pneumatic clamp: a 400 V "
"supply, a 24 V DC control circuit, a motor starter, a PLC with its cards on "
"a PROFIBUS DP line, a valve terminal with two cylinders, the panel layout "
"and the generated reports. Where a real project names a customer or a "
"person, it holds a placeholder such as `Sample Customer`. You only read it "
"here; none of the steps changes it."
msgstr ""
"Het voorbeeldproject is een kleine transportbandaandrijving met een "
"pneumatische klem: een voeding van 400 V, een stuurstroomkring van 24 V DC, "
"een motorstarter, een PLC met zijn kaarten op een PROFIBUS DP-lijn, een "
"ventieleiland met twee cilinders, de montageplaat en de gegenereerde "
"rapporten. Waar een echt project een klant of een persoon noemt, staat er "
"een plaatsaanduiding zoals `Sample Customer`. U leest het hier alleen; geen "
"van de stappen verandert het."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:12
msgid "Before you start"
msgstr "Voordat u begint"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:14
msgid "You need:"
msgstr "U hebt nodig:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:16
msgid ""
"a checkout of the ElektraWatt repository, which holds the project in "
"`fixtures/sample/`;"
msgstr ""
"een checkout van de repository van ElektraWatt, die het project in "
"`fixtures/sample/` bevat;"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:17
msgid ""
"the `ew` program, built from the checkout with `cargo build --release -p "
"ew`. It is then `target/release/ew` (`target\\release\\ew.exe` on Windows). "
"The examples write plain `ew`; put the program on your `PATH` or type its "
"path."
msgstr ""
"het programma `ew`, gebouwd uit de checkout met `cargo build --release -p "
"ew`. Het is dan `target/release/ew` (`target\\release\\ew.exe` op Windows). "
"De voorbeelden schrijven gewoon `ew`; zet het programma in uw `PATH` of typ "
"het pad ervan."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:21
msgid ""
"Run every command from the root of the checkout. Check that `ew` works; it "
"prints its name and its version:"
msgstr ""
"Voer elke opdracht uit vanuit de hoofdmap van de checkout. Controleer dat "
"`ew` werkt; het toont zijn naam en zijn versie:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:27
msgid "Then make a directory for the results:"
msgstr "Maak daarna een map voor de resultaten:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:33
msgid "Where the project comes from"
msgstr "Waar het project vandaan komt"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:35
msgid ""
"EW makes the sample project itself. The script "
"`fixtures/sample/sample.uiscript` holds the window's own actions in order, "
"from the new-project wizard to the rails of the panel layout, as a user "
"chooses them; `cargo xtask gen` runs it and writes the project "
"`fixtures/sample/sample.ew`, and a test checks that the checked-in project "
"is what the script makes."
msgstr ""
"EW maakt het voorbeeldproject zelf. Het script "
"`fixtures/sample/sample.uiscript` bevat de eigen acties van het venster in "
"volgorde, van de wizard voor een nieuw project tot de rails van de "
"montageplaat, zoals een gebruiker ze kiest; `cargo xtask gen` voert het uit "
"en schrijft het project `fixtures/sample/sample.ew`, en een test controleert "
"dat het ingecheckte project is wat het script maakt."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:39
msgid ""
"A project is a directory, here `fixtures/sample/sample.ew`. Every `ew` "
"command that works on a project takes it as its first argument."
msgstr ""
"Een project is een map, hier `fixtures/sample/sample.ew`. Elke opdracht van "
"`ew` die met een project werkt, neemt het als eerste argument."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:42
msgid "Step 1: check the project"
msgstr "Stap 1: het project controleren"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:44
msgid ""
"`ew check` runs the check rules over the project and prints what it finds, "
"one finding per line:"
msgstr ""
"`ew check` voert de controleregels uit op het project en toont wat het "
"vindt, één bevinding per regel:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:52
msgid ""
"The sample project is clean, so only the counts print. In a project with "
"findings each one is a line before them, for example a tag that is frozen "
"and differs from what the numbering scheme would give:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:59
msgid ""
"Each line gives the severity, the rule ID, the rule's six-digit code, the "
"thing the finding is about and a message. The last line counts the findings, "
"and the standard error stream repeats the count with the scheme's name."
msgstr ""
"Elke regel geeft de ernst, de regel-ID, de zescijferige code van de regel, "
"het onderwerp van de bevinding en een melding. De laatste regel telt de "
"bevindingen, en de standaardfoutuitvoer herhaalt het aantal met de naam van "
"het schema."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:62
msgid "Look at the exit code:"
msgstr "Bekijk de afsluitcode:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:69
msgid ""
"`ew` exits with 0 when the project has no unwaived errors, 1 when it has "
"some and 2 when it could not run the check at all (a project that cannot be "
"opened, a wrong option). A script should treat 2 as a failure of the tooling "
"and 1 as an answer about the drawing. The exit codes of every subcommand are "
"in [doc 17 "
"§3.2](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/design/17-extensibility.md#32-exit-codes)."
msgstr ""
"`ew` sluit af met 0 als het project geen fouten zonder vrijstelling heeft, "
"met 1 als het die wel heeft, en met 2 als het de controle helemaal niet kon "
"uitvoeren (een project dat niet te openen is, een verkeerde optie). Een "
"script hoort 2 te behandelen als een fout van het gereedschap en 1 als een "
"antwoord over de tekening. De afsluitcodes van elke subopdracht staan in "
"[doc 17 "
"§3.2](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/design/17-extensibility.md#32-exit-codes)."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:74
msgid ""
"Each rule has a page in the reference: "
"[`EW-NUM-001`](../reference/rules/ew-num-001.md) is the information that a "
"frozen device tag differs from what the numbering scheme would give, and "
"[`EW-CORE-033`](../reference/rules/ew-core-033.md) is the error of a cable "
"without a length, which the sample does not have."
msgstr ""
"Elke regel heeft een pagina in de naslag: "
"[`EW-NUM-001`](../reference/rules/ew-num-001.md) is de melding dat een "
"vastgezet kenmerk van een apparaat afwijkt van wat het nummeringsschema zou "
"geven, en [`EW-CORE-033`](../reference/rules/ew-core-033.md) is de fout van "
"een kabel zonder lengte, die het voorbeeldproject niet heeft."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:78
msgid ""
"`ew check` takes a scheme and a mode. The default is the `pre-delivery` "
"scheme in `release` mode; `--format json` and `--format md` change the "
"output, and `--out <file>` writes it to a file."
msgstr ""
"`ew check` neemt een schema en een modus. Standaard is dat het schema "
"`pre-delivery` in de modus `release`; `--format json` en `--format md` "
"veranderen de uitvoer, en `--out <file>` schrijft die naar een bestand."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:81
msgid "Step 2: write the pages as PDF and SVG"
msgstr "Stap 2: de pagina's als PDF en SVG schrijven"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:83
msgid ""
"`ew export pdf` writes every page of the project into one PDF/A-2b file, "
"with an outline, page labels and cross-reference links:"
msgstr ""
"`ew export pdf` schrijft elke pagina van het project in één "
"PDF/A-2b-bestand, met een inhoudsopgave, paginalabels en links voor de "
"kruisverwijzingen:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:92
msgid ""
"The second line, on the standard error stream, counts remarks about the "
"rendering of single pages and says what they are: here, fields of the title "
"blocks that the project leaves empty. They do not stop the export. The "
"directory of `--out` must exist, which is why you made `out` first."
msgstr ""
"De tweede regel, op de standaardfoutuitvoer, telt opmerkingen over het "
"weergeven van afzonderlijke pagina's en zegt wat ze zijn: hier velden van de "
"titelblokken die het project leeg laat. Ze houden de export niet tegen. De "
"map van `--out` moet bestaan; daarom hebt u eerst `out` gemaakt."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:96
msgid ""
"`ew export svg` writes one SVG file per page into a directory. Use `--pages` "
"to select folios; this writes the drawn pages 101 to 105:"
msgstr ""
"`ew export svg` schrijft één SVG-bestand per pagina in een map. Kies folio's "
"met `--pages`; dit schrijft de getekende pagina's 101 tot en met 105:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:105
msgid ""
"The directory now holds `101.svg` to `105.svg`. Both ends of a range are "
"folio labels of the project. `ew` replaces the directory as a whole, so it "
"must be empty or one an earlier SVG export wrote (the file "
"`.elektrawatt-export` in it says so); a stale page of an earlier export "
"cannot remain in it, and a folder of your own files is refused."
msgstr ""
"De map bevat nu `101.svg` tot en met `105.svg`. Beide einden van een bereik "
"zijn foliolabels van het project. `ew` vervangt de map als geheel, dus ze "
"moet leeg zijn of door een eerdere SVG-export geschreven (het bestand "
"`.elektrawatt-export` erin zegt dat); een oude pagina van een eerdere export "
"kan er niet in achterblijven, en een map met eigen bestanden wordt geweigerd."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:109
msgid ""
"The same export always gives the same bytes. The only date in the output "
"comes from the project, or from the environment variable `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` "
"when you set it, never from the clock of your machine; `--threads <n>` "
"changes the speed and never the result."
msgstr ""
"Dezelfde export geeft altijd dezelfde bytes. De enige datum in de uitvoer "
"komt uit het project, of uit de omgevingsvariabele `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` als u "
"die zet, nooit uit de klok van uw computer; `--threads <n>` verandert de "
"snelheid en nooit het resultaat."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:113
msgid "Step 3: export the cable list and the bill of materials"
msgstr "Stap 3: de kabellijst en de stuklijst exporteren"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:115
msgid "Two more exports write the rows of a report as CSV:"
msgstr "Nog twee exports schrijven de rijen van een rapport als CSV:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:124
msgid ""
"Open `out/cables.csv` in a text editor or a spreadsheet. It starts like this:"
msgstr ""
"Open `out/cables.csv` in een teksteditor of een spreadsheet. Het begint zo:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:134
msgid ""
"The supply cable `-101W1` comes in from outside the cabinet, so it has no "
"From end; the motor cable `-103W1` runs from the strip `-X1031` to the "
"motor, and the bus cable `-105W1` from the PLC to the valve terminal. Each "
"has the length typed in its Properties. Below these rows come the cores of "
"each cable, one row per core."
msgstr ""
"De voedingskabel `-101W1` komt van buiten de kast, dus hij heeft geen "
"Van-einde; de motorkabel `-103W1` loopt van de strook `-X1031` naar de "
"motor, en de buskabel `-105W1` van de PLC naar het ventieleiland. Elke kabel "
"heeft de lengte die in zijn Eigenschappen is getypt. Onder deze regels "
"volgen de aders van elke kabel, één regel per ader."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:138
msgid ""
"`--mode` of `ew export bom` is `parts` (the default; every component part) "
"or `assembly`, which replaces the parts a configured product covers by one "
"row for the product. Both write the rows of the project's own report page, "
"so the CSV equals what the drawing prints. A project without that page (a "
"new project from the built-in template) refuses them and names the command "
"that writes the rows of the whole project instead, such as `ew export report "
"--kind cable_overview_diagrams`."
msgstr ""
"`--mode` van `ew export bom` is `parts` (de standaard; elk onderdeelartikel) "
"of `assembly`, dat de artikelen die een geconfigureerd product omvat "
"vervangt door één rij voor het product. Beide schrijven de rijen van de "
"eigen rapportpagina van het project, dus de CSV is gelijk aan wat de "
"tekening afdrukt. Een project zonder die pagina (een nieuw project uit de "
"ingebouwde sjabloon) weigert ze en noemt de opdracht die in plaats daarvan "
"de rijen van het hele project schrijft, zoals `ew export report --kind "
"cable_overview_diagrams`."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:144
msgid "Step 4: ask the project questions with SQL"
msgstr "Stap 4: het project vragen stellen met SQL"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:146
msgid ""
"`ew sql` runs one read-only `SELECT` over the project's SQL views, which are "
"named `api_v1_...`:"
msgstr ""
"`ew sql` voert één alleen-lezen `SELECT` uit op de SQL-views van het "
"project, die `api_v1_...` heten:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:148
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ ew sql fixtures/sample/sample.ew \"SELECT count(*) FROM api_v1_terminal\"\n"
"count(*)\n"
"18\n"
"1 rows\n"
"```"
msgstr ""
"```console\n"
"$ ew sql fixtures/sample/sample.ew \"SELECT count(*) FROM api_v1_terminal\"\n"
"count(*)\n"
"18\n"
"1 rows\n"
"```"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:155
msgid ""
"The result is CSV with a header line, on the standard output; the row count "
"goes to the standard error stream. Ask for the cables and where their "
"lengths come from:"
msgstr ""
"Het resultaat is CSV met een kopregel, op de standaarduitvoer; het aantal "
"rijen gaat naar de standaardfoutuitvoer. Vraag naar de kabels en waar hun "
"lengtes vandaan komen:"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:158
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ ew sql fixtures/sample/sample.ew \"SELECT tag, cores, length_source FROM "
"api_v1_cable ORDER BY tag\"\n"
"tag,cores,length_source\n"
"-101W1,5,manual\n"
"-103W1,5,manual\n"
"-105W1,3,manual\n"
"3 rows\n"
"```"
msgstr ""
"```console\n"
"$ ew sql fixtures/sample/sample.ew \"SELECT tag, cores, length_source FROM "
"api_v1_cable ORDER BY tag\"\n"
"tag,cores,length_source\n"
"-101W1,5,manual\n"
"-103W1,5,manual\n"
"-105W1,3,manual\n"
"3 rows\n"
"```"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:167
msgid ""
"`ew sql schema` prints every view with its columns. Only `SELECT`, `WITH` "
"and `VALUES` statements run; anything else, such as `ATTACH`, is refused and "
"the exit code is 2."
msgstr ""
"`ew sql schema` toont elke view met zijn kolommen. Alleen opdrachten "
"`SELECT`, `WITH` en `VALUES` worden uitgevoerd; al het andere, zoals "
"`ATTACH`, wordt geweigerd en de afsluitcode is 2."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:170
msgid "What you have done"
msgstr "Wat u hebt gedaan"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:172
msgid ""
"You checked a project and read the exit code, wrote its pages as PDF and "
"SVG, exported a cable list and a bill of materials, and queried the project "
"in SQL, all from the terminal. The same commands run in a script or a CI "
"job, because they read the project without locking it and never need a "
"display."
msgstr ""
"U hebt een project gecontroleerd en de afsluitcode gelezen, de pagina's als "
"PDF en SVG geschreven, een kabellijst en een stuklijst geëxporteerd en het "
"project in SQL bevraagd, alles vanuit de terminal. Dezelfde opdrachten "
"draaien in een script of een CI-taak, omdat ze het project lezen zonder het "
"te vergrendelen en nooit een beeldscherm nodig hebben."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:176
msgid "Where to go next"
msgstr "Waar u verder kunt"

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:178
msgid ""
"The [command reference](../reference/commands/index.md) lists the commands "
"that change a project, and the [rule reference](../reference/rules/index.md) "
"the rules that `ew check` runs."
msgstr ""
"De [opdrachtnaslag](../reference/commands/index.md) toont de opdrachten die "
"een project veranderen, en de [regelnaslag](../reference/rules/index.md) de "
"regels die `ew check` uitvoert."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:180
msgid ""
"More [how-to guides](index.md) for single tasks, such as checking a project "
"in a CI job, will follow this one."
msgstr ""
"Meer [instructies](index.md) voor losse taken, zoals een project controleren "
"in een CI-taak, volgen na deze."

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:181
msgid ""
"The [explanation](../explanation/index.md) section links the design of the "
"model and of the check engine."
msgstr ""
"Het onderdeel [uitleg](../explanation/index.md) verwijst naar het ontwerp "
"van het model en van de controle-engine."

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide gives a drawn potential its name, its type and its voltage in the "
"application window, so that its wires take the colours of the project's "
"profile and the reports print its name. A potential that you have only drawn "
"is known by its connection points and has a fallback name until you define "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:9 src/how-to/choose-parts.md:9
msgid "A project open for writing, with a schematic page shown."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:10
msgid "A wire, a junction or a terminal on the potential."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:14
msgid "Select one item on the potential:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:15
msgid ""
"a wire: the potential is defined at its start, a connection point or a "
"junction;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:16
msgid "a junction;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:17
msgid "a terminal: the potential is defined at its first connection point."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:18
msgid "Open **Define potential…** in one of these ways:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:19
msgid "the **Draw** menu, under **Connect**, beside **Bond**;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:20
msgid "a right-click on the item, then **Define potential…**;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:21
msgid ""
"the command palette (`Ctrl+K`): type `potential`, `supply` or `voltage`, "
"then press Enter."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:22
msgid ""
"In the form, type the **Name** (`24VDC-1`). The **Type** and the **Voltage** "
"may stay empty; choose the type from its list, the words Properties shows "
"(`L+`, `L-`, `L1`, `N`, `PE`, …), and type the voltage as the pages print "
"it: `24 V DC` or `230 V AC`. `DC` or `AC` in the voltage also sets the "
"current."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:25
msgid ""
"Press Enter. The potential is defined in one step: **Undo** takes it back."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:27
msgid ""
"When nothing, or more than one item, is selected, **Define potential…** is "
"greyed out and its tooltip says what to select. A potential is electrical: "
"on a fluid port (an air supply's) it is greyed out too, and the air supply's "
"own menu and its **Medium** row in Properties set the medium instead. A name "
"left empty is refused with \"Name the potential first\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:32
msgid "The supply that comes in"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:34
msgid ""
"Points marked with **Supply comes in here…** (L1, N, PE from another "
"drawing) ask for their potential as soon as they are marked: a panel at the "
"marks has a row per mark, its kind of conductor and its voltage already "
"filled in from the name where the name tells it. Correct what is wrong and "
"press Enter: each mark's potential is defined, named as the mark, in one "
"step. Escape leaves them without a potential. **Potential…** on a mark's "
"right-click menu opens the panel again later."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:40
msgid "Accept a reasoned potential"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:42
msgid ""
"A selected wire whose potential has no name yet may show a small card beside "
"it: \"Potential 0VDC?\" with the reason, such as \"-K1:A2 returns a coil fed "
"from 24VDC\". The window offers a name only where it is safe to reason: a "
"pin whose part or function definition gives its role (the − of a DC supply, "
"an N or PE pin), or the A2 of a coil whose A1 is on L+ (then A2 is the 0 V) "
"or on one phase (then A2 is the N). The name is the project's named "
"potential of that type; where there is none, the card offers a new one "
"(\"New potential 0V?\"). **Accept** defines it, as Define potential… would, "
"in one step; **Decline** hides the card for that wire until the project is "
"opened again. Nothing is set without your Accept."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:49
msgid "Check the result"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:51
msgid ""
"Open the **Potentials** navigator: the potential's row now carries the name "
"you typed, with its type and voltage. In the navigator's table view you can "
"also change them later in the **Potential**, **Type** and **Voltage** cells."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:54
msgid ""
"The command behind the form is "
"[`potential.define`](../reference/commands/potential.define.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide gives a device its part, the orderable article with its "
"manufacturer and order number, and shows where the project's parts are "
"listed. A device without a part is reported by the check EW-CORE-041 and is "
"missing from the parts list."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:10
msgid "The starter parts that come with EW, or a library of your own."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:12
msgid "Give a device its part"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:14
msgid "Click the device on the page, for example a lamp."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:15
msgid ""
"Choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**. The property grid's **Parts** row offers the "
"same in its row menu."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:16
msgid ""
"In the part selection, type a few letters of the part, such as `lamp`, "
"choose the part (EW-GEN-LMP-1 for the starter lamp) and confirm with "
"**Choose**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:19
msgid ""
"The device's **Parts** row now shows the part, and the warning EW-CORE-041 "
"for the device disappears from the Messages pane. **Project ▸ Parts** (`F9`) "
"opens the part in Parts, where a part of your own is changed and a starter "
"part is made your own."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:23
msgid "Place a device with its part"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:25
msgid ""
"When you know the part before you draw, place the device from it, so that "
"the device has its part from the start:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:27
msgid "Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** (`Shift+I`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:28
msgid ""
"In the browser, double-click the part, for example the terminal "
"EW-GEN-TRM-1; its row places the part's main function. To place another "
"function, open the part with its arrow and double-click the function."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:30
msgid "Click where the device goes on the page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:32
msgid "Read the parts list"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:34
msgid ""
"**Output ▸ Open parts list** opens the parts-list page of the project, when "
"it has one: a project made from a template of the template wizard (**Project "
"▸ New template…**), and the sample project, have one."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:36
msgid ""
"**View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Parts in use** lists the parts the project "
"uses with their devices, in any project."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:38
msgid ""
"The part data comes from the library: [Edit "
"part](../reference/window/dialogs.md#edit-part) changes the project's copy, "
"and the [glossary](../reference/glossary.md#part) relates EW's part to "
"EPLAN's article and QElectroTech's manufacturer reference."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:42
msgid "The workshop stock"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:44
msgid ""
"The workshop stock is the list of wire, ferrule and marker parts your shop "
"keeps, in the order you prefer them. A wire takes the first wire part of its "
"size and colour, a wire end the first ferrule that fits it, a label the "
"first marker made for it; the workshop lists count them from the wires."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:48
msgid ""
"Open it with **Project ▸ Edit stock…**, or with the Edit stock… link under "
"the project's Properties. Each row can move up or down or be removed; type a "
"part number and press **Add** to add a part, held or from a library. **Cut "
"to** is what the cut list rounds every rail, duct, cable and tube up to; "
"leave it empty for 5 mm. **Apply** writes the stock in one step."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:53
msgid ""
"A new project starts with the starter's generic ferrules and markers. A "
"project made before has none: **Add the generic stock** (in the dialog, or "
"Project ▸ Add the generic stock) adds those the stock lacks, after its own "
"parts."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:3
msgid ""
"A panel layout page shows an enclosure's mounting plate to scale, with its "
"rails, its wiring ducts and the devices placed on them. The devices are the "
"project's own: a device of the schematic is placed once, and the panel "
"layout, the parts list and the enclosure legend read it from there."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:7
msgid "Make the page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:9
msgid ""
"Choose **Page ▸ New page…**, set **Type** to Panel layout, give the page a "
"title such as \"Panel layout\", and choose **Create**. The page opens empty "
"and names its first step: start with an enclosure."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:12
msgid "Add the enclosure"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:14
msgid ""
"Choose **Enclosure** in the Draw tab's Layout group (also **Project ▸ Setup "
"▸ Enclosure…**). An enclosure belongs to a location (`+CAB1`), the cabinet. "
"The dialog proposes the page's location, else the project's first; in a "
"project without one it proposes **New location**, the first choice of the "
"Location list, with a code such as `CAB1` that you can change. It proposes "
"the first enclosure part of the project or of an installed library, such as "
"\"Wall enclosure 600 × 800 × 250 mm\"; choose others from the lists if you "
"like, then **Create**. The page now shows the enclosure and its mounting "
"plate, centred on the whole sheet at the scale that fits it, and the layout "
"gets its legend: a list page right after the layout page (902.1 after 902) "
"with the number, tag, quantity, part number and description of each item, "
"which runs on over as many pages as it needs. Each device shows its tag "
"where the tag fits inside it, and a numbered balloon where it does not (a "
"terminal, a small part). A new location, the enclosure, and the plate on the "
"page with its legend page are three steps of Undo. Another plate goes on the "
"page through the page's **Surface** row in Properties, or **Mounting "
"plate…** in the menu of Enclosure's arrow or the command search, which opens "
"that row whatever Properties showed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:27
msgid ""
"A location that has its enclosure already (made from another page) is not "
"made twice: with that location chosen, the dialog's button reads **Draw -UC1 "
"of +CAB1 on this page** and puts its mounting plate on the page. On a page "
"that is not a panel layout page, the button reads **Create and draw on a new "
"panel layout page** (or **Draw … on a new panel layout page**): it makes the "
"panel layout page after the page shown and draws the enclosure there. "
"**Create only** beside it makes the enclosure without drawing it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:33
msgid "Rails and ducts"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:35
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Layout ▸ Rail** and click the rail's start and end on the "
"plate (a rail runs across or up and down); the tool's card offers the rail "
"part and the grid step. Draw as many rails as the cabinet needs."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:37
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Layout ▸ Duct** and click a duct's first point and each next "
"point; a double click or `Enter` finishes it (`Esc` cancels it). The card "
"asks for a duct's size when its part gives none."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:40
msgid "Place the devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:42
msgid ""
"Show **View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Unplaced devices**. It lists the devices "
"of the page's location that have no place yet. A device without a location "
"is not listed: give it the location in Properties, under Structure, or give "
"many at once: in the Devices list, mark their rows (or their group "
"\"(none)\"), right-click and choose **Location…**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:46
msgid ""
"Drag a device from the list onto a rail, or select its row, press `Enter` "
"and click the place. On a rail it snaps to its neighbour, the rail's ends or "
"the plate grid; a device mounted by screws goes on the plate."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:48
msgid ""
"To fill a rail at once, select the rail and choose **Draw ▸ Layout ▸ "
"Auto-fill rail…**: it lists the cabinet's unplaced devices and the devices "
"without a location, each with a tick. The devices you marked in the list "
"start ticked; else the cabinet's own devices and those whose part goes on a "
"rail, while sensors, cylinders, motors and valve terminals without a "
"location start unticked. Untick what stands in the field and choose "
"**Fill**: the ticked devices are placed in the order of the schematic or of "
"their tags, a ticked device without a location takes the cabinet's (one step "
"of Undo), and a toast says what did not fit. When there is nothing to place, "
"the dialog says why."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:56
msgid "Labels and the legend"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:58
msgid ""
"The page's right-click menu (**Labels: Tags**, **Labels: Item numbers**, "
"**Labels: Both**, **Labels: Tags or numbers**) and, with nothing selected, "
"the page's **Labels** row in Properties choose what each device shows: its "
"tag, its item number in a balloon, both, or its tag where it fits and its "
"balloon where it does not (what a new layout shows). The legend lists every "
"item whatever the labels show. A legend added while the devices show their "
"tags offers **Show item numbers**. Labels that would run into each other "
"move apart, above and below the row, with a line to their device. On the "
"cabinet's own layout the tags leave out the cabinet's location."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:64
msgid ""
"A layout without a legend gets one through **Add legend** in the page's "
"right-click menu (also **Page ▸ Order ▸ Add legend**): in the free space "
"beside the layout where every row and every text fits there whole, one table "
"as wide as its block and as long as its rows; otherwise, as a toast says, on "
"its own page after the layout page. Click a legend beside the layout to "
"select it, drag it to move it, and drag one of its corners to make it wider "
"or narrower. **Move the legend to its own page** in its right-click menu "
"(also **Page ▸ Order**) makes such a legend, or one of a project made "
"before, the layout's legend page, in one step of Undo. The legend page's "
"columns and caption are chosen as any list's: **Output ▸ List columns…** on "
"its page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:71
msgid ""
"**Follow reference** (`F`) over a device, its tag or its balloon, or with "
"the device selected, goes to its row on the legend page; a click on a row's "
"tag goes back to the device on the layout, in an exported PDF too."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:73
msgid ""
"A label or balloon can be dragged on its own: one beside its device at once, "
"one on its device once the device is selected (the label then shows a dashed "
"box with a handle). Its line follows it to the device. **Reset label "
"position** in the device's right-click menu puts it back; **Reset label "
"positions** in the page's right-click menu puts every label of the page back."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:78
msgid ""
"The tools are described one by one in the [tool "
"reference](../reference/window/tools.md): Place device, Rail and Duct."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide is where you look up one PLC job. To learn the jobs in order, "
"follow the tutorials [A PLC with its modules](../tutorials/plc-station.md) "
"and [A bus network](../tutorials/bus-network.md) first."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:6
msgid ""
"EW keeps the PLC hardware of a project beside its drawings: each CPU and "
"remote I/O head with the modules in its rack, every channel with its "
"address, symbol and what it is wired to, and the bus networks between them. "
"You draw the modules on schematic pages as boxes with their pins and wire "
"the field devices to them, as in any ECAD tool; the PLC side knows which "
"channel each pin is and exports it all to TIA Portal or CODESYS."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:11
msgid "The PLC workspace shows the PLC side beside the page:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:13
msgid ""
"![The PLC workspace: the PLC panel at the left, the page, the PLC grid under "
"it, the PLC tab](images/plc-workspace.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:15
msgid ""
"the **PLC** tab of the command bar at the top, with a button for each PLC "
"job;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:16
msgid ""
"the **PLC panel** at the left: each PLC with its stations, their modules and "
"each module's channels;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:17 src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:18
msgid "**Properties** under it, for what is selected;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:18
msgid "the page in the middle;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:19
msgid ""
"the **PLC grid** under the page: one row per channel, edited like a "
"spreadsheet."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:21 src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:22
msgid "The words"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:23 src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:24
msgid "Word"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:23 src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:24
msgid "What it is"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:25 src/reference/window/command-bar.md:143 src/reference/window/panels.md:111
msgid "PLC"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:25
msgid ""
"One controller's configuration, shown as PLC1 in the PLC panel: what a CPU "
"and its remote stations share."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:26
msgid "Station"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:26
msgid ""
"A CPU or a remote I/O head with the modules it carries; a valve terminal "
"with a bus node is one too. TIA Portal calls it a device in its network view."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:27
msgid "Rack"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:27
msgid "The row of slots of a station."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:28
msgid "Slot"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:28
msgid ""
"A place in the rack, numbered from 1. The slot order gives the addresses."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:29
msgid "Module"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:29
msgid "An I/O card in a slot (the window says module)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:30
msgid "Channel"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:30
msgid ""
"One input or output of a module, such as I0.0: one row of the PLC grid and "
"one pin of the module's box."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:31 src/reference/window/tools.md:60 src/reference/symbol-editor.md:267
msgid "Symbol"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:31
msgid ""
"The PLC tag name of a channel, such as `Door_closed`, as in the PLC tag "
"table. The drawing symbol of a device is another thing."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:33
msgid "In TIA Portal you … here you …"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:35
msgid "In TIA Portal you …"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:35
msgid "Here you …"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:37
msgid "add a CPU in the device view"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:37
msgid ""
"choose **New station** on the PLC tab and double-click its head part in the "
"catalogue"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:38
msgid "open the device view of a station"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:38
msgid ""
"choose **Rack** on the PLC tab, or double-click the station in the PLC "
"panel: its rack opens in a tab"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:39
msgid "plug modules into the rack from the catalogue"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:39
msgid ""
"double-click a module in the rack's **Catalogue**, or drag it onto a slot"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:40
msgid "move a module to another slot"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:40
msgid ""
"drag its card to the slot, or choose **Move left** or **Move right** above "
"the rack"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:41
msgid "read the I/O addresses in the device overview"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:41
msgid "read the **Addresses** column of the station's rack, or the PLC grid"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:42
msgid "change a start address"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:42
msgid "type the address in the PLC grid, or choose **Re-address…**"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:43
msgid "type the PLC tags"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:43
msgid "type each channel's **Symbol** in the PLC grid"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:44
msgid "add an IO device in the network view"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:44
msgid "place its part on a bus-topology page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:45
msgid "connect it to the subnet"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:45
msgid "draw a bus line between the sockets with **Draw ▸ Wire**"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:46
msgid "assign an IO device to a controller"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:46
msgid "right-click it and choose **Add to** and the PLC"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:47
msgid "set the PROFINET device name and IP address"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:47
msgid "type them into the **Address** cell of the Bus panel's table"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:49
msgid ""
"The exchange of the hardware and the tags with the PLC software is at the "
"end of this guide."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:51 src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:32
msgid "Open the workspace"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:53
msgid ""
"Choose **PLC** in the workspace switch at the right of the tab row, or **PLC "
"workspace** on the PLC tab."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:54 src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:35
msgid ""
"**Schematic** in the switch goes back. A change you make to the layout is "
"kept for the workspace."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:56
msgid "Make a station"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:58
msgid ""
"Choose **New station** on the PLC tab (**PLC ▸ New station…**), or **+** in "
"the PLC panel. A tab **New station** opens beside the page tabs; the rest of "
"the window keeps working."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:60
msgid ""
"Choose what it is: **PLC (CPU)**, **Remote I/O**, **Valve terminal** or "
"**Compact device** (a hover says each in full), and the PLC it belongs to (a "
"remote station: the PLC it serves), or **New PLC**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:62
msgid ""
"Double-click its head in the **Catalogue** at the right: the CPU, the "
"interface module of a remote station, the valve terminal. **New part…** "
"under the catalogue opens Parts on a new part of that kind; the part you "
"save is in the catalogue when you come back, and it is the head when you "
"clicked the empty **Head** first."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:65
msgid ""
"Double-click each module in the catalogue: it goes into the next free slot. "
"A click on a card selects it: **Remove**, **Move left** and **Move right** "
"above the rack change it before it is made."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:67
msgid ""
"Choose **Create**: the station and its modules are made in one step, drawn "
"on no page yet. Nothing is written before; closing the tab before Create "
"asks first."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:69
msgid ""
"**To do** under the table says what is left, one line for each side of the "
"station that is on no page of its kind yet: its boxes on an electrical page, "
"its bus ports on a bus page, a valve terminal's valves on their positions on "
"a fluid page. **Draw each card on a page of its own** makes a new page for "
"each module in the section of the schematic pages, after the page the view "
"was opened from when that page stands there, titled from the module (**PLC 8 "
"DI**), the station's head on the first, in one Undo step; **All on one "
"page…** beside it lists the electrical pages and **New page…**, and the one "
"you pick takes them all. Each other button goes to the page the view was "
"opened from when it is of that kind (**Put it on page 101**), to a new page "
"when the project has none, else lists the pages of that kind and **New "
"page…**. The station and its modules are drawn in slot order, each input or "
"output module as a low, wide box with its channels' pins in one row: input "
"modules along the foot of the page with their pins on the upper edge, output "
"modules at the head with their pins on the lower edge; the bus ports and a "
"valve terminal's box hang on the pointer, and a click puts them down."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:81
msgid ""
"Click the station's tag in the header, or press `F2`, to change it in place; "
"`Enter` takes it, `Esc` leaves it. `F2` on a card, or a click on the tag of "
"the card under the cursor, changes the card's tag in its row. A tag another "
"device has is refused under the rack; a change that moves the cards' tags "
"too waits for **Apply** in Properties. The PLC's own name, **Belongs to PLC: "
"…** at the header's right, changes the same way: click it; a name another "
"PLC of the project has is refused."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:87
msgid ""
"A part of a station that no page draws yet has **Place here** on its row in "
"the PLC panel and on its card's menu: on an electrical page its box hangs on "
"the pointer, on a bus page its bus ports, on a fluid page a valve terminal's "
"positions. On another page the entry is greyed with the page to open."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:91
msgid ""
"A station part placed straight from the device browser opens its rack in a "
"tab at once; `Esc` goes back to the page, and the tab stays beside it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:94
msgid "Read and change a rack"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:96
msgid ""
"Right-click the station (on the page, in the PLC panel) and choose "
"**Rack…**, choose **Rack** on the PLC tab, or double-click the station in "
"the PLC panel. Its rack opens in a tab beside the page tabs:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:99
msgid ""
"![A station's rack in its tab: the cards, To do, the table, the channels of "
"the selected module, and the catalogue](images/plc-rack.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:101
msgid "**The buttons above the rack** act on the selected module."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:102
msgid ""
"**The rack, drawn**: one card per place, the head first, then each slot. A "
"card says what the module gives (8 DI), its tag, its part number and the "
"page it is drawn on; a free slot is an outline with a plus. A station whose "
"part takes no modules, such as a valve terminal of valve positions alone, "
"has no slots: its positions follow the head."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:105
msgid ""
"**To do**, under the rack, says what is wrong or left, each in a sentence "
"with its fix beside it: a module in a slot its part does not allow, a module "
"drawn on no page, a station on no bus page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:107
msgid ""
"**The table** under it lists the same places: slot, module, part (number and "
"description), what it gives, its addresses and the page it is drawn on. A "
"module without a part says **No part**, with **Choose part…** beside it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:109
msgid ""
"**Channels** lists the channels of the selected module: address, symbol "
"name, function text (the wired device's, as the PLC overview prints it) and "
"what is wired to it, which is a link to its place on its page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:111
msgid ""
"**The catalogue** at the right lists the parts that fit the station, by "
"kind, under a filter. Parts that do not fit the selected slot are greyed and "
"say why, as is a library part that nothing can draw yet because it has no "
"pins, with what to do in Parts."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:115
msgid ""
"With **Side by side**, a station's view in the other group shows the same "
"rack, To do, table and channels; where the room is short it leaves out the "
"catalogue until you click in it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:118
msgid "To change it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:120
msgid ""
"Click a card or a row: the module is selected as a click on a page selects a "
"device. Properties shows it, and the buttons above the rack act on it: "
"**Replace** (the parts that fit its slot), **Remove**, **Draw on page**, "
"**Move to page** (a drawn card's box onto another schematic page, the same "
"card; a wire to something left behind becomes a pair of interruption points, "
"which a preview lists first), **Go to page**, **Move left** and **Move "
"right**. A right click gives the same commands. `Delete` empties the slot, "
"and the arrow keys go from slot to slot."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:125
msgid ""
"Double-click a part in the catalogue to put it into the selected free slot, "
"else into the first free one; or drag it onto a slot. Dropped on a module, "
"it replaces that module's part."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:127
msgid ""
"Drag a card onto a free slot to move it there, or onto another module to "
"make the two change places. The addresses follow, and a line under the rack "
"says so."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:129
msgid ""
"A slot the module's part does not allow is refused: the slot is outlined in "
"red and the reason stands under the rack."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:131
msgid ""
"A valve terminal's positions are places like slots: select one and "
"double-click a valve in the catalogue, or **Remove** to empty it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:134
msgid "Every step is one Undo."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:136
msgid "Put a module into a station"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:138
msgid ""
"A module drawn on a page by itself (from the device browser or the black "
"box) joins the project's only station, and its toast names the slot it got, "
"with **Rack…**, which opens the station's rack."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:140
msgid ""
"With two stations it joins the one drawn beside it on its page; on a page "
"without one, a window asks which station takes it. Without any station, its "
"toast offers **New station…**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:142
msgid ""
"A module that waits is listed under **Modules without a station** in the PLC "
"panel: drag its row onto the station's row, or right-click the station and "
"choose **Attach modules without a station**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:145
msgid ""
"Only a module goes into a rack: a device whose part has no PLC data is "
"refused in words."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:147
msgid "Wire a field device to a channel"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:149
msgid ""
"Draw the device (a sensor, a lamp, a coil) on the page beside the module's "
"box."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:150
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`) and wire it to the module's pin: pin 1 is "
"channel 0."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:151
msgid ""
"Or drag a channel's row from the PLC panel onto the device's connection "
"point: the device is attached to the module and the channel bound to it in "
"one step, which one Undo takes back."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:154
msgid "Addresses"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:156
msgid ""
"A channel's address comes from its module's slot and the modules before it: "
"the first input module of a station starts at I0.0, the first output module "
"at Q0.0."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:158
msgid ""
"To give one channel another address, type it into its **Address** cell in "
"the PLC grid; it keeps it, with the badge **manual** in the panel and a lock "
"in the grid."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:160
msgid ""
"Right-click a module and choose **Re-address…** (or the PLC row for all its "
"modules): the Preview tab lists each channel whose address moves, old and "
"new; **Apply** makes the change in one step."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:162
msgid ""
"Right-click the PLC row and choose **Freeze addresses…** to keep every used "
"or reserved channel's address when modules move later, or **Make manual "
"addresses automatic…** to drop manual addresses that equal the automatic "
"ones. The three are also under **Addresses** on the PLC tab."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:166
msgid "The PLC grid and the symbols"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:168
msgid ""
"Choose **PLC grid** on the PLC tab (**PLC ▸ PLC grid**). Each row is a "
"channel."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:169
msgid ""
"Click a cell and type: the designation, address, symbol, state, target, "
"comment, data type or signal range. The slot cell takes a number or "
"`<rack>.<slot>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:171
msgid ""
"Type into the grid's filter to narrow the rows: a word of a cell, or one of "
"`spare`, `unplaced`, `unassigned`, `tbd`, `duplicate`, `safety`, "
"`config:<name>` and `bus:<net>` (`bus:profibus-dp`). A click on a column's "
"header sorts by it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:174
msgid ""
"To have EW write the symbols, right-click the PLC row and choose **Symbol "
"policy…**: the template of the automatic symbol, the PLC tag table and "
"whether symbols must be strict IEC 61131-3 names."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:177
msgid "Give a bus device to a PLC"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:179
msgid ""
"On a bus-topology page, a schematic page or in the Bus panel, right-click a "
"bus device that belongs to no PLC."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:180
msgid ""
"Choose **Add to** and the PLC, or **Add to a new PLC**: it becomes a station "
"of that PLC and its rack opens in a tab."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:183
msgid ""
"A switch or a plug takes no part in a PLC; its menu says why. A station's "
"menu says **PLC:** and its PLC's name."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:185
msgid "Bus ports and nets"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:187
msgid ""
"A bus-topology page is the network view: each device stands as one box per "
"bus interface (PROFINET X1, PROFIBUS DP), with a connection point per "
"socket, and the bus lines run between the sockets. The device's power and "
"I/O pins stay on the schematic pages."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:191
msgid ""
"Choose **New bus page** on the PLC tab: the new-page form opens with the "
"type **Bus topology**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:192
msgid ""
"Place a station or another bus device from the device browser (**Draw ▸ "
"Place ▸ Device from part…**) with two clicks for its box: it stands there as "
"a box per interface, and a station's rack opens in a tab (`Esc` goes back to "
"the page)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:195
msgid ""
"A station or bus device the project has already (drawn on a schematic page, "
"or made with **New station**) goes onto the bus page with **Put a station on "
"this page…**: right-click an empty spot of the page, or open the list of "
"**Place port** on the PLC tab, and choose it. It stands there as a box per "
"interface. Dragging its row from the PLC panel, the Bus panel or the Devices "
"panel onto the page does the same at the drop point. Placing its part from "
"the device browser, or choosing the Black box tool, on the bus page asks "
"first: **Put -1A1 here** or **A new station**. One port box comes from "
"**Place port DP** (or **Place port X1**) on the station's row in the PLC "
"panel; **Ports not drawn yet** in the Bus panel lists every interface no "
"page draws yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:202
msgid ""
"Draw the bus lines with **Draw ▸ Wire**: the ports become one network, named "
"for its bus, with the CPU's port as its controller or master."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:204
msgid ""
"Choose **Bus panel** on the PLC tab and switch it to its table. Type each "
"node's address into its **Address** cell as the cell says: a PROFIBUS DP "
"station address such as `3`, a PROFINET device name with its IP address such "
"as `plc1 192.168.0.1`. The box prints it. An unmanaged switch passes the net "
"through and takes none."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:207
msgid ""
"Select a bus line and choose **Cable** on the Draw tab: choose the cable "
"(the ones made for that bus first), its length and the plug at each end with "
"its termination, and **Apply**. The line prints the cable's tag; the cable "
"and the plugs go into the parts list."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:211
msgid ""
"The starter parts hold a CPU with PROFINET and PROFIBUS DP, a remote I/O "
"head, an unmanaged and a managed switch, a PROFINET device, PROFIBUS and "
"Ethernet cables and their plugs. Your own devices with their ports are made "
"in Parts: see [Make your own parts](own-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:215
msgid "Exchange with the PLC software"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:217
msgid ""
"Choose **Export to PLC** on the PLC tab (**PLC ▸ Export to PLC software…**) "
"and pick the format: **AutomationML AR APC 1.4.0** (or 1.2.0) carries the "
"stations, their modules, addresses and symbols to TIA Portal; **TIA Portal "
"PLC tags (XLSX)** carries the tag table; **PLCopen XML (CODESYS)** is for "
"CODESYS. Choose **Export**, then the file."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:220
msgid ""
"Choose **Import from PLC** on the PLC tab (**PLC ▸ Import from PLC "
"software…**) for the file the PLC software wrote back. Look through its "
"tabs: each conflict shows the side **Apply** takes, and offers the file's "
"value where it can be taken. Choose **Apply**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:223
msgid ""
"After an import, what the PLC software owns (the module starts, the symbols "
"or the PLC data) is protected: right-click the PLC row and choose **Unlock "
"module starts** (or the other group) to edit it here again."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:225
msgid ""
"Right-click the PLC row and choose **Import instrument list…** for a list of "
"process tags and signal types (CSV or workbook): the Preview tab lists the "
"spare channels it reserves."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:228
msgid "Settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:230
msgid "**Process-tag scheme** on the PLC tab: the pattern process tags follow."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:231
msgid ""
"**Addresses at terminals** on the PLC tab: each channel's address printed at "
"the terminals on its path."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:232
msgid ""
"Right-click a module and choose **Safety data…** for its F-addresses and "
"F-watchdog time; right-click a channel and choose **Mode and safety…** for a "
"multi-function port's mode and the channel's safety data."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/plc-workspace.md:235
msgid ""
"The panels are described in the [panel "
"reference](../reference/window/panels.md) (PLC, PLC grid, Bus), and the "
"dialogs in the [dialog reference](../reference/window/dialogs.md) (PLC "
"import)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:3
msgid ""
"Every part, the starter parts and your own, lives in one place: **Parts** "
"(**Project ▸ Parts**, `F9`). On the left is the list with its search and its "
"filters, in the middle the open part's sheet, on the right its pictures and "
"**Ready for**. A part you make is typed from its data sheet: nothing here "
"depends on what ElektraWatt knows about a manufacturer's product. **Save** "
"puts it into the project and into your own parts library, **My parts**, so "
"that your next project has it too."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:9
msgid "You need an open project."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:11
msgid "Start a new part"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:13
msgid ""
"Choose **New part** in the bar of Parts, or **New part…** in the device "
"browser (**Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…**, `Shift+I`) or in the part "
"dialog (**Choose part…**)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:15
msgid ""
"The sheet asks first: **What is it?** Its cards stand in four groups, "
"Devices; Terminals, cables and wiring; Cabinet; Pneumatics, each with a line "
"of what it will ask for. Choose the card of your part: **Device with "
"symbols** for a contactor or a breaker, **Device drawn as a box** for a "
"power supply or a device on a bus, **PLC card or station**, **Pushbutton or "
"lamp in a door**, **Terminal**, **Cable**, **Wire**, **Ferrule or lug**, "
"**Marker**, **Cable gland**, **Enclosure**, **Mounting plate**, **Rail**, "
"**Wiring duct**, **End bracket or accessory**, **Valve**, **Tube** or "
"**Other pneumatic device**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:21
msgid ""
"The sheet shows what that kind needs and nothing else. Type the **Maker**, "
"the **Part number** and a **Description**: these are all a part needs to be "
"saved. A maker your libraries do not have yet is made with the part."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:24
msgid ""
"Type what the data sheet gives: a terminal's clamps and width, a cable's "
"cores and outer diameter, a cabinet's size, plate and doors. **Ready for** "
"on the right says, per feature, what the part still lacks and what follows "
"from it (for the cabinet layout, a part without its size stands on the "
"layout as a 20 × 20 mm square marked unknown size); a group of the sheet "
"that lacks something has a dot in the list of groups. Leave what you do not "
"know: the part saves without it, and Ready for keeps saying it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:29
msgid ""
"**Save**. From the part dialog the button reads **Save and use for -102Q1**: "
"the part goes to the devices you were choosing for, and you are back on the "
"page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:32
msgid ""
"A field that does not read is refused under it, in words. Save is refused "
"only when the part has no maker or no number, or when My parts or the "
"project has a part of that maker and number already: open that one from the "
"list instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:35
msgid ""
"**A part like it…**, the card under the others, or **Like this** in the bar "
"with a part open, starts a new part with everything the other part has but "
"its number. A cable type sold in several sizes has **Save and add another "
"size** beside Save: the next size opens with everything but the number and "
"the cross-section."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:39
msgid "A card such as a digital input card"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:41
msgid ""
"Choose **PLC card or station**. Under **PLC**, the role is **Module**; "
"choose **Channel kind ▸ DI** and type **Channels** `8`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:43
msgid ""
"The **Pin pattern** gives the pin of channel n: `{n+1}` gives the pins 1 to "
"8, `X2:{n+1}` gives X2:1 to X2:8. The line under it shows the first and the "
"last pin (\"Channel 0 → pin 1 … channel 7 → pin 8\"), so that a wrong "
"pattern shows before you save, and the next line the input or output bits "
"the card occupies."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:46
msgid ""
"Type the supply pins (**Supply L+ pin**, **Supply M pin**) when the card has "
"them, and the **Allowed slots** (`1-8, 10`) when the data sheet restricts "
"them. **Save**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:48
msgid ""
"Place the card beside the CPU from the device browser: it stands as a box "
"with its pins, joins the project's CPU, and its channels are addressed and "
"bound to their pins. The catalogue of a station's rack (**Rack…**) offers it "
"as a module."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:52
msgid ""
"A part has channels of one kind: a card with 16 inputs and 16 outputs is two "
"parts, one for each."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:54
msgid "A head station such as an IM 152"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:56
msgid ""
"Choose **PLC card or station**, then **PLC role ▸ Station**; type the supply "
"pins and **Most modules** (`32`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:57
msgid ""
"Under **Network ports**, choose **Add interface**: type the interface `DP`, "
"choose **Bus ▸ PROFIBUS DP** and **Role ▸ Slave**. Its PROFINET sister has "
"the interface `X1`, **Bus ▸ PROFINET**, **Role ▸ Device** and **Ports** `2`. "
"**Save**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:60
msgid ""
"Place it from the device browser on a bus-topology page: it stands there as "
"a box with its DP socket, and its supply pins wait for a schematic page "
"(drag its unplaced function from the **Devices** list there, and draw the "
"box with its pins). Placed first on a schematic page, it gets its port box "
"on a bus-topology page from **Place port DP** on its row in the PLC "
"navigator. Add its cards in its rack (**Rack…** on that row). A station that "
"only answers on the bus is a head station."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:66
msgid ""
"**New part…** at the foot of a station's catalogue starts the part the rack "
"asks for: a CPU, a remote station, a valve terminal, a compact device or a "
"module."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:69
msgid "A switch such as a SCALANCE, or a device on PROFINET"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:71
msgid ""
"Choose **Device drawn as a box**. Under **Network ports**, **Add "
"interface**: `X1`, **PROFINET**, and the number of **Ports** (sockets) of "
"the data sheet."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:73
msgid ""
"An **unmanaged** switch has the role **Passive**: no address, it passes the "
"net through."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:74
msgid ""
"A **managed** switch and a drive, an HMI or a gateway have the role "
"**Device**: one PROFINET name and IP address for the whole interface, "
"however many sockets it has."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:76
msgid ""
"**Save**, then place it from the device browser on a bus-topology page: it "
"stands as a box with a connection point per socket, P1, P2 …. A device with "
"several interfaces, such as the starter CPU with DP and X1, stands as a box "
"per interface, side by side. Draw the bus lines with **Draw ▸ Wire**: the "
"CPU, the devices and the managed switch are one network, and an unmanaged "
"switch stands in it with \"passes the net through\" in the **Bus** "
"navigator. Type each node's name in the navigator's table (its **Address** "
"column): the box prints it (\"Node switch-1 192.168.0.2\"), and \"Node TBD\" "
"while it is open."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:84
msgid ""
"The starter parts hold such parts to begin with: EW-GEN-ESW-5 (unmanaged "
"switch, 5 ports), EW-GEN-ESW-8 (managed switch, 8 ports) and EW-GEN-PND-1 (a "
"PROFINET device, 2 ports)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:87
msgid "Change a part"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:89
msgid ""
"Open the part in Parts: from the list (type a part of its number, maker or "
"description), or select a device and choose **Edit ▸ Parts**, which opens on "
"the device's part."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:91
msgid ""
"A part of your own, in My parts or only in this project, opens ready to "
"change. A starter part or a part of another library is not yours to change: "
"its fields show what it is, and **Make my own** starts a part of your own "
"with its data, which you save under a part number of your own."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:94
msgid ""
"Change what you need and **Save**. A part of My parts is saved there as its "
"next version, and the card beside the sheet lists what changes in words "
"(\"Interface X1: 8 ports → 6 ports\", \"Channels: 8 → 16\", the parts list "
"row's changed column) and the devices that use it; **Apply** brings the "
"project's copy to it. A part only in this project changes in place, with the "
"same card. **Discard** drops what you typed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:98
msgid ""
"A device already drawn follows the correction: its box shows the pins and "
"sockets the part still has, and its bus port keeps its bus, role and "
"address. A bus line at a socket the part no longer has keeps its end there; "
"the card names it before **Apply**, so that you draw it to another socket. A "
"pin or socket the part adds stands after the last one of its edge, the box "
"growing to take it; a box that would then run past the drawing area goes "
"back along that edge until it fits, its wires with it. The card names each "
"box drawn anew and its page before **Apply**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:104
msgid ""
"A part only in this project has **Keep in my parts** in the bar: it is "
"written into My parts, and the project's devices take that copy. **Remove** "
"takes a part of your own out of My parts (it asks first); a project that "
"uses it keeps its copy. **Back** returns to where you came from."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:108
msgid "A device that is a box with pins"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:110
msgid ""
"A circuit protector, a power supply, a drive: a device the program has no "
"symbol for is drawn as a box with its pins on the edges. The part says which "
"pins it has and where they stand."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:113
msgid ""
"Under **On the schematic**, **Pins** lists the part's pins, each with its "
"name and the potential it carries. **Add pin** adds one, the cross at a "
"row's end removes it; each pin has its row under **Connections** for its "
"clamp. A PLC card's pins come from its channels and a part drawn with a "
"symbol takes that symbol's connections: the group lists them and says so."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:117
msgid ""
"Choose **Pins and box…**. The device box opens on the symbol editor's "
"screen: drag each pin along its edge or onto another edge, drag the box's "
"right or bottom edge to size it, and choose **Save** ([The device "
"box](../reference/symbol-editor.md#the-device-box))."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:120
msgid ""
"**Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** then places the part's box with one "
"click, laid out as you drew it. On the page the box's edges and pins can "
"still be dragged. When you drag an edge, the pins of each side stay centred "
"on it with their spacing; the box goes no smaller than its pins need (one "
"grid step each and one to spare at each corner), and no lower than its pin "
"names need, and the status line says so. **Centre the pins** in the box's "
"right-click menu centres them without a resize. The pin names of the top and "
"bottom sides are turned a quarter, reading from bottom to top; those of the "
"left and right sides are horizontal."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:126
msgid ""
"To change the box of a device already drawn, choose **Edit this device's "
"box…** in its menu. The message of the save says how many boxes of the part "
"are drawn and offers **Redraw them**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:129
msgid "A part drawn with your own symbol"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:131
msgid ""
"In the [symbol editor](../reference/symbol-editor.md), save the symbol with "
"**Save**, then open **Draft** and choose **Make a part for this symbol…**. "
"Parts opens on a new part drawn with that symbol; a device placed from the "
"part is drawn with it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/own-parts.md:135
msgid ""
"The PLC exchange with PLC software exports the stations of a configuration "
"project; a switch or a device without a PLC section is not in the exported "
"file. See [Work in the PLC workspace](plc-workspace.md) for the navigators, "
"the rack editor and the exchange."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide is where you look up one pneumatics job. To learn the jobs in "
"order, follow the tutorial [A pneumatic "
"circuit](../tutorials/pneumatic-circuit.md) first."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:6
msgid ""
"EW draws pneumatic circuits on fluid pages with the ISO 1219 symbols, beside "
"the electrical pages of the same project. It knows which medium each tube "
"carries, which tube runs from which port to which, and which valve each "
"solenoid belongs to, so the solenoid drawn on an electrical page and the "
"valve on the fluid page are one device, and the tube list is made from the "
"drawing."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:11
msgid "The Fluid workspace shows the pneumatics beside the page:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:13
msgid ""
"![The Fluid workspace: the Fluid panel, the fluid page, the tube and medium "
"tables, the Fluid tab](images/fluid-workspace.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:15
msgid ""
"the **Fluid** tab of the command bar at the top, with a button for each "
"fluid job;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:16
msgid ""
"the **Fluid panel** at the left: the valve terminals with their valves, "
"every device a tube ends at with its tubes, and the media;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:19
msgid "the fluid page in the middle;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:20
msgid "the **Tube table** and the **Medium table** under the page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:26
msgid "Fluid page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:26
msgid ""
"A page for the circuit: the symbols are ISO 1219 and the Wire tool draws "
"tubes."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:27
msgid "Medium"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:27
msgid ""
"What the tubes carry, such as compressed air at 6 bar. It is defined at the "
"air supply and reaches every tube the supply feeds through the valves."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:28 src/reference/window/tools.md:196
msgid "Tube"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:28
msgid ""
"A line between two ports, with its line kind (working, pilot, exhaust, "
"drain, flexible), size and material."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:29
msgid "Solenoid"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:29
msgid ""
"The coil of a valve. It belongs to the valve and is drawn on an electrical "
"page, where it is wired."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:30
msgid "Valve terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:30
msgid ""
"A manifold of valve positions, drawn as its positions; a position holds a "
"valve or a blanking plate."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:34
msgid ""
"Choose **Fluid power** in the workspace switch at the right of the tab row, "
"or **Fluid workspace** on the Fluid tab."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:37
msgid "Draw a circuit"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:39
msgid ""
"Choose **Fluid page** on the Fluid tab: the new-page form opens with the "
"type **Fluid**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:40
msgid ""
"Choose **Air supply** on the Fluid tab for the supply point and its "
"preparation (service unit, filter, regulator, lubricator), or **Fluid "
"symbol** for a valve, cylinder, flow control or any other pneumatic part: "
"both open the device browser, so what you place has its part. A bare ISO "
"1219 symbol without a part is **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:43
msgid ""
"A symbol placed with a port facing a free port is joined to it with a tube; "
"hold `Alt` as you click to place it without one. `R` before the click turns "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:45
msgid ""
"Choose **Tube** on the Fluid tab and click from port to port. The bar under "
"the page says the line kind and the project's tube, such as \"Working line · "
"tube 6/4 mm\"; its **Line kind** button changes the kind for the next tubes. "
"**Draw ▸ Wire** draws working lines."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:48
msgid ""
"Right-click a tube to change its line kind (**Line kind: Working**, **Line "
"kind: Pilot** and the others) or its medium. A flexible tube is drawn as an "
"arc."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:51
msgid "The medium"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:53
msgid ""
"Choose **New medium** on the Fluid tab (**Fluid ▸ New medium…**). Under "
"**Start from** pick a usual medium, such as **Compressed air** (which fills "
"the name and 6 bar), or type a name and the pressure in bar, and choose "
"**Create**. Each field says under it what it changes."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:56
msgid ""
"Select the air supply and choose the medium in its **Medium** row in "
"Properties, or right-click it and choose **Medium:** and the medium. While "
"the project has no medium yet, the list offers the usual ones, such as "
"**Compressed air, 6 bar**: choosing one makes it and sets it in one step."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:59
msgid ""
"Every tube the supply feeds through the valves carries it; a device's "
"Properties show the medium each port carries, and say where to set it when "
"it carries none."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:61
msgid ""
"Select a medium's row in the Medium table and press `Ctrl+Shift+H` to "
"highlight its zones on the page, in a colour with a hatch pattern."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:64
msgid "The solenoid of a valve"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:66
msgid ""
"Select a solenoid valve on its fluid page. Its Properties list each "
"solenoid, such as **Solenoid 14**, with where it is drawn and the PLC output "
"that drives it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:68
msgid ""
"Click \"draw it…\" in that row, or right-click the valve and choose **Draw "
"solenoid 14 on an electrical page…**: the window asks on which electrical "
"page (or **New page…**), and that page shows with the coil on the pointer; "
"click where it goes. The coil carries the valve's tag. With an electrical "
"page in front, the entry reads **Draw solenoid 14 on this page…** and draws "
"there; the coil's row in the Devices list offers it too, with **Go to the "
"valve**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:72
msgid ""
"Wire the coil to a PLC output. The valve's menu then reads **Go to solenoid "
"14 at** and the place; going there selects the coil and names it beside it. "
"From the coil, **Solenoid 14 of -201Q1**, or **Follow reference** (`F`) with "
"the coil selected, goes back to the valve."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:75
msgid ""
"A coil placed on its own joins a valve when you type the valve's tag into "
"the coil's **Tag**: the field asks whether to make it the valve's undrawn "
"solenoid."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:78
msgid "Valve terminals"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:80
msgid ""
"There are two kinds, and its Properties, its menu and its row in the Devices "
"list say which (\"Valve terminal · PLC station on PROFIBUS DP\", \"Valve "
"terminal · multipin, wired\"):"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:83
msgid ""
"a **valve terminal with bus node** is a PLC station on the bus: its valves "
"are driven inside it, each by the output its position gives, and nothing is "
"wired per solenoid;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:85
msgid ""
"a **multipin valve terminal** has a multipin plug: each solenoid is wired "
"from a PLC output to a pin of the plug."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:87
msgid ""
"Choose **Valve terminal** on the Fluid tab and choose the terminal, such as "
"**Valve terminal with PROFIBUS DP bus node, 4 valve positions, 8 outputs**; "
"click once where its top-left corner goes. It is drawn as its positions: a "
"supply end, then one numbered cell per position. An empty position is a "
"dashed cell, a blanking plate a plate with both diagonals. The supply end "
"holds the terminal's supply port 1 and exhaust ports 3 and 5 on its bottom "
"edge: draw the air to port 1 there. The sub-base under the cells shows the "
"supply and exhaust galleries that join every valve. The toast names the "
"terminal and its positions; a click onto another box is refused. A terminal "
"made first as a PLC station (**PLC ▸ New station…**) comes first: **Valve "
"terminal** offers **Draw -104A1 here** for it, its rack's tab **Draw on a "
"fluid page…**, and its valves stand in their cells. With several fluid pages "
"**Draw on a fluid page…** asks which; without one it makes one. **Valve "
"terminal**, **Air supply** and **Fluid symbol** work from any page in the "
"same way: they go to the nearest fluid page, or make one."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:97
msgid ""
"Click an empty cell, or right-click it and choose **Fill position 2…**: the "
"device browser opens with valves; choose a valve for a valve terminal, and "
"it stands in that cell with its output. Its working ports 4 and 2 stand on "
"the cell's top edge: draw the tubes to the cylinder from there. A valve put "
"on a position in the terminal's rack or the PLC list stands in its cell the "
"same way. A valve wider than a cell, such as a 5/3 valve, widens its cell: "
"the terminal grows to the right, and the valves beside it move with their "
"tubes. A valve without a symbol is not offered, with the reason. A valve "
"drawn on another page stays there, and its cell names the place, a link to "
"it. In a project made before that shows valves only by their tags, "
"right-click the terminal and choose **Draw its valves in the valve "
"terminal**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:105
msgid ""
"Right-click a held cell for **Replace at position 2…**, **Empty position 2** "
"and **Blanking plate at position 2** (an empty cell's device browser lists "
"**Blanking plate** first; the check's warning on an empty position offers it "
"as its fix, and **Fix all** blanks every empty position at once); "
"right-click the terminal's body for every position, each under a heading "
"such as **Position 2: empty**. A valve dragged onto another cell moves "
"there; onto a held cell the two swap. A valve's **Valve position** in "
"Properties moves it too. A valve whose working ports 2 and 4 lead nowhere is "
"plugged with **Plug ports** on the Fluid tab (the selected valves), with the "
"check's fix **Plug the open ports**, or one port at a time from its menu; "
"**Set spare** on a valve's menu plugs its open ports with it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:113
msgid ""
"A valve for a valve terminal dropped onto a cell takes that position on "
"either kind of terminal, and the toast says which. A stand-alone valve, a "
"coil or a cylinder dropped into the box is not taken as a position: it stays "
"a device of its own, and the toast says so."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:116
msgid ""
"On a multipin terminal, right-click it on any page and choose **Draw the "
"plug of -201K1 on an electrical page…** (or **Draw plug** on the Fluid tab, "
"or the **Plug** row in its Properties): the window asks on which electrical "
"page, and that page shows with the plug on the pointer; click once where it "
"goes. With an electrical page in front it is drawn there. Its pins stand "
"down both sides, and beside each pin stands the valve and solenoid it "
"drives. Wire a pin to a PLC output, and the valve on the fluid page shows "
"that output."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:121
msgid ""
"<a id=\"the-positions-of-a-valve-terminal\"></a>The terminal's rack "
"(**Rack…**, a tab beside the page tabs) shows its positions as places like "
"slots: select one and double-click a valve in the catalogue; its right-click "
"menu offers **Replace valve**, **Empty the position** and **Blanking "
"plate**. **Generate overview…** on the terminal's menu makes the overview "
"pages of its modules and valves, previewed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:126
msgid "Tubes and the tube list"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:128
msgid ""
"Choose **Fluid settings** on the Fluid tab for the default tube of every "
"line: its outer and inner diameter in mm and its material. A toast says what "
"the default tube is now; it reaches the tubes drawn already."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:130
msgid ""
"Choose **Tube table** on the Fluid tab: one row per tube with its two ends, "
"medium, line kind, size and material."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:131
msgid ""
"Type a length into **Length** (in mm, or with its unit: `2 m`, `35 cm`) or a "
"tube part into **Tube part**; **Source** then says the value is manual. An "
"empty cell goes back to the derived value."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:133
msgid ""
"To print the tube list, choose **Output ▸ Report block…** and the report "
"**Tube list**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/fluid-workspace.md:135
msgid ""
"The panels are described in the [panel "
"reference](../reference/window/panels.md): Fluid, Tube table and Medium "
"table."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide takes a project made with an earlier release of ElektraWatt to "
"the release you have installed. Installing the new release itself is in "
"[Install ElektraWatt](install.md#upgrade)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:6
msgid "What a release reads and writes"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:8
msgid ""
"The version of ElektraWatt and the version of the project format are "
"numbered separately. Each release writes one format, the current one, and "
"reads that format and every older one it has a migration for. The [release "
"notes](../reference/release-notes/index.md) of each release name the format "
"it writes."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:12
msgid ""
"**An older format** opens migrated in memory: you see and edit the project "
"as the current format holds it, and nothing on disk changes until you save."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:14
msgid ""
"**A newer or an unreleased format**, one that a later release or a "
"development build wrote, opens read-only. The banner says so: \"This project "
"is open read-only: a file format this version does not write.\" Open it with "
"the release that wrote it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:18
msgid "Upgrade in the window"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:20
msgid ""
"Open the project with **File ▸ Open…** (`Ctrl+O`). The banner says \"This "
"project is in an older format; saving converts it.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:22
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Save** (`Ctrl+S`). The dialog \"Convert this project to the "
"current format?\" names the older format and the new one. Older versions of "
"ElektraWatt cannot open the project once it is converted."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:25
msgid ""
"Choose **Upgrade and save**. ElektraWatt first writes a backup of the "
"project as it was, `<project folder>.pre-<old format>.ewz`, beside the "
"project folder, then saves the project in the current format. If a file of "
"that name already holds something else, the backup takes the next free name "
"(`.pre-<old format>-2.ewz` and so on)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:30
msgid ""
"To go back, keep the backup: it is the whole project in one file. **File ▸ "
"Import…** opens it read-only, or extracts it into a new folder beside it, "
"which the earlier release opens."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:33
msgid "Upgrade from the terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:35
msgid "`ew upgrade` does the same without the window:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:42
msgid ""
"`--check` only reports: it exits 0 when the project needs no upgrade and 1 "
"when it is in an older format, and it writes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:44
msgid ""
"Without `--check`, `ew upgrade` asks you to type `upgrade` to confirm, "
"writes the backup beside the project folder and saves the project. `--yes` "
"confirms without asking, for a script; without a terminal and without "
"`--yes` it changes nothing and exits 2."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:48
msgid ""
"The [CLI reference](../reference/cli/upgrade.md) lists every flag and exit "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:50
msgid "What changes in the format from 0.1.0"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:52
msgid ""
"Until the release 0.1.0 the format changed from build to build without "
"migrations. From 0.1.0 on, the format that 0.1.0 writes is fixed: a later "
"release changes it only by adding to it, so that a project of 0.1.0 opens "
"and saves unchanged, or by a new format version that comes with a migration "
"from the released one, and with the first save's backup described above. The "
"rule is the project's [format "
"decision](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/adr/0011-format-versioning-and-migration.md), "
"amendment 6."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:3
msgid ""
"The reference states exact facts about EW and nothing else: what a command "
"takes, what a rule checks, what a program prints. Look here when you know "
"what you are doing and need the detail. To learn, use a "
"[tutorial](../tutorials/index.md); to understand the design, read the "
"[explanation](../explanation/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:7
msgid "Generated reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:9
msgid ""
"These pages are generated from the code by `cargo xtask docs-gen` and "
"checked in CI, so they cannot fall behind it (NFR-DOC-06). Do not edit them "
"by hand."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:12
msgid ""
"[Command reference](commands/index.md): every registered command, with its "
"parameters, types, constraints and exit codes, from the parameter schemas of "
"the command registry."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:14
msgid ""
"[Rule reference](rules/index.md): every shipped check rule, one page per "
"rule with an index page per pack, from the rule registry and the rule notes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:16
msgid ""
"[CLI reference](cli/index.md): every subcommand of `ew`, with its flags, "
"exit codes and `--json` result schema, from the CLI table."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:18
msgid ""
"[Query reference](queries.md): every public query of `ew query`, with its "
"key, example and output, from the query registry."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:20
msgid ""
"[api_v1 reference](api-v1.md): every `api_v1` view of `ew sql`, with its "
"columns, types and Preview marks."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:21
msgid ""
"[Format reference](format/index.md): every record kind of the project "
"format, with its file, rank and fields, from the format's JSON Schemas."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:23
msgid ""
"[Release notes](release-notes/index.md): the release and migration notes of "
"every release, newest first, from the notes in the repository."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:26
msgid "The window"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:28
msgid ""
"What each part of the desktop application's window does and how to open it. "
"`F1` opens the section of what is under the pointer, or of the tool, dialog "
"or pane you are in."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:31
msgid "[Tools](window/tools.md): every tool of the Draw tab."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:32
msgid ""
"[The command bar's buttons](window/command-bar.md): every other button of "
"the command bar, by tab."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:33
msgid "[Dialogs](window/dialogs.md): every dialog."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:34
msgid ""
"[Panels](window/panels.md): every pane and navigator, the strip editor and "
"the table mode."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:35
msgid ""
"[Things on a page](window/things.md): every kind of thing a page holds, how "
"to make and change it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:36
msgid "[Properties](properties.md): what each row of Properties means."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:38
msgid "Command-line program"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:40
msgid ""
"The usage text of `ew` is part of the program: `ew --help` lists the "
"subcommands and their options, and `ew sql schema` prints the `api_v1` views "
"that `ew sql` queries. The authoritative design catalogue, with the exit "
"codes of every subcommand, is [doc 17 "
"§3](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/design/17-extensibility.md#3-the-ew-cli)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:45
msgid "Not generated yet"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:47
msgid ""
"The documentation of the plugin interface (WIT, 1.x) is also meant to be "
"generated (NFR-DOC-06). It is not part of the book yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:3
msgid ""
"A tool decides what a click on the page does. One tool is active at a time; "
"Select is active when no other is. The Draw tab of the command bar holds the "
"tools, and each is in the Draw menu at the path given below. `F1` with a "
"tool active, or over its button, opens its section here."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:7
msgid ""
"The keys of every tool are on the [keymap](../keymaps.md) page, for each "
"keymap preset. A tool's own keys work only while it is active. `Esc` or a "
"right click on the page cancels what the active tool is doing, one step at a "
"time, and ends at Select; with Select active, a right click opens the page's "
"menu."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:11
msgid ""
"While a symbol hangs on the pointer or is dragged, a dotted line shows where "
"one of its pins would stand in line with a pin it faces, and a faint one "
"where it would stand level with a symbol drawn alike beside it. Within three "
"quarters of a grid step of the first line the symbol takes it and the line "
"turns solid; move on and it lets go. The symbol always stands on the grid, "
"and nothing moves it after you set it down. Hold `Alt` for no guides; Alt "
"also places or moves it without connecting it."
msgstr ""
"Terwijl een symbool aan de aanwijzer hangt of wordt versleept, toont een "
"gestippelde lijn waar een van zijn aansluitpunten in lijn zou staan met een "
"aansluitpunt tegenover, en een lichte lijn waar het op gelijke hoogte zou "
"staan met een gelijk getekend symbool ernaast. Binnen driekwart rasterstap "
"van de eerste lijn neemt het symbool die lijn en wordt de lijn "
"doorgetrokken; beweeg verder en het laat los. Het symbool staat altijd op "
"het raster, en niets verplaatst het nadat u het hebt neergezet. Houd `Alt` "
"ingedrukt voor geen hulplijnen; met Alt plaatst of verplaatst u het ook "
"zonder het te verbinden."

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:17
msgid ""
"The page's menu is the quick way to what fits the thing under the pointer: "
"its first entries are that thing's, then come the general ones (Rotate, "
"Mirror, Renumber devices… and Move to page… on a symbol; Cut, Copy, Paste, "
"Paste in place, Delete, Properties of selection). A right click selects the "
"thing first; Shift+F10 or the menu key opens the same menu for the selection."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:22
msgid ""
"On a wire: Route, Wire annotation, Define potential…, Cable… and Show wire "
"numbers on the pages; a wire does not turn, so there is no Rotate or Mirror. "
"On several selected wires: Make cable from the selection…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:24
msgid ""
"On a terminal: Add terminal (the strip's next number, of the terminal's kind "
"and part, floating at the pointer), Choose part… and Edit strip, which opens "
"the strip editor. On several selected terminals: Choose part…, for them all, "
"Bridge terminals (two terminals of one strip), and Spacing…, which spaces a "
"row of them the grid steps you choose apart from the leftmost (their wires "
"follow, one undo step). A strip's row in the Terminal strips navigator "
"offers Add terminal (with the part of the strip's last terminal of that "
"kind), Choose part for its terminals without one, and Edit strip."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:29
msgid ""
"On a relay's coil: Add contact, one entry per free contact of the relay by "
"name; it floats the contact's symbol for a click. On a contact: Go to coil."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:31
msgid ""
"On a symbol of a device: Join device… (only where another device can take "
"it, such as a contact another relay has free), Choose part…, Edit symbol and "
"Copy and edit."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:33
msgid ""
"On an interruption point: Supply comes in here…, Go to partner, Direction: "
"Source or Target, and Continue on page…. Supply comes in here… says that the "
"supply enters here from another drawing set (L1, L2, L3, N, PE from another "
"drawing): its form proposes where the supply comes from and the device "
"there, and one Apply marks every selected point; the points then print where "
"they come from and are no longer reported as unpaired, and a panel at them "
"asks what each carries (its kind of conductor and voltage; Enter takes them, "
"Escape leaves them without). On a marked point the entry reads Remove the "
"supply mark, and Potential… opens that panel again. The same link stands "
"under the point's name in Properties."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:40
msgid ""
"On the title block: Title block fields…. On an empty spot: Paste, Paste in "
"place, Page properties, New page… and Show wire numbers on the pages."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:42
msgid ""
"A navigator's row offers what the same thing offers on the page; a right "
"click on a row selects it first. A device's row offers Go to (its first "
"placement), its symbol's entries and Properties of selection; a cable's row "
"Cable… and Go to; a page's row Open page in a new tab, Title block fields…, "
"Page properties, New page… and Delete page (the right click opens the page). "
"See [Navigators](panels.md#navigators)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:47
msgid ""
"An entry that cannot be used now is greyed out, and its tooltip says why."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:49
msgid "Select"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:51
msgid ""
"Selects, moves and opens what you click: a click selects one item, a drag "
"draws a selection band, a double click opens the item's properties. Open it "
"with Draw ▸ Select. See [Use the application "
"window](../../how-to/application-window.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:55
msgid "Measure"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:57
msgid ""
"Measures the distance between two points on the page, in millimetres of the "
"sheet. Open it with Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Measure."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:62
msgid ""
"Places a symbol from the library. A symbol is a drawing without a part; the "
"symbol browser lists the symbols alone (the parts are the device browser's, "
"the terminals Terminal's). Double-click the symbol in the browser (its "
"categories at the left, a picture of the chosen row at the right), then "
"click where it goes; each click places one more until you finish. Under the "
"picture, **Edit symbol** opens the symbol in the symbol editor, where its "
"next version is saved to its library; a symbol of a read-only library (the "
"starter's) or one only the project holds offers **Copy and edit** alone, "
"which opens a copy that is saved to your own library. Open it with Draw ▸ "
"Place ▸ Symbol…. See [A first schematic page](../../tutorials/first-page.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:70
msgid "Device from part"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:72
msgid ""
"Places a device from a part of the library, so that the device has its part "
"from the start. A device is a part of the catalogue with its functions. The "
"browser lists one row per part; a part with several functions opens to them "
"with its arrow (or Right). Edit symbol and Copy and edit act on the symbol "
"of the chosen row, as in the symbol browser; a part drawn as a box offers "
"**Pins and box…** instead, which opens its device box in the symbol editor "
"(a copy of your own for a part you cannot change). Open it with Draw ▸ Place "
"▸ Device from part…. See [Choose parts](../../how-to/choose-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:78 src/reference/symbol-editor.md:285
msgid "Terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:80
msgid ""
"Asks which strip (one of the project's, or a new strip with the tag it will "
"take), which kind of terminal (feed-through, protective earth and the others "
"of the page's library), which part, how many and from which number (empty: "
"the strip's next free number), and the Spacing between the terminals in grid "
"steps, each shown with its length (\"2 grid steps · 8.0 mm\"; the project's "
"next row takes the spacing chosen last). The Part list holds No part and the "
"terminal parts of the project and the installed libraries. It proposes the "
"part of the strip's last terminal of that kind, and for a new strip the "
"starter part of the kind (EW-GEN-TRM-1 for a feed-through terminal, "
"EW-GEN-TRM-2 for a protective-earth terminal); another strip or kind "
"proposes again, and a part you choose sets the kind it is drawn as. Place "
"then floats the first terminal; one click lays the whole row, each terminal "
"the Spacing right of the one before and each with the part, and the tool "
"ends; one Undo takes the row back. Draw ▸ Place ▸ Terminal…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:90 src/explanation/eplan-and-ew.md:114
msgid "Black box"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:92
msgid ""
"Draws a black box: a rectangle with connection points for a device that the "
"library has no symbol for. Open it with Draw ▸ Place ▸ Black box…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:95
msgid ""
"A box drawn without a part takes pins where it stands. Select the box and "
"choose **Add pin** (Draw ▸ Arrange, beside Rotate and Mirror): the new pin "
"follows the pointer along the box's edge nearest it, and a click puts it "
"there. Right-click the box and choose Add pin to put the pin on the edge "
"nearest the click at once. A field then asks the pin's name; Enter adds it, "
"Tab adds it and asks for the next pin further along the same side. Two pins "
"may have one name (two 24 V inputs), as a part's pins may; where a name "
"cannot be taken, the line under the field says why. A wire drawn to the "
"box's edge where no pin is asks for a pin there in the same way and ends at "
"it. Double-click a pin to rename it; right-click it for **Remove pin**, "
"offered while no wire holds it. Each is one Undo step. The box goes no "
"narrower than its pins need and no lower than its longest top pin name and "
"its longest bottom pin name need (those two sides' names are turned a "
"quarter, reading from bottom to top; the left and right sides' are "
"horizontal). A box of a part draws the part's pins: its Add pin opens the "
"part's device box, where they are edited."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:106
msgid "Tool step back"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:108
msgid ""
"Takes back the last step of the active tool, such as the last corner of a "
"wire, without leaving the tool. It has no menu item; use its key."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:111
msgid "Rotate clockwise"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:113
msgid ""
"Turns the symbol that the tool floats, or the selection, by 90° clockwise. "
"Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Rotate clockwise. A turned symbol stays where it stands; "
"its wires leave its pins the new way, and a text of it you moved stays where "
"you put it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:117
msgid "Rotate counter-clockwise"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:119
msgid ""
"Turns the floating symbol, or the selection, by 90° counter-clockwise. Draw "
"▸ Arrange ▸ Rotate counter-clockwise."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:121
msgid "Mirror left to right"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:123
msgid ""
"Mirrors the floating symbol, or the selection, about its vertical axis. Draw "
"▸ Arrange ▸ Mirror left to right."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:125
msgid "Mirror top to bottom"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:127
msgid ""
"Mirrors the floating symbol, or the selection, about its horizontal axis. "
"Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Mirror top to bottom."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:129
msgid "Next variant"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:131
msgid ""
"Switches the floating symbol to its next variant, such as another contact "
"arrangement of the same function. Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Next variant."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:134
msgid "Next representation type"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:136
msgid ""
"Switches the floating symbol to its next representation type, for example "
"from the multi-line to the single-line form. Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Next "
"representation type."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:139
msgid "Move"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:141
msgid ""
"Moves the selection with the pointer or the keyboard; wires attached to it "
"follow. Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Move."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:143
msgid ""
"When you let go, a wire whose followed route would now run through a symbol, "
"a text, a connection point or along another wire, or would bend more than "
"before, is routed again; a wire that stays clean keeps its shape. Both "
"happen in the same undo step as the move."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:147
msgid "Finish"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:149
msgid ""
"Ends what the active tool is drawing, such as a wire or a polyline, at the "
"current point. It has no menu item; use its key or double-click where a tool "
"allows it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:152
msgid "Wire"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:154
msgid ""
"Draws connections between connection points: click a start point, then the "
"end point; corners are placed where you click in between. Open it with Draw "
"▸ Wire. See [A first schematic page](../../tutorials/first-page.md). A click "
"takes the connection point within about ten screen points of it, at any "
"zoom, and the ring under the pointer shows which. A click that misses and "
"lands right beside one is said in the status line at once (\"The click "
"placed a bend beside a connection point, not on it: …\"), as is a start "
"beside one that took nothing and a wire just drawn that bends beside one "
"without joining it; Ctrl+Z takes such a wire back. A first click on an empty "
"point away from any connection point starts nothing and says so (\"A wire "
"starts at a connection point, a junction or a wire: click one of them to "
"start it\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:163
msgid ""
"While you draw, the wire shows the route it will take. The route never runs "
"through another connection point, along another wire, through a symbol, over "
"the frame's bands, or through a junction, and never makes a fourth arm of a "
"junction. It keeps clear of texts too, unless the only way round them is far "
"longer (round a group of symbols or the sheet) or there is none: then the "
"route crosses the text and the hint says \"The route crosses a text: keeping "
"clear of it is a far longer way.\" A device tag on the wire then moves to a "
"free place beside its symbol. When no route exists at all the wire shows as "
"a dashed right-angled line and the hint says \"No clear route: the wire "
"stays a dashed line without a route. Click free points to lead it, or route "
"it later with Route.\" Ending it there stores an unrouted wire: it keeps its "
"connection, but it is drawn dashed and the check reports it (\"Wire … has no "
"route: it is drawn as a dashed line.\") until it has a route. Click on empty "
"points to place bends and the wire follows them (\"Bend placed: click a "
"connection point to end the wire.\"); a click inside a symbol places no bend "
"and says so (\"A bend cannot sit inside a symbol; click one of its "
"connection points, or a free point outside it\"). Where no clean way leads "
"on from your last bend, the wire is routed from its start as if drawn anew, "
"without your bends, or stays unrouted: a wire never runs through a pin or "
"along another wire. The hint says what a click at the pointer does: on "
"another wire it joins that wire with a tee, drawn with a bevel (Alt+Click "
"places a bend there instead), on the start point it does nothing. A junction "
"that already joins three wires is not an end: the hint says \"This junction "
"already joins three wires: end on a wire to make a new junction.\" The point "
"where two wires cross is not an end either (a junction there would join all "
"four arms): end on the wire a step beside the crossing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:181
msgid ""
"An unrouted wire, from an import or a placement, is drawn as a dashed line "
"between its ends, straight or with one corner and never diagonal, on the "
"page and in the PDF. The line takes no notice of what lies under it: it is "
"no route yet. Route draws it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:185
msgid ""
"A route the tool finds between your two clicks counts as the program's: "
"Route may draw it again later. A route with a bend you placed is yours, and "
"so is one you change by hand; Route leaves it alone unless you select the "
"wire."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:188
msgid "Dot at the joint"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:190
msgid ""
"A wire that ends on another wire joins it with a bevel: the wires then run "
"from terminal to terminal, and the bevel shows which terminal carries two of "
"them. Press `.` while you draw the wire, before you click on the other wire, "
"to join it with a dot instead: then every wire of the joint starts from the "
"terminal the potential comes from. Press `.` again for the bevel. A joint "
"you made keeps its form; change it later in the junction's editor (select "
"the junction and press Enter)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:198
msgid ""
"The Wire tool on a fluid page, drawing tubes at once: open it with Tube on "
"the Fluid tab (Fluid ▸ Tubes ▸ Tube). It draws the line kind used last, "
"Working at first; the bar under the page says the kind and the project's "
"default tube, and its Line kind button steps through Working, Pilot, "
"Exhaust, Drain and Flexible. Draw ▸ Wire draws working tubes. A pilot or "
"drain tube is dashed; a flexible one (a hose) is drawn as an arc between its "
"ends. Right-click a tube to change its line kind."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:204
msgid "Next autoconnect proposal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:206
msgid ""
"While a symbol floats near other connection points, shows the next of the "
"connections EW proposes to draw when you place it. It has no menu item; use "
"its key."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:209
msgid "Connect while moving"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:211
msgid ""
"While moving a symbol, connects its connection points to the ones it lands "
"on. It has no menu item; use its key."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:213
msgid ""
"Connect while moving is on when you start: the wires the moved symbol would "
"make are shown while you drag and are drawn with the move when you let go, "
"in the same undo step. Hold the key that places without wires as you let go "
"to move without them. The key of Connect while moving turns it off, and on "
"again, until you close the program; the hint of the move and the status bar "
"show the key followed by \"Connect on move: on\" or \"off\". A move with the "
"keyboard follows that setting."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:219
msgid "Change a wire's route by hand"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:221
msgid ""
"Select the wire with Select: it shows as a band along its path, with a round "
"handle in the middle of each segment and on each bend; the handle under the "
"pointer grows and the pointer shows which way it moves. Drag a segment's "
"handle (or the segment itself) and it moves sideways; drag a bend's handle "
"and the corner moves. The wire stays right-angled and its ends stay on their "
"connection points: a first or last segment that is dragged keeps a short "
"stub at its end and jogs after it, so a straight wire dragged sideways goes "
"round what was in its way. A dashed line shows the new route while you drag, "
"`Esc` puts it back, and one undo takes the change back. A wire without a "
"route, drawn dashed, takes its first route this way too. Only the selected "
"wire has handles: where wires lie over one another, click again on the same "
"spot to select the next of them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:230
msgid ""
"With the keyboard: select the wire, then `Tab` gives its first handle the "
"focus and the handle shows as the one under the pointer does; `Tab` and "
"`Shift+Tab` go from handle to handle along the wire. The arrow keys move the "
"handle one grid step, each step one undo step, and `Esc` gives the focus "
"back to the wire."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:234
msgid ""
"When it draws or routes a wire, the router never puts a corner of one wire "
"on a corner or an end of another: it reads as a joint. Between pins that "
"stand close, such as two two-pole devices wired cross-over a few grid steps "
"apart, a wire may step half a grid step to cross the other once instead of "
"going round."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:238
msgid "Route"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:240
msgid ""
"Routes the wires of the selection (the selected wires and every wire with an "
"end on a selected symbol), or with nothing selected the wires of the page "
"that need it: those without a route, those whose route is broken (it lies on "
"another wire, runs through a pin or a symbol) and those the program routed "
"before. Symbols stay where they are. A wire you drew yourself keeps its "
"route unless you select it or it is broken. Open it with Draw ▸ Connect ▸ "
"Route, or Route in the menu of a wire or a symbol."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:246
msgid ""
"The wires of one potential are routed together. A potential that runs over "
"several symbols, from pin to pin or through junctions, becomes one straight "
"line with a straight drop to each pin; the junctions move onto that line and "
"keep their wires and their order. Two wires of one pin may run on one line "
"out of it and part where they go different ways: the page draws the parting "
"with a 45° bevel, the way two wires on one terminal are drawn. A wire "
"between two pins that face each other on one line is one straight line. What "
"is left is routed as the Wire tool routes it, and a tag that stands in a "
"wire's way steps aside."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:253
msgid ""
"Route applies at once, in one undo step: the toast names the wires it "
"changed, by their numbers or their ends, three of them and then how many "
"more (\"Routed 5 wires: 101.3, 101.5, 101.7 and 2 more.\"), and the page "
"shows them as selected wires while the toast is up. A wire without a way "
"keeps its route, or stays a dashed line, and the toast says what is in its "
"way, such as \"No way for -101F1:2 → -101E55:L: other wires are in its "
"way.\" Where Route would replace a broken route you drew, the Preview tab "
"shows the plan first and names those wires; Apply stores it, Cancel leaves "
"the page as it is."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:260
msgid ""
"With nothing selected, Route draws again by itself only the wires the "
"program routed, the unrouted ones and the broken ones. Every other routed "
"wire counts as drawn by you, and so does every wire of a project made before "
"this version: the toast says how many keep their routes (\"Nothing changed: "
"14 wires keep the routes they have.\"), and where routing them anew would "
"make the page better, its Route these too… shows that plan in the Preview "
"tab first. To have some of them drawn again, select them and choose Route. "
"When no wire would run better, it says \"Nothing to route: no wire would run "
"better.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:267
msgid "Arrange and route"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:269
msgid ""
"Moves the symbols of the selection, or with nothing selected the symbols of "
"the page, to where the page is best for them, and routes their wires: every "
"wire of the page, or of the selection and the symbols in it, also the wires "
"you drew yourself. Open it with Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Arrange and route, or "
"Arrange and route in the menu of a symbol. Nothing changes until you choose "
"Apply."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:274
msgid ""
"Each symbol is put in line with the pin it is wired to, on the side that pin "
"faces: a contact under the breaker that feeds it, a sensor over its input's "
"pin, a cylinder over its valve, a mark at the left end of the potential that "
"comes in. A symbol may cross the page for it, within the rules of a drawing "
"of its kind: on an electrical page the potentials stand at the head and the "
"foot and current runs down a circuit, a card's channels keep their order; on "
"a fluid page the supply stands below the valves and each actuator above its "
"valve, the actuators in their order; on a bus page the stations stand in the "
"order of the line, level. The space you left along a circuit stays: a symbol "
"is neither pulled nearer nor spread out, unless it has to move. A row you "
"made (the terminals of a strip, cards side by side, like symbols level) "
"moves as one or stays as one; a terminal wired to a card stands in the "
"column of its channel, so a strip between a card and its field devices takes "
"the order and the spacing of the channels. A strip drawn close together over "
"a card opens as one in the order it stands in: the terminals of channels "
"that stand in their channels' order go to their channels' columns, every "
"other terminal (a feed or common terminal, one wired to a card's second row "
"of pins, a spare) keeps its place between its neighbours, and the sensors "
"over the strip come over their terminals. A terminal never passes another: "
"the strip reads in the order you drew it. Every arrangement is routed and "
"measured before you see it. A move has a price, so a symbol moves only where "
"the page gets visibly better, and a page already in order gives no plan. It "
"never offers less than Route would do with the same wires: when moving "
"symbols does not make the page better than routing alone, it offers Route's "
"plan and says that no symbol moves. That is also what it does on a page "
"wired against its symbols, such as a card whose pins face up wired to the "
"upper ends of terminals that stand below it: no arrangement mends that, wire "
"the terminals by the ends that face the card. A card drawn before this "
"version has two rows of pins, so half its channels face away from their "
"circuits. You can draw it again without wiring it anew: delete its box on "
"the page (the card, its channels and its wires stay in the project), choose "
"Draw on a page for it in the station view, and run Arrange and route, which "
"draws the wires to the new pins. A terminal whose card now stands on its "
"other side still has to be wired by the end that faces the card."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:297
msgid ""
"It never moves a symbol you keep in place (right-click ▸ Keep in place), a "
"symbol outside the selection, a symbol in a structure box, or a symbol to "
"another page, and it never turns or mirrors one. The symbols you keep stay "
"where they are and the others are arranged round them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:301
msgid ""
"The Preview tab shows what it would do: how many symbols move and how many "
"of them across the sheet, how many wires are routed and how many of those "
"you drew, the page's crossings and bends now and then, a ticked row per "
"symbol that moves (the far moves first) with its move in grid steps, a "
"ticked row per wire you drew that gets a new route, and what is left as it "
"is: the symbols kept in place, with their pin, the symbols that stand "
"against a rule of the drawing with what was in the way of their place on the "
"side their pin faces (a symbol standing there, the edge of the drawing area, "
"a text a wire would run through, more crossings), and the wires without a "
"way. The page shows it as it would become: the symbols that move tinted, "
"their old places as faint outlines joined to the new ones by a dotted line. "
"Hold B to see the page as it is."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:311
msgid ""
"Untick a row to plan again with that symbol where it stands, or that wire as "
"it runs; the page and the rows follow, and what you see is what Apply "
"stores. The tick of the heading \"Wires you drew yourself\" unticks all your "
"wires at once, so that each keeps its route, and ticks them all again. A row "
"you unticked stays out, and a plan made again brings in no other wire of "
"yours. When you have unticked everything, the card closes and says \"Nothing "
"is left to apply: every change of this preview is unticked.\" Apply stores "
"it in one step, and the toast says what moved and what was routed "
"(\"Arranged folio 102: 4 symbols moved, 9 wires routed.\"); one Undo takes "
"all of it back. In the history the step is called Arrange and route, or "
"Route wires when no symbol moved. When the card closes, a page that was "
"shown whole is shown whole again. When nothing would make the page better, "
"it says \"Nothing to arrange: the page is already arranged as well as the "
"rules allow.\" Where something stands in the way, its toast is titled "
"\"Nothing to arrange\" and names it: a symbol left against a rule, a "
"terminal strip that stays as it stands (its terminals wired to the card in "
"another order, too little room between the channels, a symbol in the way, "
"the edge of the drawing area, or more crossings in the strip's order than "
"now: then look at the terminals wired out of that order), a wire without a "
"route, the faults the page keeps. Where several things stand in the way, the "
"toast names the first and how many more; hover over it, or open "
"Notifications in the status bar, to read them all."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:326 src/explanation/eplan-and-ew.md:44
msgid "Interruption point"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:328
msgid ""
"Places an interruption point: the end of a potential on this page that "
"continues at a point of the same name elsewhere. Open it with Draw ▸ Connect "
"▸ Interruption point."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:331
msgid ""
"The arrow always points right, the way the signal flows. A source, where the "
"potential leaves the page, has its wire on the arrow's tail and belongs at "
"the page's right edge; a target, where the potential arrives, has its wire "
"on the arrow's tip and belongs at the left edge. The name is above the arrow "
"and the cross-reference below it. The arrow is never rotated or mirrored: "
"change the point's direction instead. While you draw a wire, the "
"interruption point's key ends the wire in a point of the right form for that "
"end."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:337
msgid ""
"Source and Target beside the name field show the direction the next click "
"places, wherever on the page it lands. The tool starts at Source, as a new "
"pair begins with its source, or at Target when the name you type has its "
"source already. Clicking Source or Target, or pressing D, chooses the "
"direction, and the choice holds until you change it: with Target chosen, "
"every click places one more target, its source drawn or not, and the bar "
"says when the name still has no source (\"next: one more target of 24V, "
"which has no source yet\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:343
msgid ""
"Type the name in the field Interruption point name at the page's top left, "
"once for a pair: the tool then floats the same name as the first point's "
"partner, on this page or the next: after its source the tool turns to Target "
"by itself, as a name has one source, and the bar says so before the click "
"(\"next: a target, where 24V goes on\"). The name stays in the field until "
"you change it: once the pair is whole, each further click places one more "
"target of the same source, and the source's cross-reference lists every "
"place it goes to. Without a typed name the tool offers the partner of a "
"point that still waits for one of the direction shown (a target without a "
"source when Source is shown, a source without a target when Target is), and "
"a target of a name that has its source keeps that name; only otherwise does "
"a point get a new name. A potential comes from one source and goes on at its "
"targets. When you choose the tool from the Draw tab or the menu, the field "
"has the keys with its name selected: type the name and press Enter or Tab, "
"then click where the point goes. While the field has the keys, D is a letter "
"of the name and the bar says \"Enter ends the name\"; after Enter, Tab or a "
"click on the page, D turns the point. The toast of a placed point says what "
"it is and where its partner is (\"L1: source here at 101-B2, its target at "
"/102.3\"), or that the partner is still to come. A point dropped on a pin, "
"or with the pointer as near to it as the Wire tool catches a pin, joins that "
"pin, whichever its form, and no other point's text keeps it off the pin. Alt "
"places it without joining; a point left beside a pin that has no wire says "
"so when it is placed, and the check's message for that pin offers to join "
"the two. A free point too close to another says how far apart the two must "
"stand. A point names the potential it carries whichever its direction, so a "
"wire on a target that no source feeds yet carries the target's name."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:363
msgid ""
"A point keeps room for its texts: where its name or its cross-reference "
"would print over another interruption point's, it is not placed, and the "
"message says so. Three grid steps below or above the other point is clear; "
"points side by side are clear when their texts are. Hold Alt to place it "
"anyway."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:367
msgid "Interruption point direction"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:369
msgid ""
"Turns the interruption point that the tool floats from source to target, or "
"back. Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Interruption point direction. An undirected point, "
"which pairs either way, is a choice of a placed point's Direction in its "
"properties."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:373
msgid ""
"The point takes the form of its new direction: the wire moves from the tail "
"to the tip, or back. Changing the direction of a placed point in its "
"properties does the same, and is refused when the project does not hold the "
"other form yet: place one point of that direction first."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:377
msgid "Star source"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:379
msgid ""
"Marks the interruption point as the source of its name in star mode: one "
"source, many targets. Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Star source."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:382
msgid "Wire annotation"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:384
msgid ""
"Places a wire annotation, the label that prints the cross-section and "
"colours of the wires it marks. Open it with **Annotate** on the Draw tab or "
"Shift+W. Drag a line across the wires, type the cross-section and the "
"colours in the tool's fields if they are to change, and press Enter. The "
"fields may be filled before or after the line: a field takes its value when "
"you leave it (Enter, Tab, a click on the other field or back on the page), "
"and the marker prints what the fields show. A colour is a code in any case "
"(`BK`, `bk`), two codes of one wire (`GN+YE` or `GNYE`), a company code of "
"the project's colour profile, or a colour's name from that profile; one "
"colour is every marked wire's, and a list (`BK, BU`) gives each wire its own "
"in the order the line crosses them. A colour the project does not know is "
"refused with the codes it knows. The marker prints a short line across the "
"wires and its text beyond the line's end, clear of the wires."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:394
msgid ""
"The wires' numbers print on the page with **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Show wire "
"numbers on the pages** or the checkbox of that name in the project's "
"Properties, one setting for the whole project that the PDF and print follow "
"too: each wire's number stands beside it, once, at the middle of its longest "
"segment, and on a wire that carries its own marker the number comes first in "
"the marker's text (`101.1 1.5 BK`). A cable's core prints no number; its "
"cable and core name it. Each wire is one conductor with its own number: a 24 "
"V rail tapped by four branches is four wires, each piece of the rail sharing "
"the number of the branch it feeds. **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Number wires by "
"potential** gives every wire of a potential the potential's name instead, "
"one code for the whole rail."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:401
msgid "Text"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:403
msgid "Places a free text on the page. Open it with Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Text."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:405
msgid "Graphic"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:407
msgid ""
"Draws a graphic: a line, polyline, rectangle, circle, arc or polygon, which "
"has no electrical meaning. Open it with Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Graphic and the "
"shape."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:410 src/explanation/eplan-and-ew.md:122
msgid "Structure box"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:412
msgid ""
"Draws a structure box: a dashed frame that puts what it encloses into a "
"function or location of the project's structure. Open it with Draw ▸ "
"Annotate ▸ Structure box."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:415
msgid "Image"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:417
msgid ""
"Places an image file (SVG, PNG or JPEG) on the page. Open it with Draw ▸ "
"Annotate ▸ Image…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:419
msgid "Place device"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:421
msgid ""
"On a panel layout page, places a device of the location on a rail or the "
"mounting plate. Open it from the Unplaced devices navigator, which lists the "
"devices of the location that have no place yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:424
msgid "Place glands"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:426
msgid ""
"On a panel layout page of the bottom, the top or a side, places the cable "
"glands dragged from Glands in the Unplaced devices navigator: while you "
"drag, the outline of each gland's nut follows the pointer, in the row they "
"stand in; let go to place them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:430
msgid "Rail"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:432
msgid ""
"On a panel layout page, draws a mounting rail. Open it with Draw ▸ Rail. The "
"page shows an enclosure first: Project ▸ Setup ▸ Enclosure… puts the new "
"enclosure's plate on it ([Draw a panel "
"layout](../../how-to/panel-layout.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:435
msgid "Duct"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:437
msgid "On a panel layout page, draws a wiring duct. Open it with Draw ▸ Duct."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:439
msgid "Edit frame"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:441
msgid ""
"Changes the page's frame and its title block on the page. Open it with Page "
"▸ Frame ▸ Edit frame, a double click on the title block or on the column "
"numbers or row letters, or Edit frame in the title block's menu; Done or Esc "
"ends it. The page's drawing fades, and the HUD says on which frame and title "
"block the page prints: every change shows on each page that prints them. A "
"click chooses a cell (Shift adds one); Properties then shows its Text, what "
"it Shows (a value of the project, the page, the revision or a person, a "
"field of your own, New field of your own…, Company logo, A picture… or Text "
"only), its Text size and its Alignment; a double click on the cell opens its "
"text in the field Edit text has, Enter takes it and Esc keeps the cell as it "
"was. The HUD splits the chosen cell side by side or one above the other, "
"joins the chosen cells when together they make a rectangle, and puts a "
"picture into the cell. A line between two cells is dragged: every cell on "
"either side of it grows or shrinks with it, snapped to 0.5 mm (Alt: free), "
"none narrower or lower than 2 mm. Each change is one undo step. The first "
"change of the program's frame and title block makes them the project's own, "
"and every page that printed them prints the project's copies; the toast says "
"so once."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:454
msgid ""
"With nothing chosen, Properties shows the frame: its paper, its title block "
"(Shared with the other frames, or Its own: a copy of the block for this "
"frame alone), Give this page its own frame, its columns and rows (a click "
"chooses them) and Use the frames of a template…, which takes a template's "
"frames, title blocks, fields of your own and company logo into the project "
"after a preview; a page with a frame of its own keeps it. Chosen, the "
"columns and rows show How many, Counted with, First, the rows' Skip I and O, "
"Drawn and the title block's height: each value typed is previewed on the "
"card, with the cross-references that print differently, and Apply writes "
"them in one step. The title block's height is the block's: every frame that "
"prints it takes the new height, and the block ends at the border on each."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:3
msgid ""
"The command bar at the top of the window holds the program's commands in "
"tabs: Project, Page, Draw, PLC, Fluid, Check, Output and View. A button that "
"cannot be used now is greyed out, and its tooltip says why. `F1` over a "
"button opens its section here; the tools of the Draw tab have theirs on the "
"[Tools](tools.md) page, and a button with a dialog of its own opens that "
"dialog's section."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:8
msgid "Project"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:10
msgid "Project properties"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:12
msgid ""
"Shows the project's own rows in Properties: the document title, the drawing "
"number, the parties and the settings the title blocks print. See "
"[Properties](panels.md#properties)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:15
msgid "Structure node…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:17
msgid ""
"Makes a structure node: a function (`=`), a location (`+`, a cabinet such as "
"`+CAB1`) or a product (`-`). Devices and pages are given their nodes in "
"Properties. See [Structure identifiers](panels.md#structure-identifiers)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:20
msgid "Enclosure…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:22
msgid ""
"Makes a cabinet's enclosure from an enclosure part, with its mounting plate, "
"at a location. On a panel layout page the page shows the plate; a location "
"that has its enclosure already gets it drawn on the page instead (**Draw "
"-UC1 of +CAB1 on this page**). On another page the button makes a panel "
"layout page after it and draws the enclosure there; **Create only** makes it "
"without drawing it. See [Draw a panel "
"layout](../../how-to/panel-layout.md#add-the-enclosure)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:27
msgid "Parts"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:29
msgid ""
"Opens Parts (`F9`), the one place where a part of every kind is looked up, "
"seen whole, changed and made: the list of your parts, the project's and the "
"libraries', the open part's sheet with its groups, and beside it how the "
"part is drawn and what the program's features still lack of it. **Back** "
"returns to where you were. See [Make your own "
"parts](../../how-to/own-parts.md) and [Parts ▸ Fields](../part-fields.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:34
msgid "Page"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:36
msgid "New page…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:38
msgid ""
"Makes a page: its number, its type (multi-line, single-line, panel layout, "
"fluid, report and others), its title and its grid. See "
"[Pages](panels.md#pages)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:41
msgid "New section…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:43
msgid ""
"Makes a section of the drawing set: a range of page numbers with a title, "
"such as 100 to 199 \"Power\". New pages in that range belong to it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:46
msgid "Connection grid…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:48
msgid ""
"Changes the grid of the page shown. It asks for the grid and says what the "
"change does to what the page holds; with a fit, the symbols and junctions "
"move to the nearest points of the new grid, except that where two of them "
"would end on one another, or close a gap a wire could pass through, the one "
"to the right (or below) moves on by whole steps; the wires are drawn anew. "
"See [Pages](panels.md#pages)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:53
msgid "Renumber pages…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:55
msgid ""
"Gives the pages new numbers in their order, in steps you choose, keeping the "
"cross-references right."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:57
msgid "Move page up"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:59
msgid "Moves the page shown one place up in the page order."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:61
msgid "Move page down"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:63
msgid "Moves the page shown one place down in the page order."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:65
msgid "Copy pages…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:67
msgid ""
"Copies the marked pages (else the page shown) for a second, identical "
"installation: each device with all its functions, so a coil and its contacts "
"stay together, each strip with its terminals, each cable with its cores. The "
"form asks for the new folio numbers (a number added to each, or the page the "
"copies follow), a structure node the copy gets of its own, and texts to "
"change in frozen tags and in the names of interruption points (24V into "
"24V.2). The tags follow the new pages. Copies whose new numbers no section "
"holds get a section of their own over that hundred, titled as their pages' "
"section. The preview says what the copy leaves on pages that are not copied "
"and which section it makes; Apply makes the copy in one undo step."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:75
msgid "Move to page…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:77
msgid ""
"Moves the selected symbols to another schematic page, or to a new page in "
"the section of the schematic pages, titled as the page they come from. It "
"asks for the page, then shows a preview before anything moves: how many "
"symbols go to which page, what the changed items are, and each wire to a "
"symbol that stays behind, which becomes a pair of interruption points. Apply "
"moves them and shows the page. The right-click menu of selected symbols has "
"it too. See [Interruption points](things.md#interruption-points)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:83
msgid "Add legend"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:85
msgid ""
"On a panel layout page, puts the legend beside the layout: each item number "
"of the page with its tag, quantity, part number and description. See [Draw a "
"panel layout](../../how-to/panel-layout.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:88
msgid "Report block…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:90
msgid ""
"Puts a list on a page: a parts list, a terminal diagram, a cable list and "
"the others, generated from the project and kept up to date. See [Lists on a "
"page](things.md#lists-on-a-page)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:93
msgid "List columns…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:95
msgid ""
"On a page of a list (the Output tab's Reports group): which columns the list "
"prints, in what order, with what heading and width, a caption above the "
"table and a legend under it. See [Lists on a "
"page](things.md#lists-on-a-page)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:98
msgid "Rows of your own…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:100
msgid ""
"On a page of a list (the Output tab's Reports group): rows the model does "
"not hold, such as a spare terminal or a note; they stay in the list however "
"often it is made again from the drawing. See [Lists on a "
"page](things.md#lists-on-a-page)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:104
msgid "Open parts list"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:106
msgid ""
"Shows the project's parts list: every part with its quantity, its "
"description and the tags of its devices."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:108
msgid "Draw"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:110
msgid "Supply"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:112
msgid ""
"Places the supply that comes in from the mains in one go: its form asks what "
"comes in (single phase L, N, PE; three phase with or without N; 24 V DC; "
"your own names) and where it comes from, the marks follow the pointer as one "
"group, and a click places them, one under the other; their Potential panel "
"then asks what each carries. See [Supply comes in here](things.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:117
msgid "Cable…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:119
msgid ""
"Makes a cable of the selected wires, or gives a wire's cable its part, and "
"draws the cable's mark across them. See [Cables and their "
"marks](things.md#cables-and-their-marks)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:122
msgid "Renumber devices…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:124
msgid ""
"Gives the devices new numbers by their place in the drawings "
"(`Ctrl+Shift+N`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:126
msgid "Symbol editor…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:128
msgid ""
"Opens the symbol editor: a new symbol, a copy of a library symbol, or the "
"box of a device. See [Symbol editor](../symbol-editor.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:131
msgid "Auto-fill rail…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:133
msgid ""
"On a panel layout page, places the cabinet's devices that are not placed yet "
"on a rail, in the order of the schematic or of their tags. Devices without a "
"location can be given the cabinet's in the same step. See [Draw a panel "
"layout](../../how-to/panel-layout.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:137
msgid "Add pin"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:139
msgid ""
"On a selected box drawn without a part, puts a new pin on its edge: the pin "
"follows the pointer along the edge nearest it, a click puts it there, and a "
"field asks its name. On a box of a part it opens the part's device box. See "
"[Black box](tools.md#black-box)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:145
msgid "New station…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:147
msgid ""
"Makes a PLC station: a CPU or a head station with its rack, chosen from the "
"catalogue. See [Make a "
"station](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#make-a-station)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:150
msgid "Rack…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:152
msgid ""
"Opens the selected station's view: its rack with a card per slot, the "
"catalogue beside it, and the module's channels. See [Read and change a "
"rack](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#read-and-change-a-rack)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:155
msgid "Attach modules without a station"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:157
msgid ""
"Gives the modules that have no station yet (cards made on their own) the "
"selected CPU's station."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:159
msgid "Preview PLC addressing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:161
msgid ""
"Shows the addresses the channels get before they are given: the old and the "
"new address of each channel. See "
"[Addresses](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#addresses)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:164
msgid "Symbol policy…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:166
msgid ""
"Sets how the channels' symbol names are made: by hand, or from a template "
"such as `{target.dt}_{pin}`. See [The PLC grid and the "
"symbols](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#the-plc-grid-and-the-symbols)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:169
msgid "Process-tag scheme…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:171
msgid ""
"Sets the form of the process tags the instrument list and the channels carry."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:173
msgid "Channel addresses at terminals"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:175
msgid ""
"Prints a channel's address (`I0.0`) at the terminals its wire runs through, "
"on or off for the project."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:177
msgid "Export to PLC software…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:179
msgid ""
"Writes the project's stations, modules and symbols to a file the PLC "
"software reads. See [Exchange with the PLC "
"software](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#exchange-with-the-plc-software)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:182
msgid "Import from PLC software…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:184
msgid ""
"Reads a file of the PLC software and shows what it would change before it "
"does. See [Exchange with the PLC "
"software](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#exchange-with-the-plc-software)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:187
msgid "Import instrument list…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:189
msgid ""
"Reads an instrument list (a table of the field devices with their signals) "
"and shows the channels and devices it would make before it does."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:192
msgid "New bus page…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:194
msgid ""
"Makes a bus topology page, where the bus devices and their ports are drawn. "
"See [Bus ports and nets](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#bus-ports-and-nets)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:197
msgid "Place port"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:199
msgid ""
"Places a port of a bus device that is not drawn yet on the bus page. See "
"[Bus ports and nets](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#bus-ports-and-nets)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:202
msgid "Bus panel"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:204
msgid ""
"Shows the Bus panel: the bus nets and the ports on them. See "
"[Bus](panels.md#bus)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:206 src/reference/window/panels.md:128
msgid "PLC grid"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:208
msgid ""
"Shows the PLC grid in the bottom dock: every channel in a row, with its "
"address, its symbol and its device. A column that cuts a text grows to show "
"it. See [PLC grid](panels.md#plc-grid)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:211
msgid "PLC workspace"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:213
msgid ""
"Arranges the window for PLC work: the PLC panel on the left, the PLC grid "
"below. See [Open the "
"workspace](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#open-the-workspace)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:216
msgid "PLC guide"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:218
msgid ""
"Opens the guide to the PLC workspace: [Work in the PLC "
"workspace](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:220 src/reference/window/panels.md:149
msgid "Fluid"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:222
msgid "New fluid page…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:224
msgid ""
"Makes a fluid page, where the pneumatic or hydraulic circuit is drawn. See "
"[Draw a circuit](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md#draw-a-circuit)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:227
msgid "Fluid symbol…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:229
msgid ""
"Places a fluid symbol on the fluid page: a valve, a cylinder, a service unit "
"and the others. See [Valves and cylinders](things.md#valves-and-cylinders)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:232
msgid "Valve terminal…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:234
msgid ""
"Makes a valve terminal with its positions, or draws a station's valve "
"terminal on the fluid page. See [Valve "
"terminals](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md#valve-terminals)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:237
msgid "Air supply…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:239
msgid "Places the air supply: where the compressed air enters the circuit."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:241
msgid "Generate overview…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:243
msgid ""
"Makes the overview page of the selected valve terminal: its positions with "
"their valves and order codes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:245
msgid "Draw the plug on an electrical page…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:247
msgid ""
"Draws the multipin plug of the selected valve terminal on an electrical "
"page, with a pin for each solenoid."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:249
msgid "Plug the open ports"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:251
msgid ""
"Plugs the open working ports of the selected valves in one step, so that no "
"port is left open."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:253
msgid "New medium…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:255
msgid ""
"Defines a medium (compressed air, hydraulic oil) with its colour and "
"pattern. See [The medium](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md#the-medium)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:258 src/reference/window/panels.md:164
msgid "Medium table"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:260
msgid ""
"Shows the media of the project and the tube each medium takes. See [Medium "
"table](panels.md#medium-table)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:262 src/reference/window/panels.md:157
msgid "Tube table"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:264
msgid ""
"Shows the tubes of the project with their medium, size and length. See [Tube "
"table](panels.md#tube-table)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:266
msgid "Fluid settings…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:268
msgid ""
"Sets the fluid defaults of the project: the default tube of every line and "
"how the tubes are numbered."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:270
msgid "Fluid workspace"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:272
msgid ""
"Arranges the window for fluid work: the Fluid panel on the left, the tube "
"table below. See [Open the "
"workspace](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md#open-the-workspace)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:275
msgid "Fluid power guide"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:277
msgid ""
"Opens the guide to the Fluid workspace: [Work in the Fluid "
"workspace](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:279
msgid "Check"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:281
msgid "Check project"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:283
msgid ""
"Checks the project now and lists the findings in the Messages panel, with "
"the change since the last check. See [Messages](panels.md#messages)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:286
msgid "Next finding"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:288
msgid ""
"Goes to the next finding of the Messages panel and selects its things on the "
"page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:290
msgid "Previous finding"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:292
msgid "Goes to the previous finding of the Messages panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:294
msgid "Highlight"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:296
msgid ""
"Marks the thing of the selected row of a list (a potential, a wire, a "
"medium) on the pages, in a colour, until **Clear highlights** "
"(`Ctrl+Shift+H`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:299
msgid "Clear highlights"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:301
msgid "Removes every highlight from the pages."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:303
msgid "Output"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:305
msgid "Export DXF…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:307
msgid ""
"Writes every page as an AutoCAD 2010 DXF file into a folder you choose, with "
"the title block's picture as a file beside them: a symbol is a block, and a "
"device's tag and other texts are attributes of it. The folder must be empty "
"or hold such a set already, which the new one replaces. For a DWG, open the "
"files in your CAD program or a converter."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:311
msgid "Open wire list"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:313
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Shows the first page of the project's wire list, the "
"fitter's sheet: per wire its ends, its colour, its cross-section, its length "
"and what goes on each end; List columns… adds the potential, the source, the "
"joins and the wire part. A project without a wire list opens Report block… "
"on the wire list."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:317
msgid "Open assembly lists"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:319
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Shows the first page of the project's assembly lists: "
"per terminal strip first where it goes on its rail and how long it is, then "
"every item in the order the fitter mounts it (end brackets, terminals with "
"their part, colour and marker, end plates), then its bridges. A project "
"without assembly lists opens Report block… on them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:323
msgid "Open cut list"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:325
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Shows the first page of the project's cut list: the "
"rails, the ducts with their covers, the cables, the wires and the tubes to "
"cut, each piece rounded up to the cut increment of Project properties ▸ "
"Workshop (a wire as long as the wire list says). A project without a cut "
"list opens Report block… on it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:329
msgid "Open consumables"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:331
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Shows the first page of the project's consumables: "
"the metres of each wire, the ferrules and lugs per part and the markers per "
"label set, what to order beside the parts list. A project without one opens "
"Report block… on it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:335
msgid "Rework list…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:337
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Compares the project as it is with a release and "
"lists what the fitter changes in the built cabinet, in the order he does it: "
"the wires to pull, to replace, the ends to move, the lengths to check and "
"the wires to add. It compares with the newest release whose archive is in "
"the folder beside the project, or with another release or an archive file "
"you choose; Export… writes the list as XLSX or CSV. A wire that only moved "
"on a page, or only got another number, is not listed. Labels for these "
"changes… opens Labels… on the labels the list names."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:343
msgid "Labels…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:345
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Lists the label files for your label program, one per "
"label set (wire markers, terminal markers, device labels…) and marker part, "
"each with how many labels it holds: every label, or only those changed since "
"a release. The line Label file says the form of the files (Excel unless the "
"project says CSV) and their columns; Change… sets the columns a file has, "
"their headings and order, Excel or CSV, and a CSV's delimiter, for the whole "
"project (also Project properties ▸ Workshop ▸ Label file). A line says how "
"many labels do not fit their marker part. Export… writes the ticked files "
"into a folder you choose. A CSV writes each value as it is, for your label "
"program's import; opened in a spreadsheet, a value that starts with `-`, `+` "
"or `=` is read as a formula, so open an Excel file there."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:353
msgid "Hole tables"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:355
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Shows the first page of the project's hole table: "
"every hole and cut-out of each surface of the cabinet (the mounting plate, a "
"door, a side, the top, the bottom), surface by surface, numbered in the "
"order they are made, with X and Y from the corner the surface's heading "
"names, how each is made and what it is for. A project without a hole table "
"opens Report block… on the hole table."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:360
msgid "Drilling…"
msgstr "Boren…"

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:362
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Exports the drawings for drilling: for each surface "
"with holes (ticked in a list that names its page, its holes and the sheets "
"its template takes) a template to print at full size (PDF) and a DXF for a "
"machine, on A4 or A3, into a folder (Drilling, beside the project). The "
"template lies in the surface's corner nearest its holes: lay its two thick "
"edges against the surface's edges, tape the sheets together at their crosses "
"and centre-punch each hole. Print it at actual size and measure its 100 mm "
"bar. Greyed while no surface has holes."
msgstr ""
"In de groep Werkplaats. Exporteert de boortekeningen: voor elk vlak met "
"gaten (aangevinkt in een lijst die zijn pagina, zijn gaten en de bladen van "
"zijn boormal noemt) een boormal om op ware grootte te printen (PDF) en een "
"DXF voor een machine, op A4 of A3, in een map (Boortekeningen, naast het "
"project). De boormal ligt in de hoek van het vlak die het dichtst bij zijn "
"gaten ligt: leg zijn twee dikke kanten tegen de kanten van het vlak, plak de "
"bladen aan elkaar op hun kruisen en körn elk gat. Print hem op ware grootte "
"en meet zijn balk van 100 mm. Grijs zolang geen vlak gaten heeft."

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:368
msgid "Workshop settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:370
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Shows the project's Properties at its Workshop rows: "
"the stock, the ferrules, two wires in one clamp, the marker text, the "
"marking of cores and jumpers, the device labels, the label file and the "
"holes: how a fixing hole in the plate is made, the rails' and the ducts' "
"fixings, the web between holes and the corner the holes are measured from."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:375
msgid "Package…"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:377
msgid ""
"In the Workshop group. Writes one ZIP file with what the workshop builds the "
"cabinet from: the drawings as one PDF, each workshop list as an Excel file, "
"the label files and the drawings for drilling, each in a folder of its kind, "
"with a Contents.txt that says what is in it and a manifest for a program. On "
"the left you untick what the shop does not need, and change the label file's "
"form; a line says what a member lacks, such as a marker part for cables in "
"the workshop stock. On the right the release check says what stands in the "
"way: an error stops a package of a released revision, a warning or an info "
"is shown and stops nothing; Show lists a finding's wires or devices, and its "
"button mends it. While a revision is open the package is a draft and every "
"page of its drawings says so. Without personal data leaves out the name and "
"the address of each party marked as personal data. The package is written as "
"a job, as Export PDF is, and a toast names the file with Show in folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:387
msgid "View"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:389
msgid "Back"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:391
msgid ""
"Goes back to the place shown before the last jump (a cross-reference "
"followed, a page opened)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:393
msgid "Forward"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:395
msgid "Goes forward again after **Back**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:397
msgid "Follow cross-reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:399
msgid ""
"Goes to the place the cross-reference under the pointer or selected names."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:401
msgid "Zoom in"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:403
msgid "Shows the page larger, around the middle of the view."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:405
msgid "Zoom out"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:407
msgid "Shows the page smaller."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:409
msgid "Actual size"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:411
msgid "Shows the page at its printed size."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:413
msgid "Fit page"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:415
msgid "Shows the whole page in the view."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:417
msgid "Side by side"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:419
msgid "Shows two pages side by side, each in its own group of tabs."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:421
msgid "Grid"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:423
msgid "Shows or hides the drawing grid on the page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:425
msgid "Theme"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:427
msgid ""
"Chooses the window's colours. See "
"[Appearance](../../how-to/application-window.md#appearance)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:429
msgid "Density"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:431
msgid "Chooses how close the window's rows and buttons stand."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:433
msgid "UI scale"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:435
msgid "Makes the whole window larger or smaller."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:437
msgid "Show tips"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:439
msgid ""
"Shows or hides the tips the window gives on the canvas and in empty panels."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:441
msgid "Frame statistics"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/command-bar.md:443
msgid ""
"Shows how long the window takes to draw each frame, for a report of a slow "
"window."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:3
msgid ""
"A dialog asks for a decision or a few values and closes when you answer. "
"`F1` in an open dialog opens its section, here or in [Use the application "
"window](../../how-to/application-window.md), which describes these dialogs:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:7
msgid ""
"About ElektraWatt and the third-party notices: "
"[About](../../how-to/application-window.md#about);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:8
msgid ""
"the keyboard shortcuts: [Keyboard "
"shortcuts](../../how-to/application-window.md#keyboard-shortcuts);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:9
msgid ""
"Save, Discard or Cancel before a change would be lost, and the recovery of "
"unsaved changes: [Unsaved changes and "
"recovery](../../how-to/application-window.md#unsaved-changes-and-recovery);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:11
msgid ""
"New project, Templates, Save as template and Check level: [Start a project "
"and use templates](../../how-to/start-a-project.md);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:13
msgid ""
"the name of a saved layout: "
"[Workspaces](../../how-to/application-window.md#workspaces);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:14
msgid ""
"the QElectroTech import: [Import a QElectroTech "
"project](../../how-to/application-window.md#import-a-qelectrotech-project)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:16
msgid "The keys of a dialog's buttons are on the [keymap](../keymaps.md) page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:18
msgid "Go to page or cell"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:20
msgid ""
"Opens a page by its folio, or a cell of a page by its folio and grid "
"reference, such as 102-B4. Open it with Page ▸ Go ▸ Go to page or cell…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:23
msgid "Form dialogs"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:25
msgid ""
"The fields of an action that needs values before it runs, such as Page ▸ New "
"page…, Project ▸ Setup ▸ Structure node… and Project ▸ Setup ▸ External "
"reference…. The primary button runs the action with the values; Cancel "
"changes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:29
msgid ""
"Page ▸ Title block fields…, also a link under the page's name in Properties, "
"lists every field the page's title block prints from the project's data, in "
"the title block's order: the page's title and description, the project "
"reference, the drawing and object IDs, the dates, the parties' names and the "
"revision. Apply writes each changed field where its value comes from in one "
"undo step: the description to the page when the page overrides it, else to "
"the project; a revision label typed in a project without a revision makes "
"that revision. Author and Checked are the names of the parties the current "
"revision names as its author and its checker: the form edits those names. An "
"open revision without an author takes the name typed as its author's (Set "
"revision author's step); Checked has a field only once the revision names "
"its checker, who is set when it is approved, and the form says so. A text "
"the title block's form holds itself (the fixed texts of an imported "
"QElectroTech template) is not a field, and the form says so under its "
"fields. The fields of the project's own that the title block prints stand by "
"their names, the page's first; the line under each says whether it has one "
"value for the project or one for each page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:41
msgid ""
"Project ▸ New field of your own…, also the last row of Project properties ▸ "
"Fields of your own, makes a value the title block prints that the program "
"does not know, such as an order number: its name, whether it has one value "
"for the whole project or one for each page, and its value now. Project "
"properties then hold each field of the whole project with its value, Page "
"properties each field of each page; a field's row menu has Rename… and "
"Remove, which is refused while a title block prints the field, naming the "
"pages."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:47
msgid "Revisions"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:49
msgid ""
"The revision dialogs of **Check ▸ Revision**. A revision is one issue of the "
"drawing set. **Set revision author…** names who drew it, **Approve "
"revision…** is the checker's sign-off of the saved project, and **Release "
"revision…** issues it: the release gate first runs the checks of the "
"project's check level on the saved project and lists what blocks the "
"release. **Withdraw revision…** takes a released revision back, with a "
"reason. **Settle the incomplete release…** completes or discards a release "
"that stopped half way."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:55
msgid "Purge unused images"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:57
msgid ""
"Lists the image files that no page uses any more and deletes them on Purge. "
"Undo does not bring them back. Open it with Project ▸ Purge unused images…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:60
msgid "Choose part"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:62
msgid ""
"The part selection: search the library, filter the results and choose the "
"part for the selected devices. Open it with Edit ▸ Choose part…. See [Choose "
"parts](../../how-to/choose-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:65
msgid "Print"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:67
msgid ""
"The print options beside a preview of the print set; Print… hands the set to "
"the printer, Open in PDF viewer opens it there. Open it with Output ▸ "
"Print…. See [Reports and PDF](../../tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:70
msgid "Changes on disk"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:72
msgid ""
"The project changed on disk while you have unsaved edits to the same fields: "
"each colliding field with its base, your value and the value on disk. Choose "
"a side per field and Apply, take all of one side, or decide later. It opens "
"by itself; File ▸ Check for changes on disk looks again."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:76
msgid "Upgrade the format"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:78
msgid ""
"The first save of a project in an older format: Upgrade and save writes a "
"backup beside the project, then saves in the current format; Cancel saves "
"nothing. It opens by itself. See [Upgrade a "
"project](../../how-to/upgrade-a-project.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:81
msgid "Re-import a matched file"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:83
msgid ""
"A QElectroTech file that matches an earlier import of this project by its "
"items: Re-import compares it with that import; Cancel stops. Open it with "
"Project ▸ Re-import QElectroTech project…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:86
msgid "Re-import QElectroTech project"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:88
msgid ""
"The re-import card: the records the source added, changed, changed on both "
"sides and removed, in words. Apply takes them over as one undo step; Cancel "
"changes nothing. The migration report then lists what needs a decision."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:91
msgid "Open .ewz"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:93
msgid ""
"An `.ewz` archive of a project: Extract and open unpacks it into a new "
"folder beside it, Open read-only opens it without unpacking. Open it with "
"File ▸ Import…, which takes a QElectroTech project as well."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:96
msgid "Import a drawing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:98
msgid ""
"A DXF or DWG drawing, or a PDF, chosen with File ▸ Import…. Add as page "
"makes the drawing a new graphic page after the last page. Place under page "
"puts it under the shown page as a locked underlay to redraw over: no check, "
"report or export reads it, unless an export prints underlays. An underlay is "
"kept outside the project unless you choose Place and embed; a customer's "
"drawing may be confidential. A drawing that names no unit is read in "
"millimetres. A DWG is read through the ODA File Converter, which ElektraWatt "
"does not include: install it, and choose its program when the import asks "
"for it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:105
msgid "Import EPLAN data"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:107
msgid ""
"An EPLAN part package (`.edz`), a parts XML (`.xml`) or a report spreadsheet "
"(`.xlsx`, `.csv`), chosen with File ▸ Import…. Before the first EDZ of a "
"session a notice says that the EPLAN Data Portal's terms restrict its data "
"to EPLAN products; ElektraWatt reads the file from your disk only, and "
"records the file and its SHA-256 on every part."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:111
msgid ""
"For parts, the dialog says how many parts the file holds, how many of them "
"the project has a copy of (matched by part number) and how many of those "
"copies would change. Update shows the changed fields and the devices that "
"use the copies before anything changes; one undo step reverts it. Add to my "
"parts library writes the parts into your own library, as a new version where "
"a part was there already. A part the project does not have is never added to "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:116
msgid ""
"For a report, the dialog offers the mappings that fit the file's column "
"headers: your saved one, and the shipped German and English ones for a "
"device list, a terminal diagram and a connection list. The result is a new "
"project without drawings. A file that no mapping fits is mapped column by "
"column (Map the columns…: the kind of report and what each column is; Import "
"saves the mapping, which the next file of the report is offered first), or "
"imported as a parts table."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:120
msgid "Set up merge driver"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:122
msgid ""
"Sets up the repository's git configuration so that git merges the project's "
"files record by record. It asks first and writes nothing on Cancel. Open it "
"with Project ▸ Set up merge driver."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:125
msgid "PLC import"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:127
msgid ""
"**PLC ▸ Import from PLC software…** asks for an AutomationML file or a TIA "
"Portal \"PLC Tags\" workbook and shows what it would change in the project's "
"configuration project, by tab: **Changed** (each field with the project's "
"and the file's value and what the import makes of it: applied, rejected "
"because the project owns it or the file leaves it out, kept because the "
"project changed it, or pending), **Conflicts** (both sides changed the "
"field: keep the project's value or take the file's; a comment without a "
"language never replaces one with languages, nor the other way round), "
"**Removed** (what the file no longer holds) and **Parking** (the file's "
"objects that match nothing in the project, or more than one; a module or "
"station can be created unplaced). Nothing changes until **Apply**, which "
"makes the import one step of Undo. **PLC ▸ Export to PLC software…** is a "
"form: the format (AutomationML AR APC 1.4.0 or 1.2.0, the TIA tag workbook, "
"PLCopen XML for CODESYS), the stations (their tags; empty: all) and the "
"document date (empty: the project's); it then asks for the file, writes it "
"whole or not at all, and says what the file holds. An AutomationML file or a "
"tag workbook is recorded as the base of the next import, for what it "
"carries; a PLCopen list never comes back and is not recorded. The file holds "
"no personal data."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:3
msgid ""
"The window's panes sit around the page view: the navigators on the left, the "
"property grid on the right, and the find pane, the jobs, the preview, the "
"history and the glossary in the bottom dock. View ▸ Panels shows and hides "
"them. `F1` over a pane's header or in a focused pane opens its section here. "
"The keys of each pane are on the [keymap](../keymaps.md) page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:8
msgid "Panes"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:10
msgid "Page view"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:12
msgid ""
"The canvas: the open pages as tabs, one or two groups side by side (**View ▸ "
"Side by side**). The tools of the Draw tab act on it; see [Tools](tools.md), "
"and what a page holds, [Things on a page](things.md). **Zoom in**, **Zoom "
"out**, **Fit page** and **Grid** are in the View menu; the mouse wheel zooms "
"(or scrolls, as the View menu sets). **Follow cross-reference** goes to the "
"place a cross-reference names, **Back** and **Forward** go through the "
"places you came from, and **Highlight** (`Ctrl+Shift+H`) marks the thing of "
"a list's row on the pages until **Clear highlights**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:19
msgid "Properties"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:21
msgid ""
"The property grid: the members of the selection, by category, each editable "
"in place. A click on a category's header, or Enter on it, folds the "
"category. The pointer resting on a row's label explains the row. A derived "
"value's chip names where it comes from, such as the profile or the "
"potential. A row's menu holds the actions of that member, such as Choose "
"part on the Parts row, Go to source on a derived value, and Mark not used on "
"a device's connection point. A wire and an interruption point show their "
"potential's definition, where its type and voltage are set, or Define "
"potential… when it has none. View ▸ Panels ▸ Properties shows it; Edit ▸ "
"Properties of selection moves the focus to it. See [Choose "
"parts](../../how-to/choose-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:29
msgid "Find"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:31
msgid ""
"The find pane: search texts, tags and fields of the project, and replace "
"them. Open it with Edit ▸ Find… or Edit ▸ Find and replace…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:34
msgid "Jobs"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:36
msgid ""
"The jobs: the PDF exports that run in the background, with their progress "
"and results. Open it with Output ▸ Jobs. See [Reports and "
"PDF](../../tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:39
msgid "Preview"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:41
msgid ""
"The preview of the open card: what a command, a quick fix or a paste would "
"change, before you apply it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:43
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:45
msgid ""
"The undo steps of the project and the marker lines of exports and "
"recoveries; undo to any step from here. Open it with View ▸ Panels ▸ History."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:48
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:50
msgid ""
"The terms of EW with their EPLAN and QElectroTech names. Open it with Help ▸ "
"Glossary. See [EPLAN and EW](../../explanation/eplan-and-ew.md) and "
"[QElectroTech and EW](../../explanation/qet-and-ew.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:53
msgid "Command bar"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:55
msgid ""
"The tabs and groups of commands at the top of the window: Project, Page, "
"Draw, Check, Output and View. View ▸ Layout ▸ Collapse command bar folds it "
"to its tab row."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:58
msgid "Strip editor"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:60
msgid ""
"The Terminal strips navigator in table mode for one strip: insert, delete "
"and move terminals, add accessories and edit the cells of the terminal rows. "
"Open it with Edit ▸ Strip editor with a strip selected. See [A terminal "
"strip and a cable](../../tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:64
msgid "Table mode"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:66
msgid ""
"A navigator shown as a table: one row per record and a column per member, "
"edited cell by cell; fill down, pattern fill and paste work on several cells "
"at once. The navigator's Table button switches it. Output ▸ Export table… "
"and Output ▸ Import table… exchange the table with a spreadsheet."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:70
msgid "Navigators"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:72
msgid ""
"A navigator lists one kind of record of the project. View ▸ Panels ▸ "
"Navigators shows each one; clicking a row selects the record and opens its "
"page. A right click on a row selects it and opens its menu: what the same "
"thing offers on the page, listed below for each navigator."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:76
msgid "Pages"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:78
msgid "The pages in folio order, flat, by section or by structure."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:80
msgid ""
"The two buttons beside the filter make a page and a section; a right click "
"on a section offers the same, and Edit section… changes that section's title "
"and folio range (Project properties link each section to it as well). A "
"right click on a page opens it and offers Open page in a new tab, Title "
"block fields…, Page properties, New page… and Delete page. A section is a "
"range of folios with a title, such as 100 to 199: New section proposes the "
"next number and the next hundred, and the tree lists the section at once, "
"still empty. New page fills in the next free folio: after the page you "
"right-clicked, inside its section; after the last page of a section you "
"right-clicked (its first folio when it is empty); else, in the section for "
"pages of the kind you choose (the one that holds such pages, or whose title "
"says so: Pneumatics for a fluid page), after the open page. Place offers the "
"end of the section instead. The folio stays yours to change: the dialog says "
"where the number in the field puts the page (\"Stands after 101 Wiring, "
"before 105 Controls\") and which page has a folio already taken; a number "
"you typed stays when you change the kind."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:91
msgid "Devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:93
msgid ""
"The devices with their functions and representations. A right click on a "
"device offers Go to (its first placement, such as `201-E4`), what its symbol "
"offers on the page (Add contact on a relay or a contactor, Choose part…, "
"Edit symbol, Copy and edit) and Properties of selection."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:97
msgid "Connections"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:99
msgid "The connections, the wires drawn on the pages."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:103
msgid ""
"The terminal strips with their terminals. Its table mode is the strip editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:105
msgid "Cables"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:107
msgid ""
"The cables with their cores. A right click on a cable offers Cable…, which "
"shows the page of its first wire with the cable's wires selected and opens "
"the cable's form, and Go to its first wire. See [A terminal strip and a "
"cable](../../tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:113
msgid ""
"The PLC configuration projects, stations, modules and channels, with their "
"addresses. A station lists its modules under their racks once a module has a "
"rack position, and its bus ports after its modules. A right click on a "
"station or a module offers what a device's row offers; on a channel, Go to "
"its place and Properties of selection."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:117
msgid ""
"A station that is its own module (a compact device, a valve terminal with a "
"bus node) is one row with its channels under it. A station or a module drawn "
"on no page carries the badge **not drawn**: its tag shows `?` where the page "
"goes. A module that no station takes (a project with two stations, the "
"module drawn on a page of neither) is listed under **Modules without a "
"station**. A right click on a station's row offers **Rack…** and **Attach "
"modules without a station**, which makes them that station's modules in one "
"step. The panel's **+** and its empty state offer **New station…**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:124
msgid ""
"The filter takes the words `spare`, `unplaced`, `unassigned`, `tbd`, "
"`duplicate` and `safety` (a channel or module with safety data), "
"`config:<name>` for a configuration project and `bus:<net>` for the bus net "
"of a station's port, its name written with hyphens for spaces "
"(`bus:profibus-dp`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:130
msgid ""
"The PLC navigator's channels as a table, in the bottom dock of the [PLC "
"workspace](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md): address, target, symbol, function "
"text, designation, module, channel, kind, data type, signal range, safety, "
"process tag, state, the page it is placed on, station, rack, slot and "
"comment. The designation, address, symbol, state, target, comment, data type "
"and signal range are edited in their cells, the slot in its cell as a number "
"or `<rack>.<slot>`; a channel's safety data is edited in Properties. Its "
"filter takes the PLC navigator's words."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:136
msgid "Bus"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:138
msgid ""
"The project's bus networks, on the rail as **Bus**: **Logical nets**, each "
"with its rate and its ports (the master port marked), **Physical nets**, "
"each with its segments as the program derives them from the drawn bus lines, "
"each segment's members in order from its start (a port at an end marked "
"_end_, a connector's termination as _terminated_ or _not terminated_, the "
"cables between them), and **Ports without a net**. Selecting a net shows its "
"name, bus, rate and master in Properties; selecting a port shows its "
"interface, role, address, termination and nets."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:144
msgid ""
"As a table each port is a row: device, interface, address, termination, bus, "
"role and nets. Type an address into its cell as the row shows it: a number "
"for a PROFIBUS station, a PROFINET device name with its IP address (`cpx-1 "
"192.168.0.10`, then `mask` and `gateway` with theirs), `pos 2` and `alias "
"1001` for EtherCAT, `port 3` for an IO-Link port, or `TBD`; an empty cell is "
"a port without an address. The termination cell takes on or off."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:151
msgid ""
"The fluid side of the project, on the rail as **Fluid**: **Valve "
"terminals**, each with its modules in position order and their valves, then "
"**Fluid devices** (cylinders, grippers and the other devices a tube ends "
"at), each device with its tubes (`→` and the port at the tube's other end), "
"then **Media**, each with the number of tubes its zones hold. Selecting a "
"tube selects it on the page; in table mode the panel lists the tubes with "
"the tube table's columns. Beside the filter stand **Fluid settings…** (the "
"default tube and tube tags) and **New medium…**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:159
msgid ""
"The tube list as a table, in the bottom dock of the [Fluid "
"workspace](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md): one row per tube with its ends "
"and their port roles, medium, line kind, size, material, colour, tube part, "
"length and where the length comes from, fittings, a change of location and "
"its page. Type a tube part into **Tube part** (a part of the project) and a "
"length in millimetres into **Length**; an empty cell goes back to the "
"derived value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:166
msgid ""
"The project's media, one row per medium, with the ports that feed it, its "
"pressure, quality, the devices it reaches and its default tube. With a row "
"selected, **Highlight** (`Ctrl+Shift+H`) marks the medium's zones on the "
"page in a colour and a hatch pattern; **Check ▸ Clear highlights** removes "
"them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:170
msgid "Potentials"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:172
msgid ""
"The potentials with their definitions and members. See [Define a supply "
"potential](../../how-to/define-supply-potential.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:175 src/reference/window/things.md:70
msgid "Interruption points"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:177
msgid "The interruption points grouped by name, with their partners."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:179
msgid "Structure identifiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:181
msgid "The function and location nodes of the project's structure."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:183
msgid "Parts in use"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:185
msgid ""
"The parts the project uses, with their devices. See [Choose "
"parts](../../how-to/choose-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:187
msgid "Report blocks"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:189
msgid ""
"The report blocks: the generated pages such as the terminal diagram, the "
"cable list and the parts list. See [Reports and "
"PDF](../../tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:192
msgid "Unplaced devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:194
msgid ""
"The devices of each layout space that have no place on a rail or the plate "
"yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:196
msgid "Macros"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:198
msgid "The macros of every library tier and of the project."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:200
msgid "Libraries"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:202
msgid ""
"The installed libraries and the project's library copies with their state."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:204
msgid "Messages"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:206
msgid ""
"The findings of the project's checks, by severity and rule. Check ▸ Next "
"finding and Check ▸ Previous finding walk them; `F1` on a finding opens its "
"rule's page in the [rule reference](../rules/index.md). Open it with Check ▸ "
"Messages."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:3
msgid ""
"What a page holds, one section for each kind of thing: what it is, how you "
"make one, how you change it, and where its rows of Properties are explained. "
"Point at a thing on the page, or select it, and press `F1` to open its "
"section here. The rows of Properties have their own page: "
"[Properties](../properties.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:7
msgid "Devices and symbols"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:9
msgid ""
"A device is one thing of the cabinet or the machine, such as a contactor "
"-K1, a motor -M1 or a lamp -E2. A symbol draws the device, or a part of it, "
"on a page; a device can have several. A contactor has its coil on one page "
"and its contacts where they switch, and each contact names the place of its "
"coil under it, the coil the places of its contacts."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:14
msgid ""
"To make one: **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** places a symbol and makes its "
"device, **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** places a device with its part "
"from the start ([Tools](tools.md#symbol)). A right click on a relay's or a "
"contactor's coil offers **Add contact**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:18
msgid ""
"To change one: select it and change its rows in Properties: its tag, its "
"part (**Choose part…**), what it does. A right click offers Rotate, Mirror, "
"**Edit symbol** and **Join device…**. Its rows are explained under "
"[Device](../properties.md#device), [Function](../properties.md#function) and "
"[Representation](../properties.md#representation)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:23
msgid ""
"To keep a symbol where it stands: select it, right-click and choose **Keep "
"in place**, or tick **Keep in place** in Properties. Arrange and route then "
"leaves it where it is and arranges the other symbols around it. It does not "
"hold the symbol for you: you can still move it yourself, and Route still "
"routes its wires. A small pin shows on it while it is selected. Choose "
"**Keep in place** again, with its check showing, to let it go."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:28
msgid "Wires"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:30
msgid ""
"A wire joins two connection points. A junction, where a wire branches with a "
"bevel (or a dot, if you ask for one), joins three. A wire's number, "
"cross-section and colour come from its potential and the project's profile "
"until you give it its own."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:34
msgid ""
"To make one: **Draw ▸ Wire** (`W`), then a click on a start point and one on "
"the end point ([Wire](tools.md#wire))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:37
msgid ""
"To change one: select it. The selected wire shows a ring at each of its two "
"ends and round handles; drag a handle to change its route ([Change a wire's "
"route by hand](tools.md#change-a-wires-route-by-hand)), or choose **Route** "
"to route it again ([Route](tools.md#route)). Its number, colour and "
"cross-section are its rows in Properties, explained under "
"[Connection](../properties.md#connection); click one to give the wire its "
"own value. The Cross-section row says where its size comes from, and why no "
"protective device sizes it when none does (no supply, or no protective "
"device upstream)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:45
msgid "Cables and their marks"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:47
msgid ""
"A cable bundles wires: each wire of it is one of its cores, with the core's "
"colour and cross-section. The cable's mark on the page is the line across "
"its wires, a slanted stroke on each core with the core's name beside its "
"wire, and the cable's tag with its type at the line's start."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:51
msgid ""
"To make one: select the wires, then **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Cable…**. Its form "
"proposes the tag and lists the cores; its **Cable type** lists the cable "
"parts that fit, and **New cable type…** makes a new one. The mark is placed "
"at once ([A terminal strip and a "
"cable](../../tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:55
msgid ""
"To change one: drag the mark along the wires, or select it and drag its tag. "
"The cable's length, type and cores are its rows in Properties, explained "
"under [Device](../properties.md#device); the Cables navigator lists every "
"cable with its cores ([Cables](panels.md#cables))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:59
msgid "Terminals and terminal strips"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:61
msgid ""
"A terminal is a point where a wire is clamped, on a terminal strip such as "
"-X1. Each terminal of a strip has its number (-X1:1, -X1:2) and its kind: "
"feed-through, protective earth and others."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:64
msgid ""
"To make one: **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Terminal…** lays a row of terminals of one "
"strip ([Terminal](tools.md#terminal)). A right click on a terminal offers "
"**Add terminal**, the strip's next number."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:67
msgid ""
"To change one: select it and change its rows in Properties, or choose **Edit "
"▸ Strip editor** for the whole strip as a table ([Strip "
"editor](panels.md#strip-editor)). Its rows are explained under "
"[Device](../properties.md#device)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:72
msgid ""
"An interruption point ends a potential on this page; it goes on at the point "
"of the same name on another page. The arrow points the way the signal flows: "
"a source at the page's right edge, its target at the left edge of another "
"page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:75
msgid ""
"To make one: **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Interruption point**, type its name and "
"click where it goes ([Interruption point](tools.md#interruption-point)). "
"**Move to page…** makes them for you, on the Page tab beside Copy pages… and "
"in the right-click menu of selected symbols: it asks for the page (another "
"schematic page, or a new one), the selected symbols go there, and each wire "
"to a symbol that stays behind becomes a pair of interruption points named by "
"its potential, the source where the wire left the page, the target at the "
"moved pin. A preview lists the pairs first; **Apply** moves, one Undo takes "
"it back. Texts and boxes you selected with the symbols stay on the page, and "
"the preview says so. A net that runs through two junctions is refused: move "
"it by hand."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:83
msgid ""
"To change one: select it and change its name or its direction in Properties. "
"A right click offers **Go to partner** and **Continue on page…**. Its rows "
"are explained under [Function](../properties.md#function)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:86
msgid "Supply points"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:88
msgid ""
"A supply point is an interruption point where the supply comes in from "
"another drawing set: L1, L2, L3, N and PE from a main distribution, say. It "
"prints where the supply comes from instead of a partner on another page. "
"Supply points that stand together, one under the other, print that place "
"once."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:92
msgid ""
"To make one: **Supply** on the Draw tab, beside Interruption, asks what "
"comes in (single phase L, N, PE; three phase with or without N; 24 V DC; "
"your own names) and where it comes from, and the marks follow the pointer as "
"one group until you click. A mark brought near a pin lands on it and joins "
"it when you click, as an interruption point does; the bar under the page "
"names the pin first (`L connects to -Q1:1`). Hold Alt to place the marks "
"without joining. Or draw the points with Interruption, select them, "
"right-click and choose **Supply comes in here…**; its form proposes where "
"the supply comes from and the device there. The same marks bring in signals "
"from another cabinet: name each mark for its signal and leave a field empty "
"where it is not known. A mark prints what is known: the place and the device "
"(`+MCC2 / -A1`), an address alone when only the device field holds it "
"(`Q4.1`), or nothing when the far end is not known yet. When the marks are "
"placed, a small panel opens at them and asks what comes in: a row per mark, "
"with the kind of conductor (L1, L2, L3, L, N, PE, L+, M, …) and the voltage "
"with AC or DC (`230 V AC`), filled in from the mark's name where the name "
"tells it (`L1-EXT` is a phase at the project supply's voltage, `N` the "
"neutral, `PE` protective earth, `24V` a 24 V DC plus, `0V`, `0VDC` or `M` "
"its minus). Enter defines every row's potential in one step; Escape places "
"the marks without one. A mark on a potential that has its type already is "
"not asked. **Potential…** on the marks' right-click menu opens the panel "
"again to change it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:106
msgid ""
"To change one: the place and the device are its rows in Properties; a value "
"typed there moves the point and its group. A right click offers **Group "
"these supply points**, **Take out of its group** and **Remove the supply "
"mark**. Its rows are explained under [Function](../properties.md#function)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:110
msgid "PLC cards and stations"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:112
msgid ""
"A PLC station is a CPU, a head station or another device with a rack; its "
"cards (modules) sit in the rack's slots, each with its channels. On a page a "
"card is drawn as a box with its channels' pins, and a station as the box of "
"its head."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:116
msgid ""
"To make one: **New station…** on the PLC tab, then put cards into its rack "
"in the station's view ([Make a "
"station](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#make-a-station)). **Draw on page** in "
"the view draws a card on a page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:119
msgid ""
"To change one: open the station's view from the PLC navigator, or select the "
"card and change its rows in Properties ([Read and change a "
"rack](../../how-to/plc-workspace.md#read-and-change-a-rack))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:122
msgid "Valves and cylinders"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:124
msgid ""
"A valve, a cylinder and the other parts of a pneumatic or hydraulic circuit "
"stand on a fluid page with their ports. A valve worked by a solenoid has the "
"solenoid's coil on an electrical page too."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:127
msgid ""
"To make one: open the Fluid tab on a fluid page and choose the valve or the "
"cylinder ([Draw a circuit](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md#draw-a-circuit))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:130
msgid ""
"To change one: select it; its medium and its solenoid are rows in Properties "
"([The solenoid of a "
"valve](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md#the-solenoid-of-a-valve))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:133
msgid "Tubes"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:135
msgid ""
"A tube joins the ports of fluid parts, the way a wire joins connection "
"points. A pilot or drain tube is dashed, a hose is drawn as an arc."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:138
msgid "To make one: **Tube** on the Fluid tab ([Tube](tools.md#tube))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:140
msgid ""
"To change one: right-click it for its line kind; its tube and length are its "
"rows in Properties ([Tubes and the tube "
"list](../../how-to/fluid-workspace.md#tubes-and-the-tube-list))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:143
msgid "Texts"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:145
msgid ""
"A free text is a text of its own on the page, such as a note; it can show a "
"value of the project, such as its title. The texts of a device, its tag and "
"its function text, belong to its symbol."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:148
msgid "To make one: **Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Text** ([Text](tools.md#text))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:150
msgid ""
"To change one: select it and change its rows in Properties (a double click "
"opens them). Drag a device's tag to move it beside its symbol. How big texts "
"print is set for the whole project in **Text styles…** ([Text "
"styles](../../how-to/start-a-project.md#text-styles))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:154
msgid "Graphics and images"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:156
msgid ""
"A graphic is a line, a rectangle, a circle or another shape without "
"electrical meaning; an image is a picture file on the page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:159
msgid ""
"To make one: **Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Graphic** or **Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Image…** "
"([Graphic](tools.md#graphic))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:161
msgid "To change one: select it and drag it, or change its rows in Properties."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:163
msgid "Boxes"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:165
msgid ""
"A structure box is the dashed frame round devices that belong to one "
"function or one location of the project, such as one machine part. A black "
"box is a device drawn as a rectangle with its connection points, for a "
"device the library has no symbol for."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:169
msgid ""
"To make one: **Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Structure box** ([Structure "
"box](tools.md#structure-box)) or **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Black box…** ([Black "
"box](tools.md#black-box))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:172
msgid ""
"To change one: select it; a selected black box shows grips on its corners "
"and connection points."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:174
msgid "The frame and title block"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:176
msgid ""
"The frame round the page, with its column numbers and row letters (the cells "
"such as 101-B6 that cross-references name) and the title block at the "
"bottom. The frame comes from the project's template."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:179
msgid ""
"To change it: right-click the title block and choose **Title block fields…** "
"for the texts it prints, such as the drawing's title and who drew it; a "
"page's own texts are its **Page properties**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:182
msgid "Lists on a page"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:184
msgid ""
"A list is a page the program writes: the parts list, the wire list, the "
"terminal diagram, the cable list, the list of PLC cards or of tubes. It is "
"filled from the drawing every time, so you do not type into it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:187
msgid ""
"To make one: **Report** on the Output tab (**Output ▸ Reports ▸ Report "
"block…**) ([Reports and PDF](../../tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:189
msgid ""
"To change one: change the drawing; the Report blocks navigator lists the "
"lists ([Report blocks](panels.md#report-blocks))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/things.md:191
msgid ""
"What a list prints, on one of its pages: **Output ▸ List columns…** ticks "
"the columns to print and gives each its order, a heading of its own and its "
"width (a share of the table's width), with a caption above the table and a "
"legend under it; **Output ▸ Rows of your own…** adds rows the drawing does "
"not hold, which the list keeps and sorts with its own. A list prints in the "
"project's language (its Languages row in **Project ▸ Project properties**), "
"and on the set's paper: a set whose frame is A3 prints its lists on A3. A "
"script sets the same with `fields.set` on the report block (paths `columns`, "
"`caption`, `legend` and `added_rows`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:3
msgid ""
"Open **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol editor…**. A symbol draft has its own undo "
"history, drawing, function, connection points, text fields and "
"cross-reference point. Changes do not alter existing placements. Press "
"**F1** in the editor to open this reference."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:7
msgid "The screen"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:9
msgid ""
"<a id=\"the-start-card\"></a>With nothing in hand the editor shows a **start "
"card**: **New symbol**, **Copy a library symbol…** (a list of the project's "
"and the libraries' symbols with a filter; the original stays as it is), "
"**Device box for a part…** ([below](#the-device-box)), **Import a file…**, "
"the draft in hand and the symbols edited lately. **Start** in the command "
"bar returns to it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:14
msgid "With a symbol in hand the screen is built as the main window is:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:16
msgid ""
"**The command bar** holds the tools (**Select**, **Line**, **Rectangle**, "
"**Circle**, **More shapes** with ellipse, arc, polyline and polygon, "
"**Text**, **Connection point**, **Text field**, and **Other marks** with the "
"cross-reference point and the mechanical link point), the **Drawing grid** "
"and **Snap to grid**, and at the right end **Test on a page**, **Save**, the "
"**Draft** menu (**Make a part for this symbol…**, **Export draft**, "
"**Discard draft**) and **Close editor**. Each tool's tooltip says what it "
"does and how."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:21
msgid ""
"<a id=\"the-outline\"></a>**The outline**, on the left, lists what the "
"symbol consists of: its connection points (designation, and the potential "
"each carries), its texts and its shapes. A click on a row selects the thing "
"in the drawing, and the other way round. **To do** lists what keeps the "
"symbol from being saved, each in a sentence and each a click to the thing it "
"is about; with nothing left it says **Ready to save**. An import with more "
"drawings lists them first, and an import's report stands under the lists."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:26
msgid ""
"**The drawing**, in the middle, with the symbol **Turned and mirrored** "
"under it: A is the drawing you edit, B to H show it turned and mirrored."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:28
msgid ""
"**Properties**, on the right, shows the rows of what is selected: a shape's "
"place and size, a text's words, style, alignment and rotation, a connection "
"point's designation, potential, direction and place. Several selected things "
"show the rows they share, and a change goes to all of them. With nothing "
"selected it shows the symbol's own rows: **Name**, **What it is**, "
"**Discipline**, **Listed under** and the **Library folder**; the rows a rare "
"case needs stand under **More**. A row commits when you press Enter or leave "
"it, so partially typed numbers do not reshape the drawing; Escape cancels "
"the entry."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:35
msgid "Selecting and moving"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:37
msgid ""
"What you know from a page works on the drawing. With **Select**: a click "
"selects a thing, `Shift` adds and `Ctrl` toggles; a rectangle dragged from "
"empty paper selects the things inside it when dragged to the right and the "
"things it touches when dragged to the left; `Ctrl+A` selects everything; a "
"click on empty paper or `Esc` clears the selection. A shape that is not "
"filled is taken by its line, a filled one by its face: a click or a drag "
"inside an empty box is on empty paper. A drag on a thing moves the "
"selection, with an outline of where it lands; the arrow keys move it one "
"grid step. A connection point moves on the connection grid, and so does a "
"selection that holds one; anything else moves by the drawing grid's step. "
"`Delete` removes the selection (a connection point with the text that writes "
"its number), `Ctrl+C`, `Ctrl+X` and `Ctrl+V` copy, cut and paste, `Ctrl+D` "
"duplicates, and `Ctrl+Z` and `Ctrl+Y` step the editor's own history. The one "
"selected shape shows handles on its ends or corners: drag one to reshape it. "
"With several things selected, **Line up** in Properties puts them on one "
"edge. Each change is one undo step."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:49
msgid "Drawing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:51
msgid ""
"<a id=\"a-shape\"></a>A line, a rectangle, a circle, an ellipse and an arc "
"are drawn by one drag or by two clicks; the circle takes its centre and a "
"point on its circumference, arcs and ellipses opposite corners of their box. "
"A polyline and a polygon take a click per corner and a double-click on the "
"last. **Text**: click where the text goes, type it and press Enter "
"(`Shift+Enter` starts a new line). What a tool drew is selected, so its rows "
"in Properties take exact numbers, an arc's angles, a shape's **Fill shape** "
"or a text's **Text style**. <a id=\"a-connection-point\"></a>**Connection "
"point** places a point on the connection grid; the card at the drawing's top "
"left holds its designation, which counts on, and its direction: **Along its "
"lead**, the first, faces the way the line it ends points, so a wire carries "
"straight on from it. **Text field** places a text the page fills in: the "
"device's tag, its function text, its technical data, its cross-reference, or "
"the number of a connection point. `Esc` ends a half-drawn shape, then "
"returns to **Select**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:61
msgid ""
"**Cross-reference point** (under **Other marks**) sets the point used to "
"calculate the symbol's cross-reference cell on the project page. It does not "
"move the symbol's origin, the small cross it is held by when it is placed. "
"Place it on the left edge for a supply block. **Mechanical link point** adds "
"a named point where a dashed mechanical link attaches; it makes no "
"electrical connection."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:66
msgid ""
"Native connections must lie on the 4 mm grid. **Drawing grid** chooses the "
"step that lines, shapes, texts and link points snap to (0.5, 1, 2 or 4 mm, "
"or QET 10 px; 0.5 mm at first), and **Snap to grid** turns that snapping "
"off. The drawing shows the drawing grid's dots and, larger, the connection "
"grid's. A connection point keeps to the connection grid whatever is chosen. "
"The choice stays for the session. A connection point typed off the grid "
"stays where it was typed: it shows under **To do** and blocks the save."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:72
msgid "What the symbol is"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:74
msgid ""
"**What it is** asks what the symbol stands for, in a panel builder's words: "
"a device box, a normally open contact, a coil, a protective device, a "
"pressure, position or temperature switch, an indicator light, a heater, a "
"terminal. It decides how the program lists and checks the symbol's device (a "
"contact is listed with its coil, a terminal in the terminal diagram) and "
"where the browsers list the symbol, which **Listed under** changes on its "
"own. **Discipline** says what the symbol is drawn for (electrical, "
"pneumatic, vacuum, hydraulic or bus); one connection point can differ, under "
"**More** in its rows."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:81
msgid "Function type and ready-made functions"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:83
msgid ""
"Under **More**, **Function type** shows the program's own word for what "
"**What it is** chose, and **Ready-made function** takes a function of a "
"library when one fits: it brings its type and its connection points, so you "
"do not enter them yourself, and each connection point's **Function pin** row "
"(under **More**) says which of its pins the point draws. While a ready-made "
"function is used the type, the category and the pins' names are locked; "
"choosing **None** unlocks them and keeps the function's type and connection "
"points. Repeated designations have an ordinal in parentheses, so two "
"terminals both named N remain distinguishable."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:90
msgid ""
"**Whole document (for experts)**, under the symbol's rows, shows the "
"complete draft as text, including what the rows do not show (other text "
"arrangements, an import's extra members). See the [design "
"standard](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/design/07a-symbol-design-standard.md) "
"for the full authoring profile."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:95
msgid "Saving, testing and closing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:97
msgid ""
"**Save** writes the symbol and its own function to the library named by "
"**Library folder** (a `.ewlib` folder; a name without a folder is a library "
"in your user library folder, and **Choose folder…** picks one). **Make a "
"part for this symbol…**, in the **Draft** menu, then opens Parts on a new "
"part drawn with it: its identity, network ports and PLC data are typed there "
"([Make your own parts](../how-to/own-parts.md)), and a device placed from "
"the part is drawn with the symbol. The window remembers that folder. Its "
"symbols appear under **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** (`I`), with the library "
"name; the page must use the symbol's connection grid. Already embedded "
"copies stay at their existing version until you explicitly update them. "
"Repeated placement keeps the authored outline and pins. A new symbol takes "
"its key from its name (`user.power-block` for **Power block**), and the key "
"follows the name until you type one. The first save gives the draft its own "
"identity, and later saves of that draft update the same symbol: changed "
"content is saved as the next patch version (1.0.1 after 1.0.0), and a "
"version you type below the library's is refused. The symbol's own function "
"keeps its version while it is unchanged. A library that already holds a key "
"you typed for a different symbol refuses the save: choose another key, or "
"copy that symbol to change it; a key made from the name takes the next free "
"number instead (`user.power-block-2`). A save says where the symbol went, "
"and a refused one says why above the drawing and under **To do**. Saving a "
"draft under another key than its symbol's makes a new symbol, and a message "
"says so; the symbol with the earlier key stays. **Export draft** writes "
"portable JSON to a new file, including incomplete work; an existing file is "
"never overwritten. **Close editor** keeps the draft and its undo history: "
"**Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol editor…** returns to it. Only **Discard draft** "
"drops it. While the draft has unsaved changes, a way in of the start card, "
"another drawing of an import and **Discard draft** first ask whether to save "
"the symbol. While the editor shows, Ctrl+S saves the symbol and the File "
"menu's **Save project** saves the project. Quitting with unsaved symbol "
"changes asks whether to save the symbol. **Test on a page** creates a page "
"in the current project with the symbol's connection grid and places embedded "
"copies there. The page then shows with the copy selected; the message's "
"**Back to the editor** returns to the draft. Project undo removes the "
"placement; a second undo removes the new page. Test placements are "
"independent draft copies, so **Only in this project** in the Libraries "
"navigator refers to their temporary draft source. To place a copy linked to "
"the published library, use **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** after saving."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:125
msgid "The device box"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:127
msgid ""
"A device that is a box with pins (a circuit protector, a power supply, a "
"drive, a gateway) needs no drawing: its part says which pins it has and "
"where they stand on its box. The editor edits that box on the same screen. "
"Its ways in are **Device box for a part…** on the start card (every part "
"drawn as a box, of the project and of the libraries, placed or not, or a new "
"part), **Pins and box…** in Parts and under such a part in the device "
"browser, and **Edit this device's box…** in the menu of a box placed on a "
"page. A part you cannot change (a starter or sample part) opens as a copy of "
"your own, and a line over the box says what Save makes: the project's own "
"copy, which its devices take, or, for a part not yet in the project, a new "
"part in your own library. The original stays as it is."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:135
msgid ""
"**The pin table**, on the left, lists the pins in the order they stand on "
"the box, each with its name, the potential it carries and its edge. **Add "
"pin** adds one; a row dragged onto another takes its place there. **Add bus "
"interface…** opens Parts on **Network ports** with a new interface (a "
"PROFIBUS or PROFINET port, say); the box then takes its ports from its "
"interfaces as every bus device's does. A library's part keeps the interfaces "
"its library gives it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:140
msgid ""
"**The drawing** shows the box as a device of the part is drawn. Drag a pin "
"along its edge or onto another edge; drag any edge or corner of the box to "
"size it: the pins on that edge go with it, the others stay where they are, "
"and the box gets no smaller than its pins need. A click selects a pin, "
"`Shift` and `Ctrl` select more; the arrow keys move the selected pins one "
"grid step along their edge and `Delete` removes them; `Ctrl+C` and `Ctrl+V` "
"copy them and paste pins of the same names, each last on its edge."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:145
msgid ""
"<a id=\"a-pin-of-the-box\"></a>**Properties** shows the selected pin's "
"**Name**, **Potential**, **Edge** and its place **Along the edge**; with no "
"pin selected the part's number, manufacturer and description (typed here for "
"a new part; **Part data…** opens the part in Parts for the rest) and the "
"box's width and height."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:149
msgid ""
"**Save** saves the part: a new part into your parts library and the project, "
"a part of your own in place, and a library's part as the project's own copy, "
"which the devices that used it follow. From then on **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device "
"from part…** places the part's box with one click, laid out as you drew it; "
"on the page its edges and pins can still be dragged. Where boxes of the part "
"are drawn already, the message of the save says how many and offers **Redraw "
"them**, here or back on the page; the message then says how many were "
"redrawn. **Test on a page** saves the box and arms the Device tool with the "
"part. **Close box** returns where you opened the box: the page, or the "
"editor's ways in, where a symbol you were drawing waits to be continued."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:157
msgid ""
"A PLC card takes its pins from its channels, a bus device its box on a bus "
"page from its interfaces and ports, and a part drawn with a symbol its "
"connections from that symbol: the editor shows such a part's box and says "
"where its pins come from."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:161
msgid "AI and command-line tools"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:163
msgid "No remote service or generated code is required. Use `ew symbol`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:165
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"ew symbol template example.power \"Power block\" > power.symbol.json\n"
"ew symbol schema > symbol-authoring.schema.json\n"
"ew symbol validate power.symbol.json\n"
"ew symbol publish power.symbol.json --library symbols.ewlib\n"
"ew symbol import element.elmt --key example.import > imported-drafts.json\n"
"ew symbol import manufacturer.edz --key example.import > "
"imported-drafts.json\n"
"ew symbol import custom.slk --key example.import > imported-drafts.json\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:175
msgid ""
"Template and schema print JSON. Validation prints stable issue codes, JSON "
"pointers and details, exiting 0 when valid, 1 for validation findings, or 2 "
"for unreadable/malformed input. Import prints an **array** of drafts; choose "
"one object and save it as a `.symbol.json` file. QET conversion findings are "
"retained in `import_details` with their original message, code and source "
"line, so tools can identify each loss. Publication validates again and uses "
"the ordinary library writer's lock, version and transaction protections. It "
"does not save the current project. Without `--key`, import uses the file's "
"name as the key. Publication prints `result` (`added`, `updated` or "
"`unchanged`) and the draft's `identity`; copy that `identity` into the draft "
"file to publish later versions of the same symbol. A draft without it is a "
"new symbol, refused (exit 2) when the library holds its key for another one. "
"Validation reports a member it does not know as `symbol-unknown-member`, "
"which blocks publication."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:185
msgid ""
"The portable envelope uses `format: \"ew.symbol/1\"`, `key`, `version`, "
"`symbol`, `function`, optional `identity`, `function_ref`, `import_notes`, "
"`import_details` and `import_reviewed`. File coordinates are integer "
"**nanometres**; 4 mm is `4000000`. A pin has `idx`, `fn_cp`, `pos: [x,y]` "
"and outward `dir`. The function's pin with the corresponding `idx` holds its "
"printed `designation`. Keep the reference created by the template; the "
"publisher replaces temporary references for owned functions."
msgstr ""

